window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("reliability", {"topics":[{"key":"reliability/high-availability","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Nygard, 2018 — Release It! (2nd ed.)"],"why":"- **A single server is a single point of failure.** One disk, one kernel panic, one bad deploy, one\n  availability-zone incident, and the product is down.\n- **\"Buy a more reliable machine\" fails on arithmetic.** A chain of components that must all be up\n  multiplies: three dependencies at 99.9% each give 0.999³ ≈ 99.7%, roughly 26 hours of downtime a year\n  instead of 9. Modern systems have dozens of components, so this only gets worse.\n- **Change, not hardware, is the main enemy.** Most outages in mature organisations follow deploys,\n  configuration pushes, schema migrations and certificate rotations. The Google SRE book puts the share\n  caused by changes to a live system at roughly 70%. A perfect machine does nothing for that.\n- **HA in practice means no single component is required.** You must be able to take any one instance\n  out of service, including on purpose, without users noticing.\n- **AI systems have it worse.** The request path includes an external model API with its own incidents,\n  rate-limit spikes and regional capacity limits, plus GPU hosts with more parts to fail. If the\n  provider is 99.9% and you promise 99.95%, no number of replicas of your own code can deliver it.","how":"### Measuring it\n- **Time-based:** `uptime / total time`.\n- **Request-based:** `successful requests / total requests`. This is what users feel and what SLOs\n  usually use, because a 10-minute outage at 3 a.m. and one at peak are not the same.\n- **The nines convert to a yearly budget** of 8,760 hours:\n\n```\n99%     → 87.6 h/year     99.9%  → 8.76 h/year\n99.99%  → 52.6 min/year   99.999% → 5.26 min/year\n```\n\n### Three formulas that do most of the reasoning\n| Rule | Formula | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Series** (all must work) | `A = A₁ × A₂ × …` | every extra dependency lowers the total |\n| **Parallel** (any one suffices) | `A = 1 − (1 − A₁)(1 − A₂)…` | two 99% replicas give 99.99%, assuming perfect failover and independent failures |\n| **Single component** | `A = MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR)` | mean time between failures over the whole cycle, including mean time to repair |\n\n- **The parallel formula is wildly optimistic** whenever failures are correlated, and they usually are:\n  both replicas run the same code, got the same deploy, sit in the same zone and call the same provider.\n- **MTTR is the lever you control.** A component that fails once a month (MTBF ≈ 730 h) and takes 45\n  minutes to recover is about 99.9% available. Cutting recovery to 4.5 minutes makes it about 99.99%\n  without failing any less often.\n- **So HA emphasises fast detection and automatic failover,** because making failures rare is hard and\n  slow, while making them short is engineering you control.\n\n### Removing single points of failure\n- **Stateless service tier.** Run at least two instances behind a load balancer, spread across\n  availability zones. Statelessness matters *because* it makes any instance interchangeable; session\n  state, conversation history and job progress move to replicated stores.\n- **Replicated state.** A database primary with a synchronous or asynchronous replica and automatic\n  promotion; a vector store with replicas; a cache that the service can live without.\n- **Redundant plumbing.** Managed load balancers and DNS are themselves redundant; a self-managed\n  single nginx box in front of a redundant fleet quietly undoes the fleet.\n- **Capacity headroom.** N+1 (or N+2) sizing, *because* a surviving replica that is already at 80%\n  cannot absorb its dead sibling's traffic; redundancy without headroom just moves the outage.\n\n### Detecting failure fast\n- **Detection time is downtime.** A check that probes every 10 s and needs 3 failures declares death\n  after ~30 s, and that interval is pure downtime for traffic routed to the dead node.\n- **Test the ability to serve, not to answer HTTP.** A model server whose GPU is wedged will happily\n  return 200 from `/health` while every real request times out.\n- **Split liveness from readiness.** A cheap *liveness* probe means \"restart me\"; a *readiness* probe\n  reflects dependencies and warm-up and means \"send me traffic\". Readiness keeps a freshly started model\n  server, still loading weights, out of rotation.\n- **Readiness covers only what removing this instance fixes.** If every replica's readiness checks the\n  same shared model provider, a provider blip marks the whole fleet unready at once. The load balancer\n  then has nowhere to send traffic, turning a degraded dependency into a total outage.\n- **Shared dependencies belong in circuit breakers and fallbacks,** not in the probe that removes\n  instances.\n\n### Failover and its hidden costs\n| Mode | How it fails over | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Active-passive** | a standby takes over | detection time plus warm-up; a cold standby loading a 70B-parameter model from object storage can take minutes to be useful |\n| **Active-active** | every node serves all the time; a failure only reduces capacity | must share state; doubles as proof the second node actually works |\n\n- **In-flight work is lost in both.** A streaming generation dies with its node, so clients need retry\n  logic and, for long agent runs, durable checkpoints.\n\n### Stateful failover is where HA gets hard\nStateless replicas are interchangeable; a database primary is not. Promoting a replica raises two\nquestions the stateless tier never asks:\n1. **What is lost?** An asynchronous replica trails the primary, so promotion discards whatever had not\n   yet replicated. A synchronous replica loses nothing but adds a round trip to every commit.\n2. **Is the old primary really dead?** If it was only slow or partitioned and keeps accepting writes\n   after the promotion, you have two primaries (split brain).\n\n- **Managed databases solve this with a single arbiter and fencing,** which is a large part of what you\n  pay them for. A self-built failover script that promotes on a missed heartbeat usually does not.\n\n### Change as the biggest availability risk\nBecause changes cause most outages, HA includes the deployment process:\n- **Rolling updates that drain in-flight requests;** the drain timeout must exceed your longest generation.\n- **Canaries** that take a fraction of traffic first.\n- **Flags** that can disable a feature without a deploy.\n- **Rollbacks** that take seconds.\n- **A system redundant at runtime but deploying to all replicas at once is not HA.**\n\n### Where AI systems differ\n- **Model providers are a shared dependency.** Multi-provider fallback raises availability only if your\n  prompts and evals are portable and you accept that the fallback model gives different answers.\n  \"Available but different\" is a product decision, not just an infrastructure one.\n- **GPU capacity is expensive,** so N+1 redundancy may be replaced by graceful degradation to a smaller model.\n- **For self-hosted serving,** the readiness probe should include a real tiny inference.","example":"### Composing availability from the parts\nThe script composes the availability of a chat product from its parts and shows how adding a second\nmodel provider moves the bottleneck rather than removing it. All inputs are illustrative targets, not\nmeasured figures, and the parallel rule assumes independent failures and perfect failover.\n\n```python\nfrom functools import reduce\n\ndef series(*parts):        # every part must be up\n    return reduce(lambda acc, a: acc * a, parts, 1.0)\n\ndef parallel(*parts):      # up if any part is up (independent failures, perfect failover)\n    return 1 - reduce(lambda acc, a: acc * (1 - a), parts, 1.0)\n\ndef downtime_hours_per_year(a):\n    return (1 - a) * 8760\n\nlb, api_replica, vector_db, provider = 0.9999, 0.999, 0.9995, 0.999\n\nsingle = series(lb, parallel(api_replica, api_replica), vector_db, provider)\ndual   = series(lb, parallel(api_replica, api_replica), vector_db, parallel(provider, provider))\n\nfor label, a in ((\"one provider\", single), (\"two independent providers\", dual)):\n    print(f\"{label}: {a:.4%}  ->  {downtime_hours_per_year(a):.1f} h/year\")\n# one provider:               99.8400%  ->  14.0 h/year   (provider dominates)\n# two independent providers:  99.9398%  ->   5.3 h/year   (vector DB now dominates)\n```\n\n- **One provider pins the product near 99.84%,** even though the API tier is 99.9999%.\n- **A second provider lifts it to ~99.94%,** and now the single vector database is the weakest link,\n  which is exactly where the next engineering dollar should go.\n- **Shared failure modes** (same upstream GPU cloud, your own shared gateway) put the real number\n  somewhere between the two lines.\n\n### Worked scenario: buying a nine with faster recovery\nUsing `A = MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR)` for a component that fails about once a month (MTBF ≈ 730 h):\n\n| MTTR | How it is achieved | Availability | Downtime per year |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 45 min | human paged, manual failover | ≈ 99.9% | ≈ 9 h |\n| 4.5 min | automatic failover after health checks | ≈ 99.99% | ≈ 54 min |\n| ~30 s detection alone | 10 s probe interval × 3 failures | lower bound on any automated MTTR | n/a |\n\nThe failure rate never changed; only detection and failover did. That is why the first HA investment\nis usually automation of recovery, not a sturdier box.","patterns":["**Readiness that runs a tiny inference.** For self-hosted model servers, have the readiness endpoint generate a few tokens against the loaded model, and keep liveness cheap (process alive). In Kubernetes, set a `startupProbe` or a long initial delay to cover weight loading. Monitor probe latency and the count of ready pods per zone.","**N+1 capacity per zone.** Size each zone so the fleet survives losing one zone at peak, for example keeping utilisation below about two thirds with three zones. Monitor peak utilisation against that ceiling in a weekly capacity review.","**Drain longer than the longest generation.** On shutdown, fail readiness first, stop accepting new streams, then wait; set Kubernetes `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` above your maximum generation time. Monitor client errors at each release.","**Circuit breaker plus provider fallback.** Wrap each model provider in a breaker that opens on error rate or latency, and route to a second provider or smaller model with portable prompts. Monitor breaker state changes and the share of traffic served by the fallback.","**Managed failover for stateful tiers.** Use a managed database with multi-zone standby and fencing rather than a heartbeat script. Monitor replication lag, because that is the data an async promotion would lose."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"Client request\"] --> LB[\"Load balancer\"]\n    LB -->|\"readiness passes\"| R1[\"API replica zone A\"]\n    LB -->|\"readiness passes\"| R2[\"API replica zone B\"]\n    LB -.->|\"readiness fails, removed\"| R3[\"API replica zone C\"]\n    R1 --> DB[(\"Primary DB with replica\")]\n    R2 --> DB\n    R1 --> CB[\"Circuit breaker\"]\n    R2 --> CB\n    CB -->|\"healthy\"| P1[\"Model provider A\"]\n    CB -.->|\"open, fall back\"| P2[\"Model provider B\"]","caption":"A request survives a dead replica because the load balancer only routes to instances whose readiness\npasses; the shared provider is guarded by a fallback, not by the probe."},"interview":[{"q":"What does high availability actually mean, and why is a more reliable server not the answer?","a":"- **Definition:** the system keeps answering through the failure of any single component, measured as\n  the fraction of successful requests or of time it works.\n- **Why not a better server:** availabilities in series multiply, so dozens of components drag the total\n  down, and most outages come from changes such as deploys, not hardware.\n- **So the goal is \"no single component is required\",** reached through redundancy, detection and failover."},{"q":"Your model servers pass health checks but users see timeouts. How do you debug and fix it?","a":"- **Suspect a process-level check.** A wedged GPU still returns 200 from a shallow `/health`.\n- **Fix readiness to test serving:** run a tiny real inference, with a timeout, and fail readiness when it\n  does not complete.\n- **Keep liveness cheap,** so warm-up or slow inference does not trigger restart loops."},{"q":"Should readiness probes check the model provider? Why or why not?","a":"- **No for shared dependencies.** Every replica would fail together on a provider blip, emptying the load\n  balancer pool and turning degradation into a full outage.\n- **Probe only what removing this instance fixes,** such as its own loaded model or local resources.\n- **Handle the provider with circuit breakers and fallbacks** in the request path instead."},{"q":"When would you choose active-passive over active-active?","a":"- **Active-passive fits** when state sharing between nodes is hard, such as a single-writer database, and\n  a failover gap of detection plus warm-up is acceptable.\n- **Active-active fits** stateless tiers: failures only reduce capacity and the second node is proven daily.\n- **Watch the cold standby:** loading a 70B-parameter model from object storage can take minutes, so a\n  passive GPU standby may need to be kept warm."},{"q":"A component fails monthly and takes 45 minutes to recover. How do you get it to four nines?","a":"- **Use `A = MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR)`:** with MTBF ≈ 730 h, 45 minutes gives about 99.9%.\n- **Cut MTTR to about 4.5 minutes** through automatic detection and failover, which gives about 99.99%\n  with no change in failure rate.\n- **Budget detection inside that:** a 10 s probe needing 3 failures already spends ~30 s."}],"tradeoffs":["**Extra nines vs cost.** Every extra nine roughly multiplies cost and complexity, so a 99.9% target with fast rollback is often right for internal tools; it flips to 99.99%+ when revenue or safety rides on each request.","**Active-active vs active-passive.** Active-active uses the standby capacity all the time and proves it works, but forces you to solve state sharing between nodes; active-passive is simpler and wastes the standby until the day it fails.","**Multi-provider fallback vs output stability.** It raises model availability only when prompts, tool schemas and evals are portable; if outputs must be byte-stable, the fallback is a degraded mode, not a transparent one.","**Aggressive health checks vs misdiagnosis.** They fail over faster but mark slow nodes as dead under load, turning a latency problem into a capacity problem as traffic piles onto fewer survivors."],"pitfalls":["**Counting redundancy that shares a failure mode.** Two replicas in one zone, two providers behind one gateway, two databases on one deploy pipeline. The symptom is \"redundant\" components failing together.","**Health checks that test the process, not the service.** The node stays in rotation while every real request fails, and the load balancer keeps sending it traffic.","**The opposite error: readiness probes that include a shared dependency.** One model-provider or database hiccup marks every replica unready together, and the load balancer returns errors from an empty pool even though your own code was fine.","**No headroom.** N replicas each at 80% load, so losing one overloads the rest and the \"failover\" is a cascading failure. Visible as a second outage minutes after the first node died.","**Promising an SLA above the provider's.** The math makes it impossible; the symptom is a chronic error-budget deficit nobody can engineer away.","**Forgetting that deploys are downtime.** Rolling restarts that kill in-flight generations show up as a spike of client errors at every release, not as an \"outage\"."],"when":"- **Decide the target before the design.** Ask what one hour of downtime costs, convert that into a\n  number of nines, and pay for only that.\n- **Match the design to the surface.** An internal retrieval tool may be fine at 99.5% with a 15-minute\n  rollback; a customer-facing assistant inside a checkout flow needs multi-zone active-active and\n  multi-provider fallback.\n- **Measure the way users experience it:** successful requests, by endpoint, by tenant, not host uptime.\n- **Day to day:** readiness probes, drain timeouts, N+1 capacity reviews, and the design-review question\n  \"what happens if this box disappears right now?\".\n- **Interviews:** compose availabilities, explain why the provider caps yours, and distinguish HA\n  (surviving a component failure with no visible downtime) from disaster recovery (restoring service\n  after a site or data loss, with a measured gap).","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements readiness probes that run a real inference, retry-with-fallback on provider errors, and drain timeouts longer than the maximum generation time.","architect":"Sets the availability target per surface, owns the dependency availability math, and decides between active-active, active-passive and degradation-instead-of-redundancy for GPU tiers.","fde":"Translates a customer's \"five nines\" request into the provider's actual SLA and a multi-provider or self-hosted design, and documents the residual gap honestly."},"check":{"q":"A team promises 99.95% availability for an assistant whose path is: global load balancer\n(99.99%), three stateless API replicas across zones (99.9% each), one managed vector database\n(99.95%), and a single model provider (99.9%). Can they hit the target, and what are the first two\nchanges you would make?","a":"No. The three replicas in parallel are effectively 100%, but the series product\n0.9999 × 0.9995 × 0.999 ≈ 99.84%, roughly 14 hours a year, is already below target before counting\ndeploys and their own bugs. The provider is the dominant term, so the first change is an independent\nsecond provider with portable prompts and evals, lifting the model term toward 99.9999% and the product\nto about 99.94%. The vector database then dominates; the second change is either a replica with\nautomatic failover or a degraded mode (answer without retrieval, flagged) so a vector outage is not a\nproduct outage. A strong answer adds that correlated failures make these numbers optimistic and that\nthe target should be renegotiated unless both changes are funded."},"quiz":[{"q":"Two API replicas each at 99.9% sit behind a load balancer. Why is the combined availability usually worse than the 99.9999% the parallel formula predicts?","options":["The load balancer halves the effective capacity of each replica","Their failures are correlated: same deploy, same zone, same dependencies, so they fail together","Parallel composition only applies to databases, not stateless services","Health checks cannot distinguish between two identical replicas"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The parallel formula assumes independent failures. Replicas built from the same code and deployed together share most failure modes, so the real figure sits far closer to a single replica's."},{"q":"A model server loads weights for three minutes after start-up. Which probe design keeps it from receiving traffic too early without causing restart loops?","options":["A single liveness probe with a 10-second timeout","No probes; the load balancer retries failed requests automatically","A cheap liveness probe plus a readiness probe that only passes once a test inference succeeds","A readiness probe that checks the process is listening on its port"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Readiness gates traffic, liveness gates restarts. A liveness probe that fails during warm-up would kill the pod repeatedly; a port check passes before the model can actually answer."},{"q":"What distinguishes high availability from disaster recovery?","options":["HA masks single-component failures with no visible downtime; DR restores service after a site or data loss, accepting a measured gap","HA applies to stateless services and DR applies only to databases","HA is about hardware failures while DR is about software bugs","They are the same practice measured over different time windows"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HA keeps the system up through routine faults using redundancy and failover. DR plans for the cases redundancy cannot cover, such as a region loss or corrupted data, and is measured by RPO and RTO."}]},{"key":"reliability/fault-tolerance","sources":["Avižienis, Laprie, Randell, Landwehr, 2004 — Basic Concepts and Taxonomy of Dependable and Secure Computing","Gray, 1985 — Why Do Computers Stop and What Can Be Done About It?","Nygard, 2018 — Release It! (2nd ed.)","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **Components fail at rates you cannot engineer away.** A fleet of 1,000 disks with a 1% annual failure\n  rate loses about ten a year; 10 million remote calls a day at a 0.01% failure rate is a thousand\n  failures daily.\n- **Remote calls fail ambiguously.** A request can be lost on the way out, lost on the way back, or merely\n  slow, and the caller cannot tell which. Local code never has this failure mode.\n- **Better hardware never solved it.** Jim Gray's analysis of Tandem outages found software and\n  operations, not hardware, were the dominant causes.\n- **The naive `try: call() except: call()` amplifies faults.** It retries what retrying cannot fix (bad\n  input, auth errors), duplicates side effects when the first call succeeded but the reply was lost, and\n  multiplies load on a struggling dependency, turning a partial failure into a total one.\n- **Agents make it urgent.** A 20-step agent at 99% per step finishes cleanly only 0.99²⁰ ≈ 82% of the\n  time. Most of the 18% are transient (a 429, a tool timeout, malformed JSON) and are recoverable without\n  double-charging a card or sending an email twice.","how":"### Vocabulary that keeps designs honest\nAvižienis et al. separate three links in a chain:\n\n| Term | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fault** | the underlying defect | a bug, a dead NIC, a provider incident |\n| **Error** | the incorrect internal state it produces | a timed-out call, a null field |\n| **Failure** | the service no longer doing what it promised, as users see it | a wrong or missing answer |\n\n- **Fault tolerance stops the chain between error and failure.** Naming which link you are breaking is\n  the difference between a design and a hope.\n\n### Choose the fault model first\nYou tolerate what you design for.\n- **Crash-stop:** a node dies and stays dead.\n- **Crash-recovery:** it comes back with or without its state, so you must handle duplicate work.\n- **Omission and timing:** a message or reply is lost or late. Every remote call needs a timeout,\n  because \"slow\" is indistinguishable from \"dead\" from the outside.\n- **Byzantine (arbitrary):** a component returns well-formed but wrong output, or different answers to\n  different callers. Classic systems rarely engineer against it because masking is expensive: Byzantine\n  agreement needs 3f + 1 replicas to tolerate f faulty ones, against 2f + 1 for crash faults.\n- **LLM value faults look Byzantine.** A confident, schema-valid, wrong tool argument is not malicious,\n  so strictly it is a value fault, but nothing about the reply looks broken. That is why output\n  validation against ground truth and approval gates belong next to retries.\n\n### Masking versus recovery\n- **Masking hides the error entirely.** A replica answers instead, or several components vote and the\n  majority wins (N-modular redundancy). Sampling several LLM answers and taking the consensus is the same\n  idea, and it only helps when the samples' errors are independent.\n- **Backward recovery** returns to a known-good earlier state and redoes the work: checkpoint and\n  restart, transaction rollback, replaying a workflow from its history.\n- **Forward recovery** moves to a new correct state without undoing: a compensating action (refund the\n  charge, retract the email) or a degraded answer.\n- **Real-world side effects usually cannot be rolled back,** so agent tools that act on real systems\n  need forward recovery designed in.\n\n### The toolbox, in the order you apply it\n1. **Timeouts**, *because* a hung call holds a thread, a connection and often a GPU slot; nothing else\n   in the list can act until the call returns. Derive them from the latency budget end to end, not\n   per hop in isolation.\n2. **Bounded retries with backoff and jitter**, *because* transient faults (429, 503, connection reset)\n   usually clear within seconds, and jitter prevents every client from retrying in lockstep. Retry only\n   transient classes, cap attempts, and give retries a budget (SRE book rule of thumb: around 10% of\n   total requests) so an outage cannot double your load.\n3. **Idempotency**, *because* retries are only safe if a repeated operation has the same effect as one.\n   A pure completion call is naturally idempotent apart from billing; a tool that sends money is not,\n   and needs an idempotency key the receiver deduplicates on.\n4. **Circuit breakers**, *because* once a dependency is clearly down, continuing to call it wastes your\n   latency budget and its recovery capacity; fail fast and probe occasionally.\n5. **Bulkheads**, *because* a failing dependency should exhaust its own pool of connections or\n   workers, not the shared pool that serves everything else.\n6. **Fallbacks and graceful degradation**, *because* \"what do we do instead\" is a product decision\n   made in advance: a cached answer, a smaller model, retrieval-only mode, a static \"try again\" message.\n   Each operation gets one explicit choice.\n7. **Replication and consensus for state**, *because* stateless retries cannot recover data; replicas,\n   quorum writes and leader election keep the truth alive when a node holding it dies.\n\n### Fail-fast, fail-safe, fail-operational\n| Style | Behaviour on fault | Protects |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fail-fast** | reports an error immediately rather than hanging | callers' latency budgets |\n| **Fail-safe** | moves to a safe state, e.g. an agent refusing to act when its policy check is unavailable | users and the outside world |\n| **Fail-operational** | continues full service through redundancy | availability |\n\n- **Default for AI features:** fail-fast internally and fail-safe at action boundaries.\n\n### Detection and its own failure mode\n- **Every detector has false positives.** Heartbeats, health checks and error-rate thresholds detect\n  faults, but can be wrong.\n- **A wrong diagnosis causes split brain.** Declaring a slow primary dead and promoting a replica while\n  the old one is still writing leaves two writers.\n- **Fencing tokens or a single arbiter** prevent two nodes from both believing they lead.\n- **So thresholds trade failover speed** against the risk of acting on a wrong diagnosis.","example":"### A tolerant provider call\nA provider call wrapped in tools 1, 2 and 6 from the list: an overall deadline, per-attempt timeout,\nretries only on transient statuses, full-jitter backoff that honours `Retry-After`, and a fallback that\ndegrades instead of failing.\n\nIt assumes the call itself has no side effects, so repeating it is safe (tool 3 satisfied by\nconstruction); the breaker and bulkhead (4 and 5) sit around this function, not inside it.\n\n```python\nimport random, time\n\nclass Transient(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised by the client on 408/429/5xx; carries Retry-After when the server sent one.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, status, retry_after=None):\n        self.status, self.retry_after = status, retry_after\n\ndef tolerant_complete(primary, fallback, req, *, budget_s=45, attempt_timeout_s=20,\n                      max_attempts=3, base_s=0.5, cap_s=8.0):\n    deadline = time.monotonic() + budget_s             # one budget for the whole operation\n    for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n        remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()\n        if remaining <= 0:\n            break\n        try:\n            return primary(req, timeout_s=min(attempt_timeout_s, remaining))\n        except Transient as e:                         # non-transient errors propagate: retrying cannot fix them\n            if attempt == max_attempts - 1:\n                break                                  # no attempts left: do not sleep for nothing\n            sleep = e.retry_after if e.retry_after is not None \\\n                else random.uniform(0, min(cap_s, base_s * 2 ** attempt))   # full jitter\n            if time.monotonic() + sleep >= deadline:\n                break\n            time.sleep(sleep)\n    # primary exhausted or budget spent: degrade visibly rather than fail\n    remaining = max(1.0, deadline - time.monotonic())  # floor: may overrun the budget by up to 1 s\n    return fallback(req, timeout_s=remaining)          # smaller model, cached answer, or a typed \"unavailable\"\n```\n\n- **Why three attempts, not ten?** With independent transient failures at 5% per call, one attempt\n  succeeds 95% of the time, two 99.75%, three 1 − 0.05³ ≈ 99.99%; a fourth adds almost nothing.\n- **That arithmetic only holds while failures are independent.** In a real provider outage they are\n  perfectly correlated, every attempt fails, and the loop only triples the load on the provider and spends\n  the user's budget. The deadline, the breaker around this function and the fallback matter more.\n- **Tag the fallback result** (a header or a field) so the UI and metrics know the answer came from the\n  degraded path, and export the retry count to see a provider wobble before it becomes an outage.\n- **For side-effecting tool calls,** add an idempotency key to `req` before the first attempt, never\n  inside the loop.\n\n### Worked scenario: nested retries during an outage\nThree layers each retry \"a few times\" with no shared budget: the SDK (3 attempts), the service (3) and\nthe browser client (3). The provider starts failing every call.\n\n| Layer | Attempts per call from above | Calls reaching the provider per user action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Client | 3 | 3 |\n| Service | 3 per client attempt | 9 |\n| SDK | 3 per service attempt | 27 |\n\n- **One user action becomes 27 provider calls,** so request volume spikes several-fold exactly when the\n  provider is weakest.\n- **Fix:** retry at one layer only (usually the one closest to the dependency), disable SDK retries or\n  pass a shared deadline down, and cap retries with a budget of around 10% of requests.","patterns":["**One shared client wrapper.** Every team imports the same provider and tool client that applies the deadline, transient-only retries with full jitter and `Retry-After` handling. Monitor attempts per request and retry ratio per dependency.","**Retry budget, not just a retry count.** Track retries as a fraction of requests over a short window and stop retrying when it passes about 10%, so an outage cannot double load. Monitor the budget exhaustion rate as an early outage signal.","**Idempotency key minted before the first attempt.** Generate one key per intended action (for example a UUID stored with the agent step) and send it on every attempt; the receiver stores it with a unique constraint and returns the first result on repeats. Monitor duplicate-rejected counts.","**Read-after-timeout for side-effecting tools.** Classify a timeout on a write as \"unknown outcome\" and query the target system for the key before retrying. Monitor the share of timeouts that turned out to have succeeded.","**Labelled degradation ladder.** Define per operation: primary model, smaller model, cached answer, typed \"unavailable\", and tag responses with which rung served them. Monitor fallback share and run chaos tests that force each rung."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request\"] --> T[\"Deadline and per-attempt timeout\"]\n    T --> C[\"Call dependency\"]\n    C -->|\"success\"| OK[\"Correct answer\"]\n    C -->|\"error\"| K{\"Transient class?\"}\n    K -->|\"no: bad input, auth\"| E[\"Fail fast with typed error\"]\n    K -->|\"yes: 408, 429, 5xx\"| B{\"Attempts and budget left?\"}\n    B -->|\"yes\"| J[\"Jittered backoff or Retry-After\"]\n    J --> C\n    B -->|\"no\"| F[\"Fallback: smaller model or cache\"]\n    F -.->|\"tagged as degraded\"| D[\"Degraded answer\"]","caption":"A fault is contained at each layer before it can become a user-visible failure; the dotted path is the\ndegraded answer."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a fault, an error and a failure, and why does it matter?","a":"- **Fault** is the defect (a bug, a dead NIC), **error** the bad internal state it causes (a timed-out\n  call), **failure** the user-visible broken promise.\n- **It matters because** each mechanism breaks a specific link: a retry turns an error back into success,\n  a fallback stops an error becoming a failure. Naming the link keeps the design honest."},{"q":"Why is a plain retry loop dangerous, and what makes retries safe?","a":"- **Danger:** it retries unfixable errors, duplicates side effects when only the reply was lost, and\n  multiplies load during an outage.\n- **Safe retries need:** transient-only classification, capped attempts with jittered backoff, a shared\n  deadline, a retry budget, and idempotency for anything with side effects."},{"q":"During a provider incident your request volume to it tripled. How do you find and fix the cause?","a":"- **Look for nested retries:** compare user actions to provider calls per layer; 3 × 3 × 3 gives 27.\n- **Fix:** retry in one layer only, propagate a deadline, add a retry budget, and put a circuit breaker\n  around the dependency so it fails fast once errors persist."},{"q":"When would you choose fail-safe over fail-open for a guard service?","a":"- **Fail-safe** (block the action) when the guard protects money, data or safety: an agent should refuse\n  to act when its policy check is down.\n- **Fail-open** only when the guard's absence is low-risk, because fail-safe turns a guard outage into a\n  feature outage."},{"q":"How many retries would you configure for a call with a 5% transient failure rate?","a":"- **About three:** success goes 95%, 99.75%, then about 99.99%; a fourth adds almost nothing.\n- **Caveat:** that assumes independent failures. In a real outage they are correlated, so the deadline,\n  breaker and fallback matter more than the count."}],"tradeoffs":["**Retries vs load.** Retries raise success rates for transient faults but add load exactly when the dependency is weakest; the balance flips to \"stop retrying\" once the error rate suggests a real outage, which is the breaker's job.","**Replicas vs restore.** Redundant replicas tolerate crash faults but cost double and add consistency problems; for rarely used or cheap-to-recompute state, a fast restore beats a live replica.","**Fail-safe vs feature availability.** Fail-safe defaults protect users when a policy or guard service is down, but turn a guard outage into a feature outage; fail-open is acceptable only for guards whose absence is low-risk.","**Tight vs loose timeouts.** Tight timeouts free resources quickly but cut off legitimately slow responses such as long generations; the value comes from the latency distribution, and streaming changes what \"timeout\" should mean.","**Fallback models vs consistency.** They keep the product available but change answer quality and behaviour; if customers depend on consistency, label the fallback and measure it separately in evals.","**Voting vs cost.** Masking by voting (several replicas or several sampled LLM answers) hides value faults but multiplies cost and latency by N; it pays only when errors are independent and a wrong answer is expensive, and buys nothing when every sample shares the same misleading context."],"pitfalls":["**Retrying non-idempotent tool calls after a timeout.** The first call succeeded and the reply was lost; the symptom is duplicate emails, tickets or charges that correlate with provider latency spikes.","**Nested retries across layers.** SDK retries × service retries × client retries multiply into tens of attempts per user action; visible as request volume spiking several-fold during an incident.","**Timeouts that add up.** Five sequential hops each with a 30 s timeout cannot meet a 30 s end-to-end promise, so users see failures even though every hop \"met its timeout\".","**Treating any exception as transient.** Bad prompts or revoked API keys are retried for minutes; the symptom is latency without errors in logs until the budget finally expires.","**Fallbacks that were never exercised.** The secondary provider's key expired or its tool schema drifted, discovered only during the primary's outage. Chaos tests exist for this."],"when":"- **Baseline for every remote call:** timeout, bounded jittered retry on transient classes, and\n  idempotency for anything with side effects. That is not a design choice.\n- **Add breakers and bulkheads** when a dependency's failure can starve shared resources.\n- **Define a fallback** for every user-facing operation where \"nothing\" is worse than \"something degraded\".\n- **Do not add tolerance you cannot observe.** Export attempt counts, breaker state and fallback usage,\n  or you will not know the system is quietly running on its backup path.\n- **Day to day:** the client wrapper everyone imports and the FMEA row saying what happens when each\n  dependency fails.\n- **Interviews:** explain why retries need budgets and idempotency, and walk a fault from detection to\n  degraded response.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the shared provider and tool client: deadlines, jittered retries on transient statuses, idempotency keys for side-effecting tools, and metrics for every degraded path.","architect":"Chooses the fault model and the degradation ladder per capability, and sets the rule that no retry layer exists without a budget and no action tool exists without idempotency.","fde":"Maps which of the customer's systems can be retried safely, which need idempotency keys or reconciliation, and what degraded mode the customer's operations team will accept."},"check":{"q":"An agent's \"create purchase order\" tool call timed out at 30 seconds. The orchestrator retried,\nand the customer's ERP now shows two orders. Walk through what went wrong and how the design should\nchange at each layer.","a":"The timeout is an omission fault: the caller cannot know whether the first call completed, and\nin this case it did. Retrying a non-idempotent operation after an ambiguous failure is the root\nmistake. Fixes, from the inside out: the tool must accept an idempotency key generated once per\nintended action, before the first attempt, and the ERP adapter must deduplicate on it or query for an\nexisting order with that key before creating. The orchestrator should classify timeouts on\nside-effecting tools as \"unknown outcome\" and reconcile (read-after-timeout) rather than blindly\nretry. The 30 s timeout should be derived from the ERP's actual latency distribution so it does not\ntrip on normal slow paths. Finally, an approval gate or a daily reconciliation report catches the\ncases that still slip through, and the duplicate rate becomes a tracked metric."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must retries be combined with idempotency rather than used alone?","options":["Idempotency makes the retried call faster because the server can cache it","After a timeout the caller cannot know whether the first attempt took effect, so a repeat must be harmless","Providers reject retries that do not carry an idempotency header","Idempotency allows retries to skip the backoff delay"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A lost reply looks identical to a lost request. Without idempotency, retrying after an ambiguous failure duplicates side effects; with it, the second attempt either no-ops or returns the first result."},{"q":"A dependency starts returning 503s for 40% of calls. Which combination best protects the rest of the system?","options":["Increase the retry count to five and remove the timeout so slow calls can finish","Switch all calls to synchronous mode so failures are detected sooner","Keep a short timeout, retry within a budget, open a circuit breaker if errors persist, and serve a defined fallback","Disable the dependency's health check so the load balancer keeps traffic flowing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"More retries and no timeout pour load onto a struggling dependency and tie up your own resources. Budgeted retries plus a breaker contain the fault, and the fallback keeps users served."},{"q":"Which fault model best describes an LLM returning a syntactically valid tool call with the wrong account ID?","options":["Crash-stop, because the model stopped doing useful work","Omission, because the correct answer was omitted","Byzantine, because the component produced well-formed but incorrect output that passes shallow checks","Timing, because the model answered too quickly to have reasoned"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Byzantine (arbitrary) faults are wrong-but-plausible outputs from a component that has not crashed. They are caught by validation against ground truth and by approval gates, not by retries or timeouts."}]},{"key":"reliability/multi-region-architecture","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Abadi, 2012 — Consistency Tradeoffs in Modern Distributed Database System Design (PACELC)","Helland, 2007 — Life beyond Distributed Transactions","Official docs: AWS Well-Architected Framework, Reliability Pillar"],"why":"- **A region is one blast radius.** Even spread across availability zones, it shares a control plane and\n  regional services. Regional incidents are rare but take down every zone at once, and in-region\n  redundancy cannot help, because it is correlated failure by design. Procurement teams ask \"what is\n  your multi-region story\" right after one.\n- **Physics.** Light in fibre travels roughly 200,000 km/s, about 5 ms per 1,000 km one way. A user\n  6,000 km from your only region pays at least ~60 ms of round trip before any work, more after routing,\n  a visible slice of a chat product's time-to-first-token budget of a few hundred milliseconds.\n- **Law.** Data-residency obligations require some customers' data to be stored and processed in a\n  jurisdiction. For AI, \"processed\" includes the prompt sent to the model, so the inference endpoint\n  must be in-region too.\n- **Provider quotas.** Model providers publish quotas and model availability per region. A second region\n  can double your effective rate limit and give you somewhere to run when one region's capacity for a\n  model is exhausted.\n- **\"Same Terraform in two regions behind DNS\" fails** the moment two regions accept writes to the same\n  record, or a failover promotes a replica that is seconds behind. Compute is easy to duplicate; truth\n  is not.","how":"### Three topologies, in rising cost\n1. **Active-passive.** One region serves; the other holds replicated data and enough infrastructure to\n   take over (from \"pilot light\" to \"warm standby\"). This is disaster recovery with a shorter clock,\n   covered in that lesson; the standby's real risk is that it is never exercised.\n2. **Active-active, partitioned.** Every region serves traffic, but each tenant, user or shard has a\n   single *home* region that owns its writes; the other regions hold async replicas and can take over\n   ownership on failover. This is the workable default for stateful products, *because* it keeps\n   every write single-leader and avoids write conflicts entirely while still using all regions.\n3. **Active-active, shared.** Any region accepts any write to any record. This needs either a\n   globally consistent database that pays cross-region latency on commits or multi-leader replication\n   with conflict resolution. Choose it only for data that is genuinely written from everywhere.\n\n### State is the whole problem\n| Replication | When the client gets its ack | Failover loss | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Synchronous** | after the write is durable in both regions | nothing (RPO ≈ 0) | every write waits a cross-region round trip: tens of ms within a continent, ~100 ms or more across an ocean (illustrative orders of magnitude) |\n| **Asynchronous** | immediately | the replica's lag, usually sub-second but spiking under load | failover during a spike loses that window |\n\n- **PACELC names the trade honestly:** under a partition you choose availability or consistency, and\n  even without one you choose latency or consistency.\n\n### Mechanics per topology\n- **Partitioned active-active:**\n  1. Route each request to the tenant's home region.\n  2. Replicate asynchronously to the others.\n  3. On failover, promote the replica and re-home the tenant.\n  4. Accept the lag as lost writes to reconcile later.\n- **Shared active-active:** resolve conflicts with per-key ownership, CRDTs for data that merges\n  naturally (counters, sets), or last-writer-wins where losing an update is tolerable.\n- **Helland's advice still holds:** design entities so each transaction touches one entity in one place.\n\n### Routing and failover\n- **Geo or latency DNS is slow to fail over.** DNS answers are cached and clients routinely ignore short\n  TTLs, so DNS failover takes minutes.\n- **Anycast global load balancers move traffic in seconds** and are preferred where available.\n- **The dangerous moment is the decision.** Automatic database failover on a flaky health signal causes\n  split brain: two primaries both accepting writes.\n- **Mature teams automate stateless failover** and keep data-tier failover behind a human-confirmed\n  runbook step or a strict fencing mechanism.\n\n### Static stability\n- **The failover path must work using only what already runs in the surviving region.** A runbook that\n  needs the failed region's CI/CD system, its secrets manager, a DNS or IAM change routed through a\n  control plane hosted there, or a registry that only exists there, fails exactly when needed.\n- **Pre-provision capacity rather than \"scale up on failover\",** *because* during a regional event every\n  other customer of that cloud calls the same scaling APIs in the same surviving regions at once.\n\n### Capacity is part of the design\n- **Failover moves demand, not capacity.** Two regions at 70% utilisation leave a survivor needing 140%.\n- **Two options:** run each region at or below 50% (paying for idle capacity), or plan explicit load\n  shedding on failover: which tenants and which workloads keep running.\n- **Provider quotas follow the same arithmetic** and do not move with your traffic.\n\n### What is different for AI systems\n- **Model availability and rollout timing vary by region;** the same model name may not be live in both\n  regions on the same day, so pin versions and test in both.\n- **Embeddings must come from the same model and version in every region,** or a replicated vector index\n  becomes incoherent. Replicate the index artefact or the source documents plus a rebuild pipeline, and\n  know how long rebuild takes.\n- **Prefix caches and KV caches are per-server;** a failed-over region starts cold and pays higher\n  time-to-first-token until warm.\n- **Residency pins both storage and inference:** a residency-bound tenant whose home region fails must\n  *degrade in place*, not be served from a foreign region.\n- **In-flight agent runs die with the region** unless progress is checkpointed in a replicated store,\n  which is why durable execution and multi-region go together.","example":"### Worked failover: two-region, tenant-partitioned agent platform\nAll numbers illustrative.\n\n**Setup.**\n- Regions `us-east` and `eu-west`, each with a full stateless tier, a Postgres primary for its home\n  tenants and an async replica of the other region's tenants, typical lag under 2 s.\n- Agent checkpoints live in the same database; each region runs at about 40% of capacity.\n- Provider quota is 1,000 requests per minute per region; EU tenants are residency-bound.\n\n**Timeline when `us-east` becomes unreachable.**\n\n| Time | Event |\n|---|---|\n| T+~60 s | global load balancer marks `us-east` unhealthy after failed checks and routes all users to `eu-west` |\n| T+few min | on-call confirms the region, not the checks, is the problem and runs the promotion step |\n| T+6 min | `eu-west`'s replica of US tenants becomes primary |\n| after | agent runs mid-step in `us-east` resume in `eu-west` from their last checkpoint, re-executing only the interrupted step |\n\n- **Data loss:** writes `us-east` accepted in its last ~2 s never replicated, so the RPO for US tenants\n  is about 2 s. They stay stranded in `us-east` until it returns; clients holding their idempotency keys\n  can safely resubmit now, and the rest is reconciled from `us-east`'s log at failback.\n- **Compute holds:** `eu-west` carries both regions' traffic, 40% + 40% = 80% of one region.\n- **Quota does not:** EU's 1,000 rpm must cover roughly double the demand, so the platform sheds batch\n  summarisation jobs first and keeps interactive chat.\n- **EU tenants are unaffected** and stayed in-region throughout. Had the failure gone the other way,\n  residency-bound EU tenants would be put in a read-only degraded mode rather than served from `us-east`.\n- **Failback:** when `us-east` returns, replication is reversed, the reconciliation log is replayed, and\n  US tenants are re-homed in a low-traffic window after the replica catches up.\n\n### Failover capacity and data-loss calculator\nA pre-incident check you can run in a design review: does the survivor hold, what must be shed, and how\nmany writes a given lag puts at risk. Inputs are illustrative.\n\n```python\ndef failover_check(utils, demand_rpm, quota_rpm, failed, writes_per_s, lag_s):\n    survivor_util = sum(utils.values())                 # survivor absorbs all load\n    total_demand = sum(demand_rpm.values())\n    survivor = next(r for r in utils if r != failed)\n    shed_rpm = max(0, total_demand - quota_rpm[survivor])\n    at_risk = writes_per_s[failed] * lag_s              # unreplicated writes in the lag window\n    return survivor_util, shed_rpm, at_risk\n\nutils = {\"us-east\": 0.40, \"eu-west\": 0.40}\ndemand = {\"us-east\": 900, \"eu-west\": 900}\nquota = {\"us-east\": 1000, \"eu-west\": 1000}\nprint(failover_check(utils, demand, quota, \"us-east\", {\"us-east\": 50, \"eu-west\": 40}, lag_s=2))\n# (0.8, 800, 100): compute fits at 80%, shed 800 rpm of batch work, ~100 writes to reconcile\n\nutils = {\"us-east\": 0.70, \"eu-west\": 0.70}\nprint(failover_check(utils, demand, quota, \"us-east\", {\"us-east\": 50, \"eu-west\": 40}, lag_s=2)[0])\n# 1.4: the survivor would need 140% of its capacity, so shed load or run regions at 50% or less\n```\n\n- **Use the lag tail, not the mean,** for `lag_s`: the p99 lag during peak is your real RPO.","patterns":["**Home-region routing table.** Store each tenant's home region in a small replicated config and route at the edge on it; re-homing is a data change, not a deploy. Monitor requests served outside their home region, which should be zero outside a failover.","**Region-tagged idempotency keys.** Include the originating region in idempotency keys so writes replayed after failover deduplicate against the reconciliation log. Monitor duplicates rejected during failback.","**Human-confirmed data-tier promotion.** Automate stateless traffic shifting at the global load balancer, but put database promotion behind a runbook step with fencing of the old primary. Monitor time from alert to promotion in every drill.","**Statically stable failover kit.** Keep CI/CD runners, secrets, container images and model access already provisioned in every region; test by cutting the source region off, not by draining it politely. Monitor runbook steps that depend on another region.","**Replication lag as a distribution.** Alert on p99 replica lag per store (database, vector index, prompt registry, flags), because the tail is the data you lose. Track it alongside survivor utilisation and per-region provider quota headroom."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User request\"] --> G[\"Global load balancer\"]\n    G -->|\"US tenant\"| US[\"us-east stateless tier\"]\n    G -->|\"EU tenant\"| EU[\"eu-west stateless tier\"]\n    US --> PUS[(\"Primary: US tenants\")]\n    EU --> PEU[(\"Primary: EU tenants\")]\n    PUS -.->|\"async, lag under 2 s\"| RUS[(\"Replica of US in eu-west\")]\n    PEU -.->|\"async\"| REU[(\"Replica of EU in us-east\")]\n    G -->|\"us-east unhealthy\"| H{\"Region loss confirmed?\"}\n    H -->|\"yes, fenced\"| RUS\n    H -->|\"no\"| W[\"Keep primary, investigate checks\"]","caption":"Partitioned active-active: each tenant's writes go to its home region, replicas trail asynchronously,\nand failover promotes a replica only after a human or fencing confirms the region is gone."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is multi-AZ not enough for some products, and why is multi-region not the default?","a":"- **Multi-AZ shares a region's control plane and services,** so a regional incident takes every zone down\n  together; it cannot answer a contractual region-loss requirement.\n- **Multi-region is not the default** because it duplicates infrastructure, needs idle headroom and forces\n  hard state decisions. Use it for a named driver: region-loss survival, latency, residency or quota."},{"q":"Design a two-region setup for a stateful agent platform. Where do writes go?","a":"- **Partition by tenant:** each tenant has one home region that owns its writes, so there are no\n  cross-region write conflicts.\n- **Replicate asynchronously** to the other region, checkpoint agent runs in the replicated store, and\n  make clients idempotent so lost-window writes can be replayed.\n- **Pin residency-bound tenants** so they degrade in place rather than fail over abroad."},{"q":"Should database failover across regions be automatic?","a":"- **Usually not fully.** A false-positive health signal can promote the replica while the old primary\n  still writes, producing split brain.\n- **Automate stateless traffic shifting** in seconds, and gate data-tier promotion on human confirmation\n  or strict fencing; the slower path is worth it as data value rises."},{"q":"After a regional failover the surviving region fell over too. What happened and how do you prevent it?","a":"- **Likely capacity:** at 70% each, the survivor needed 140%; or provider quota could not cover doubled\n  demand.\n- **Prevent it** by running regions at or below 50%, or with a pre-agreed shedding plan (batch jobs first),\n  pre-provisioned rather than scale-on-failover capacity, and quota headroom per region."},{"q":"Your replica lag is usually 200 ms. What RPO do you promise?","a":"- **Not 200 ms.** Lag spikes under load, and failover tends to happen under stress, so the tail matters.\n- **Measure lag as a distribution** at peak and publish the p99 or worse as the RPO, then rehearse failover\n  to confirm it."}],"tradeoffs":["**Survival and latency vs cost.** Region loss survival and lower latency for distant users are paid for with duplicated infrastructure and idle headroom; it stops paying off when all users and regulators sit in one geography.","**Synchronous vs asynchronous replication.** Synchronous gives zero data loss but taxes every write with a cross-region round trip; flip to asynchronous when write latency is user-visible and a few seconds of loss is survivable.","**Partitioned vs shared active-active.** Partitioned avoids write conflicts but makes cross-tenant features (global search, shared workspaces) hard; shared enables them at the price of conflict resolution.","**Automatic vs manual failover.** Automatic shortens outages but risks split brain on false positives; manual data-tier promotion is slower and safer, and becomes the better choice as the data's value rises.","**More quota vs more validation.** A second region doubles provider quota and model-availability options, yet model and prompt behaviour must be validated in two places, doubling eval and rollout work."],"pitfalls":["**A standby region never failed over to.** Expired credentials, missing secrets, a model not enabled in that region; the symptom is discovering it during the real incident.","**Replicating the database but not the rest.** Without the vector index, prompt registry or feature flags, the failed-over region answers from stale or empty retrieval and behaves differently.","**Assuming DNS TTLs are honoured.** Traffic keeps arriving at the dead region for minutes after \"failover\" and users see errors you believe are impossible.","**Treating replication lag as zero.** Dashboards show success, but customers report lost messages or duplicated agent actions from the seconds before failover.","**Sizing each region for its own load.** After failover the survivor overloads and a regional outage becomes a global one, visible as a second wave of errors minutes after the first.","**A failover procedure with a hidden dependency on the failed region.** Its CI/CD runner, secrets store or registry; drills that only fail over while the source region is healthy never reveal it, so the symptom is a runbook step that hangs during the real event."],"when":"- **Use when you can name the driver:** a contractual requirement to survive region loss, a latency\n  budget one region cannot meet for a material user base, a residency rule, or provider quota you cannot\n  obtain in one region.\n- **Avoid otherwise:** multi-AZ plus a tested disaster-recovery plan is cheaper and usually adequate.\n- **Start with** partitioned active-active and asynchronous replication; reserve synchronous or globally\n  consistent stores for the few records that truly need them.\n- **Measure:** replication lag as a distribution (its tail is your real RPO), failover time and survivor\n  utilisation. Rehearse failover on a schedule, ideally by cutting the source region off rather than\n  politely draining it.\n- **Interviews:** depth shows in talking about where writes are owned and what is lost on failover, not\n  about load balancers.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Makes every service region-aware (home-region routing, region-tagged idempotency keys, checkpointed long runs) and runs the failover rehearsal that proves the second region works.","architect":"Decides the topology, which data is single-leader versus replicated, the consistency model per store, and the capacity and quota headroom each region must hold for failover.","fde":"Maps the customer's residency and latency constraints to tenant home regions, and sets expectations about what degrades, rather than moves, when a residency-bound region fails."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants an AI assistant in `us-east` and `eu-west` with \"no data loss on failover\" and\n\"no added latency\". Both are stated as hard requirements. How do you respond, and what design do you\npropose?","a":"The two requirements conflict for any write: zero loss on region failure requires the write to\nbe durable in both regions before acknowledgement, which costs a cross-region round trip, tens of\nmilliseconds at best, on every write. So I would make the trade explicit and split the data. For the\nsmall set of records where loss is unacceptable, such as billing events or approval decisions, use\nsynchronous replication or a globally consistent store and accept the latency on those operations\nonly. For high-volume, user-visible writes like conversation turns and agent checkpoints, replicate\nasynchronously with sub-second lag, publish the measured RPO, and make the client idempotent so the\nfew seconds of lost writes can be replayed. Partition tenants by home region to avoid write conflicts,\npin residency-bound tenants to degrade in place, and rehearse the failover so the numbers are measured\nrather than promised."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a partitioned active-active design, why does each tenant have a single home region for writes?","options":["To reduce storage costs by keeping only one copy of each tenant's data","So every record has one leader at a time, eliminating cross-region write conflicts while all regions still serve traffic","Because cloud providers only permit one writable database per account","To guarantee that failover never loses any writes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Single-leader-per-tenant keeps writes conflict-free without global coordination. Data is still replicated to other regions; failover can still lose the replication lag window."},{"q":"What is the main risk of fully automatic failover for a cross-region database primary?","options":["It makes the replication lag longer because the replica must be verified first","It violates data-residency rules in every jurisdiction","A false-positive health signal can promote the replica while the old primary is still accepting writes, producing split brain","It prevents the application tier from also failing over"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Health checks can fail for reasons other than region death. Two primaries both accepting writes diverge, and reconciling them is far worse than a few minutes of human-confirmed failover."},{"q":"Two regions each run at 70% utilisation. One fails. What is the most likely outcome without a prior plan?","options":["The survivor serves all traffic normally because compute autoscaling is instant","The survivor is driven to 140% of its capacity and overloads, turning a regional outage into a global one","Traffic automatically waits in DNS until the failed region returns","Provider quotas shift to the surviving region to absorb the load"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Failover moves demand, not capacity. Either run each region at or below 50% or decide in advance which workloads to shed, because autoscaling and provider quotas do not react in time."}]},{"key":"reliability/disaster-recovery","sources":["Official docs: AWS Well-Architected Framework, Reliability Pillar","NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 — Contingency Planning Guide for Federal Information Systems","Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications"],"why":"- **HA redundancy is in the same place and real time,** which makes it useless for a whole class of\n  disasters: a region loss, a deleted database, corrupted data, a compromised account.\n- **Replicas copy mistakes.** Synchronous replication faithfully copies a `DROP TABLE`, a bad migration\n  or a ransomware encryption to every replica within milliseconds. A regional outage takes every zone\n  down together, and a leaked cloud credential can delete replicas with the primary.\n- **You need a copy separated three ways:** in time (point-in-time history), in place (another region\n  or account) and in trust (immutable, separately permissioned).\n- **\"Nightly backups and good intentions\" fails three ways.** Nobody tested the restore, so it takes a\n  day and surfaces a missing dependency; the backup is up to 24 hours old, so a day of conversations,\n  approvals or orders vanishes; and it covers the database but not the vector index, prompt registry,\n  fine-tuned adapter weights or secrets, so the restored system behaves differently.\n- **AI state is expensive to recreate.** Rebuilding a vector index over millions of chunks means\n  re-embedding hundreds of millions of tokens, money and hours that count in RTO. Fine-tuned weights\n  never checkpointed to durable storage are simply gone.","how":"### The two numbers, precisely\nPicture a timeline with the disaster at T:\n- **Recovery point:** the gap between the most recent recoverable state and T. The RPO is the maximum\n  gap you agreed to tolerate.\n- **Recovery time:** the gap between T and service coming back. The RTO is the maximum you agreed to\n  tolerate.\n- **Objectives vs actuals:** objectives are promises set with the business; the measured results of a\n  real recovery or drill are the actuals (some teams write them RPA and RTA). The gap between\n  objective and actual is the finding.\n\n### RPO is bounded by how often state is copied away\n| Mechanism | Worst-case RPO |\n|---|---|\n| Nightly snapshot | up to 24 h |\n| Continuous log shipping (write-ahead log archived every minute) | about a minute |\n| Synchronous cross-region replication | approximately zero, at the cost described in multi-region |\n\n### RTO is a sum\n1. Detect.\n2. Decide.\n3. Provision or promote.\n4. Restore data and replay logs.\n5. Re-point traffic.\n6. Verify.\n\n- **Teams who only measure the restore step** are usually off by an hour of detection and decision.\n- **Log replay is the term people forget.** Point-in-time recovery restores the last base snapshot and\n  re-applies every logged change since it, so a weekly base backup with continuous logs has a tiny RPO\n  and a long, growing replay time.\n- **So base-backup frequency is an RTO lever,** while log-shipping frequency is an RPO lever.\n\n### Logical disasters add time to detect\n- **You can only restore to a point you still have.** For a bad migration, a bug quietly corrupting rows\n  or ransomware that encrypts slowly, retention must exceed the longest plausible time before anyone\n  notices.\n- **Corruption found after ten days with seven days of retention is unrecoverable,** regardless of RPO.\n- **Restoring to before the bad event discards later good writes,** so recovery from logical damage is\n  usually a *selective* restore into a side database, then repairing the affected rows, not a wholesale\n  rollback.\n\n### The four strategies\nNamed roughly as the AWS guidance does, in rising cost and falling RPO/RTO:\n\n| Strategy | What runs in the DR site | RPO | RTO | Fits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **1. Backup and restore** | periodic backups to another region or account; infrastructure rebuilt from code on demand | hours | hours to a day | cheapest; internal tools |\n| **2. Pilot light** | continuous data replication; minimum core (database replica, container images, secrets) always on, the rest created at failover | minutes | tens of minutes to hours | minutes of data loss acceptable, cheap idle cost |\n| **3. Warm standby** | a scaled-down but fully functional copy, scaled up on failover | seconds to minutes | minutes | downtime must be minutes |\n| **4. Multi-site active-active** | both sites serve traffic; failover is a routing change | near zero with synchronous replication | near zero | the multi-region pattern |\n\n### Mechanisms that make the numbers real\n- **Point-in-time recovery** from a base snapshot plus an archived log lets you restore to just before\n  a bad write, *because* a replica cannot un-apply a mistake but a log can be replayed up to a chosen\n  second.\n- **Immutable, isolated backups**: object-lock or vault policies, a separate account with separate\n  credentials, *because* a disaster that includes a compromised identity must not reach the backup.\n- **Copies separated by medium and place**, often summarised as the 3-2-1 rule: three copies, on two\n  different kinds of storage, one off-site; for cloud systems, read \"off-site\" as another region *and*\n  another account.\n- **Infrastructure as code** so the DR environment can be created, not hand-built, during the worst\n  hour of the year.\n- **Restore drills** on a schedule, timed, with the resulting RTA and RPA recorded. The drill also\n  catches the quiet killers: a backup job that has been silently failing, a missing KMS key grant.\n- **Runbooks** that name who decides, how failover is triggered, and how failback happens, including\n  the reconciliation of writes that landed after the recovery point.\n\n### The AI-specific inventory\nList every store, decide whether it is *restored* or *rebuilt*, and estimate the time:\n\n| Store | Restore or rebuild | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Conversation and session databases | restore | primary source of user state |\n| Vector indexes | restore from index snapshot in minutes, or rebuild by re-embedding in hours | record the embedding model version with the index, or a rebuild produces an index the query path cannot use |\n| Source documents in object storage | usually versioned | near-zero RPO |\n| Prompt and config registries | in git, trivially recoverable | only if the deploy pipeline reads from git, not a mutable store |\n| Fine-tuned weights and adapters | copy to a second region as artefacts | retraining is not a recovery plan |\n| Agent checkpoints and workflow histories | restore | runs after the recovery point replay from an earlier step |\n| Eval datasets and golden sets | restore | losing them silently breaks release gates |","example":"### Worked RTO/RPO budget for a RAG assistant\nAll figures are illustrative orders of magnitude.\n\n**Targets and state.** The business sets RPO 15 minutes and RTO 4 hours. State: a 500 GB Postgres\ndatabase (sessions, tenants, audit log), a vector index of 2 million chunks averaging 500 tokens each,\n300 GB of source documents in versioned object storage, prompts and configs in git, one fine-tuned adapter.\n\n**Current plan:** nightly Postgres snapshot copied cross-region, no index snapshot, adapter stored only\non the training machine.\n- **Worst-case RPO is 24 hours,** failing the 15-minute target.\n- **The adapter is unrecoverable.**\n\n| RTO step | Current plan | Revised plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| Detect and decide | 30 min | 30 min |\n| Provision from infrastructure code | 20 min | 20 min |\n| Restore 500 GB snapshot | roughly 45 min (no logs to replay, which is why the RPO is so bad) | 45 min |\n| Replay WAL | none | 15 min (up to a day of WAL after the nightly base snapshot) |\n| Vector index | rebuild: 2M × 500 = 1 billion tokens at provider rate limits in the low millions of tokens per minute, on the order of eight hours | snapshot restore of roughly 20 minutes |\n| Verify | 15 min | 15 min |\n| **Total** | **about 10 hours, failing RTO** | **≈ 145 minutes, inside 4 hours** |\n\n**Revised plan in detail.**\n- **Continuous WAL archiving** to the DR region with a one-minute archive interval: RPO about 1 minute,\n  meets 15.\n- **Vector index snapshot** to the DR region every six hours. Index RPO of 6 h is acceptable because\n  documents since then can be incrementally re-embedded in the background.\n- **Adapter and its training manifest** copied to artefact storage in both regions.\n- **Replay time must be measured:** 15 minutes is an estimate for this write volume, and replay speed\n  depends on write rate, not database size.\n- **A quarterly timed drill** replaces the estimates with actuals.\n- **Retention of 35 days** of snapshots and logs, because the team's worst past case of silent\n  corruption took two weeks to notice.\n\n### RTO and RPO calculator for a design review\nTurn the budget into code so it is recomputed every time a store or interval changes.\n\n```python\ndef worst_rpo_min(copy_interval_min):\n    return copy_interval_min                       # loss is bounded by the copy interval\n\ndef reembed_min(chunks, tokens_per_chunk, tokens_per_min):\n    return chunks * tokens_per_chunk / tokens_per_min\n\ndef rto_min(steps):\n    return sum(steps.values())                     # RTO is a sum, not the restore step alone\n\ncurrent = {\"detect_decide\": 30, \"provision\": 20, \"restore\": 45,\n           \"index\": reembed_min(2_000_000, 500, 2_000_000), \"verify\": 15}\nrevised = {\"detect_decide\": 30, \"provision\": 20, \"restore\": 45, \"replay\": 15,\n           \"index\": 20, \"verify\": 15}\n\nfor name, steps, rpo in ((\"current\", current, worst_rpo_min(24 * 60)),\n                         (\"revised\", revised, worst_rpo_min(1))):\n    rto = rto_min(steps)\n    print(f\"{name}: RPO {rpo} min ({'ok' if rpo <= 15 else 'FAIL'}), \"\n          f\"RTO {rto:.0f} min ({'ok' if rto <= 240 else 'FAIL'})\")\n# current: RPO 1440 min (FAIL), RTO 610 min (FAIL)   index rebuild alone is 500 min at 2M tokens/min\n# revised: RPO 1 min (ok), RTO 145 min (ok)\n```","patterns":["**Continuous WAL archiving plus periodic base backups.** For Postgres, archive WAL to object storage in the DR region (tools such as pgBackRest or WAL-G do this) and take base backups often enough that replay stays inside RTO. Monitor archive lag and the age of the newest base backup.","**Cross-account, object-locked backup vault.** Write backups into a separate account with its own credentials and object-lock or vault-lock retention, so a compromised production identity cannot delete them. Monitor backup job success and alert on any retention-policy change.","**Index snapshots tagged with the embedding model.** Snapshot the vector index on a schedule and store the embedding model name and version beside it; the restore script refuses a mismatch. Monitor snapshot age against the index RPO.","**Timed quarterly restore drill.** Restore into a clean environment from infrastructure code, run a smoke test and the golden eval set, and record RPA and RTA per store. Monitor the gap between objective and actual, and treat a missed objective as an incident finding.","**Retention sized to detection time.** Set snapshot and log retention longer than the slowest past case of noticed corruption (35 days in the example above). Monitor the oldest restorable point."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Last recoverable copy\"] -->|\"gap is the recovery point, bounded by RPO\"| T[\"Disaster at T\"]\n    T --> D[\"Detect\"]\n    D --> DE[\"Decide: who declares DR\"]\n    DE --> P[\"Provision from infrastructure code\"]\n    P --> R[\"Restore snapshot\"]\n    R --> L[\"Replay archived logs\"]\n    L --> I[\"Restore or rebuild vector index\"]\n    I --> V[\"Re-point traffic and verify\"]\n    V -->|\"elapsed time is the recovery time, bounded by RTO\"| S[\"Service restored\"]","caption":"The timeline view: RPO looks backwards from the disaster to the last copy, RTO looks forwards and is\nthe sum of every step until verified service."},"interview":[{"q":"Define RPO and RTO, and explain what each one is bounded by.","a":"- **RPO** is the maximum tolerated data loss measured in time; it is bounded by how often state is copied\n  away (nightly snapshot up to 24 h, minute-level WAL archiving about a minute).\n- **RTO** is the maximum tolerated downtime; it is a sum of detect, decide, provision, restore, replay,\n  re-point and verify, so measuring only the restore step underestimates it."},{"q":"A bug silently corrupted rows for twelve days before anyone noticed. How do you recover?","a":"- **First check retention:** you can only restore to a point you still have; with seven days of logs the\n  clean state is gone.\n- **If you have it, restore selectively** into a side database to just before the first bad write, then\n  repair the affected rows in production, because a wholesale rollback would discard twelve days of good\n  writes."},{"q":"When is backup-and-restore enough, and when do you need a warm standby?","a":"- **Backup-and-restore** fits when hours of downtime cost less than running a standby, such as internal tools.\n- **Warm standby** fits when the business needs RTO in minutes; you pay for a scaled-down copy running\n  continuously.\n- **Let the business impact analysis decide** per capability rather than picking one tier for everything."},{"q":"Your plan says RTO 30 minutes, but the drill took two hours. Where did the time go?","a":"- **Usually detection and decision,** plus traffic cut-over and verification that the plan never counted.\n- **Then log replay:** a weekly base backup means days of WAL to re-apply.\n- **And derived state:** rebuilding a vector index by re-embedding a billion tokens takes hours, so\n  snapshot it instead."},{"q":"Why should a RAG system snapshot its vector index if it can be rebuilt, and what must be stored with it?","a":"- **Rebuild time counts in RTO:** re-embedding can take hours and provider spend, while a snapshot restores\n  in minutes.\n- **Store the embedding model version** with the snapshot, because a rebuild or restore with a different\n  model produces vectors the query path cannot use."}],"tradeoffs":["**Shorter RPO vs cost.** It means more frequent or continuous replication, costing bandwidth, storage and sometimes write latency; it flips to \"nightly is fine\" when the data is cheaply re-derivable.","**Shorter RTO vs idle infrastructure.** It means more kept running idle (warm standby, active-active); it flips to backup-and-restore when an hour of downtime costs less than the standby's monthly bill.","**Rebuild vs snapshot derived state.** Rebuilding vector indexes saves storage but spends hours and provider tokens during the worst moment; snapshotting costs storage every day to save that one day.","**Immutable backups vs agility.** Immutable, separately-owned backups protect against compromised credentials and bad automation but make routine cleanup and retention changes slower and more bureaucratic.","**Automated vs human-decided failover.** Automation shortens RTO but can trigger on false alarms and cause data loss or split brain; a human decision step adds minutes and removes that class of error."],"pitfalls":["**Backups that are never restored.** The job has been failing for months or the restore needs a key nobody has. The symptom is discovering it during the disaster; the cure is timed drills.","**Replication mistaken for backup.** A bad migration or deletion is faithfully copied to every replica within seconds, and no point-in-time copy exists to roll back to.","**Recovering the database but not the rest of the inventory.** The restored assistant answers from an empty index or an older prompt and the incident continues as a quality incident.","**Backups in the same account and region as production.** Reachable by the same credentials, so one leaked key or one wrong Terraform destroy removes both.","**Measuring RTO as the restore step only.** Forgetting detection, decision and traffic cut-over; the drill shows two hours where the plan said thirty minutes.","**Retention shorter than the time to notice logical corruption.** The symptom is a bug report about bad data whose first bad write is older than the oldest backup."],"when":"- **Every production system needs a DR plan;** the choice is the strategy tier, which follows from the\n  business impact analysis: what an hour of downtime and an hour of lost data cost per capability.\n- **Pick the cheapest tier that meets the agreed RPO and RTO,** write the inventory of state and whether\n  each item is restored or rebuilt, automate the restore, and drill it at least quarterly with actuals\n  recorded.\n- **Use cross-region or cross-account immutable backups** the moment the data has compliance value or\n  the threat model includes compromised credentials.\n- **Day to day:** the design-review question \"where does this new store get backed up and who restores it\".\n- **Interviews:** derive RPO from the replication mechanism and RTO as a sum, and explain why replicas\n  are not backups.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds every new store to the backup inventory, records embedding and model versions with index snapshots, and writes the restore script that the drill actually runs.","architect":"Chooses the DR tier per capability from the agreed RPO/RTO, owns the state inventory and the restore-versus-rebuild decision for derived data like indexes.","fde":"Answers the customer's RPO/RTO questionnaire with measured drill results, and aligns the deployment's backups with the customer's own retention and residency requirements."},"check":{"q":"A team says \"our Postgres has a synchronous replica in another zone, so our RPO is zero and we\ndo not need backups.\" Where does this reasoning fail, and what would you add?","a":"The replica protects against the primary's hardware or zone failing, which is high availability,\nnot disaster recovery. It replicates every write, including the destructive ones: a mistaken\n`DELETE`, a bad migration, an application bug corrupting rows, or an attacker with the database\ncredential. After any of those, both copies are equally wrong within milliseconds, and the RPO for\nthat class of disaster is effectively infinite because there is no earlier state to return to. A\nregional outage or an account compromise can also take both. I would add base snapshots plus\ncontinuous WAL archiving to a separate region and account with immutable retention, giving\npoint-in-time recovery to any second before the bad event, and a timed quarterly restore drill so the\nRTO is a measured number. I would also extend the inventory beyond Postgres to the vector index,\nprompt registry and model artefacts."},"quiz":[{"q":"A system takes a full backup every night at 02:00. Data is destroyed at 13:00. What is the recovery point loss, and which objective does it test?","options":["13 hours, testing the RTO","11 hours, testing the RPO","24 hours, testing the RTO","Zero, because the backup can be replayed forward to 13:00"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The last recoverable state is the 02:00 backup, so 11 hours of writes are lost; that gap is the recovery point. A full backup alone cannot be rolled forward; that requires archived logs."},{"q":"Why is a synchronous database replica not a substitute for backups?","options":["Replicas are always slower to restore than backups","Replicas cannot be placed in another availability zone","Replication copies destructive writes and corruption immediately, so there is no earlier state to recover to","Replicas increase RPO because they lag the primary"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A replica is a live copy of the present, including mistakes. Point-in-time recovery needs history: snapshots plus logs that let you stop just before the bad write."},{"q":"For a RAG system, why should the vector index be snapshotted even though it can be rebuilt from the source documents?","options":["Source documents cannot be backed up reliably","Rebuilt indexes return different search results from the original","Rebuilding means re-embedding every chunk, which can take hours and provider spend, directly inflating RTO","Vector databases do not support restoring from a snapshot"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Rebuild is a valid recovery path, but its duration is part of the recovery time. A snapshot restore turns hours of re-embedding into minutes, usually for modest storage cost."}]},{"key":"reliability/business-continuity","sources":["ISO 22301 — Security and resilience, Business continuity management systems","NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 — Contingency Planning Guide for Federal Information Systems","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **DR answers \"how do we bring the system back\", not the hour in between.** Which functions must keep\n  working, who may declare the failover, how customers are told, what staff do by hand, and what happens\n  when the failure is a supplier you cannot restore.\n- **A fast restore without authority is not continuity.** A company that restores its database in forty\n  minutes but spends six hours finding who may approve the restore has a DR plan and no continuity.\n- **More redundancy cannot touch many disruptions.** A provider deprecates your tuned model with ninety\n  days' notice; a vendor changes terms so your use case is no longer permitted; a regulator halts\n  processing of a data category; the engineer holding undocumented knowledge leaves. None show on an\n  uptime dashboard, and all stop a function as thoroughly as a region outage.\n- **AI products concentrate these risks.** Many run a core function on a single external model, a single\n  embedding model the whole index depends on, and a few people who understand the evaluation set.\n- **Buyers ask.** \"Describe your business continuity plan\" and \"what happens if your AI vendor is\n  unavailable\" are standard security-questionnaire lines, and a credible answer is often a precondition\n  for the deal.","how":"### Business impact analysis first\nThe BIA is a ranked inventory of business **functions, not systems**. For each function record:\n- what it does and who depends on it;\n- the **maximum tolerable downtime** (how long before harm becomes unacceptable; some frameworks call it\n  maximum acceptable outage);\n- the resources, systems and upstream suppliers it needs;\n- the cost of an hour of disruption in money, compliance exposure and customer trust.\n\n- **RPO and RTO for each supporting system are derived from this,** which is why an honest BIA often\n  shrinks the list needing expensive recovery: most functions can wait a day, a few cannot wait ten minutes.\n- **Restoring the system is not restoring the function.** People still clear the backlog, re-enter work\n  done by hand and verify results, so the system RTO must leave room:\n\n```\nRTO + time to catch up and verify ≤ maximum tolerable downtime\n```\n\n- **Example:** a function that tolerates four hours and needs two hours of catch-up gets an RTO of two\n  hours, not four.\n\n### Threat and dependency mapping\nFor each critical function, list how it can be disrupted and through which dependency:\n\n| Category | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Infrastructure | region, network |\n| Suppliers | model provider, vector database SaaS, identity provider |\n| Data | corruption, loss of access |\n| People | key-person loss, mass unavailability |\n| External | legal, regulatory, reputational |\n\n- **AI-specific entries:** supplier concentration on one model vendor, model and API deprecation\n  timelines, sudden pricing or quota changes, and prompts and eval sets as intellectual property that may\n  live in one person's head or one unbacked-up notebook.\n- **FMEA is the structured tool** for this step.\n\n### Strategies per function, in a ladder\nEach critical function gets a sequence of fallbacks, cheapest and most automatic first:\n1. **Automatic technical fallback**: secondary provider, smaller self-hosted model, cached results.\n   Owned by engineering and covered by fault-tolerance mechanisms.\n2. **Degraded service**: retrieval-only answers, templated responses, longer queues, with customers\n   informed. Owned by product; the acceptable degradation is decided in advance, not in the incident.\n3. **Manual workaround**: humans perform the function with a documented procedure and the minimum\n   tooling, *because* the business function matters more than the automation that normally does it.\n4. **Suspension with communication**: the function pauses, customers and regulators are told what and\n   when, and the backlog plan is known.\n\n### The three phases of an event\n| Phase | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Response** | detect, contain, decide to activate, communicate |\n| **Continuity** | operate on the chosen rung of the ladder for as long as needed |\n| **Recovery** | restore the primary path, work off the backlog, reconcile manual or fallback work into the systems of record, stand down |\n\n- **Plans are detailed about response and vague about recovery,** yet recovery is where data gets lost:\n  tickets triaged on a spreadsheet never entered, fallback-model answers nobody re-checked.\n\n### Plans, roles and communication\nThe business continuity plan documents:\n- **activation criteria:** who may declare an event and on what evidence;\n- **roles with named deputies** and decision authority during the event;\n- **a communication tree** for staff, customers and regulators;\n- **recovery and return-to-normal steps.**\n\nIt is written for someone reading it at 3 a.m. with the usual experts unavailable, a different document\nfrom an architecture diagram.\n\n### Testing and maintenance\n- **Plans decay.**\n- **Tabletop exercises** walk the team through a scenario on paper and surface missing decisions.\n- **Functional drills** actually execute the fallback: switch provider in production for an hour, process\n  a day's work manually for a sample.\n- **Each exercise ends with recorded findings and owners.**\n- **Review** when architecture, suppliers or staffing change, and at least annually. ISO 22301 formalises\n  this lifecycle; regulated customers may ask for alignment with it.\n\n### Vendor exit as a continuity control\nFor every critical supplier, know the exit:\n- portable prompts and evals that have been run on a second model;\n- an export path for the vector index and its embedding-model version;\n- contract clauses on notice periods;\n- a tested procedure to switch.\n\nThe exit plan is the BC answer to supplier failure, and it turns a model deprecation from a crisis into\nscheduled work.","example":"### Worked continuity plan for AI support triage\nFigures illustrative.\n\n**BIA entry.** A SaaS company lists \"classify and route inbound support tickets\" as critical:\n- about 300 tickets per hour at peak;\n- a contractual first-response time of four hours for enterprise customers;\n- maximum tolerable downtime of two hours before SLA penalties start;\n- dependencies: one model provider for classification, a vector database SaaS for similar-ticket\n  retrieval, the ticketing system, and two engineers who maintain the prompts and the 1,200-item eval set;\n- derived targets: RTO one hour for the triage service, RPO five minutes for the routing audit log.\n\n**Threats recorded:** provider outage or quota cut, provider model deprecation, vector SaaS outage, loss\nof both prompt maintainers, a prompt-injection incident that forces a halt.\n\n**Ladder for a provider outage.**\n\n| Time | Rung | Detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| T+0 | automatic fallback | second provider, prompts already validated against the eval set with a recorded accuracy gap |\n| T+10 minutes | degraded service | if that also fails: keyword rules route to a general queue, with a banner telling agents triage is degraded |\n| T+15 minutes | communication | status page updates |\n| T+30 minutes | manual workaround | if backlog exceeds 150 tickets, the support lead activates it: two agents triage from a shared view |\n| T+60 | communication | enterprise customers with contractual notification clauses are emailed |\n\n- **Manual capacity:** the BIA shows two agents sustain roughly half of peak volume, so the backlog grows\n  at about 150 tickets per hour, and enterprise tickets are pulled first to protect the four-hour SLA.\n- **Why RTO is one hour against two hours of tolerable downtime:** an hour at peak on the manual rung\n  leaves roughly 150 untriaged tickets. The restored service needs time to work them off and to have the\n  manually routed tickets checked before the enterprise response clock runs out.\n\n**Deprecation and people risk.**\n- The second provider is exercised in production for one hour every month.\n- The eval set lives in the repository with two named owners plus a documented onboarding runbook.\n- The plan is tabletop-tested twice a year; last year's drill found the fallback provider's key had\n  expired, which is now monitored.\n\n### Deriving RTO and sizing the manual rung\nA small model of the arithmetic above, so the plan is recomputed when volume or staffing changes. The\nrestored-service throughput of 600 tickets per hour is an illustrative assumption.\n\n```python\ndef derived_rto_h(max_tolerable_downtime_h, catch_up_h):\n    return max_tolerable_downtime_h - catch_up_h      # RTO + catch-up <= MTD\n\ndef backlog_after(hours, arrivals_per_h, capacity_per_h, start=0):\n    return max(0, start + (arrivals_per_h - capacity_per_h) * hours)\n\ndef drain_hours(backlog, restored_per_h, arrivals_per_h):\n    spare = restored_per_h - arrivals_per_h\n    return float(\"inf\") if spare <= 0 else backlog / spare\n\npeak, manual = 300, 150                               # manual mode sustains about half of peak\nleft = backlog_after(1, peak, manual)                 # one hour on the manual rung\nprint(left, drain_hours(left, restored_per_h=600, arrivals_per_h=peak))\n# 150 0.5   -> half an hour of catch-up, plus checking manual routes\nprint(derived_rto_h(4, 2))\n# 2         -> a function tolerating 4 h with 2 h catch-up gets a 2 h RTO\n```\n\n- **If `drain_hours` returns infinity,** the restored service cannot outrun arrivals and the backlog plan\n  needs extra capacity, not a shorter RTO.","patterns":["**Monthly live fallback exercise.** Route a slice of production traffic, or all of it for one hour, to the second provider with its validated prompts. Monitor the eval-measured accuracy gap, fallback error rate and credential expiry.","**Degraded mode behind a runtime flag.** Make each ladder rung switchable without a deploy, through a feature-flag service or config store, and show the active rung in the UI. Monitor which rung is active and for how long.","**Named activation roles with deputies.** Keep a one-page plan listing who may declare an event, their deputies, and the evidence required, linked from the incident channel. Monitor time from detection to activation in every drill.","**Vendor-exit checklist per critical supplier.** Record the second model's eval score, index export path with embedding-model version, contract notice period and last tested switch date. Review it when a deprecation notice arrives or quarterly.","**Reconciliation step in every recovery.** After stand-down, enter manually handled work into the system of record and re-check fallback-model outputs on a sample. Monitor items still unreconciled."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    E[\"Disruption detected\"] --> A[\"Automatic fallback: second provider\"]\n    A -->|\"works\"| R[\"Recovery: restore, catch up, reconcile\"]\n    A -->|\"fails\"| D[\"Degraded service: rules, banner\"]\n    D -->|\"backlog under threshold\"| R\n    D -->|\"backlog over threshold\"| M[\"Manual workaround, named lead activates\"]\n    M -->|\"volume sustainable\"| R\n    M -->|\"cannot sustain\"| S[\"Suspend and communicate\"]\n    S --> R\n    R --> F[\"Record findings and owners\"]","caption":"The continuity ladder for one critical function: each rung activates only when the cheaper, more\nautomatic one above it fails, and every path ends in a recovery phase that reconciles work."},"interview":[{"q":"How is business continuity different from disaster recovery?","a":"- **BC is function-first:** it keeps a business function running by any means, including manual work, a\n  different supplier or a communicated pause.\n- **DR is the technical subset** that restores systems; it does not decide who declares the event, how\n  customers are told, or what staff do by hand meanwhile."},{"q":"A function tolerates two hours of downtime. How do you set the RTO of the system behind it?","a":"- **Subtract catch-up and verification:** `RTO + time to catch up and verify ≤ maximum tolerable downtime`.\n- **So with an hour of backlog work and checks,** the system RTO is one hour, not two.\n- **Recompute when volume grows,** because a bigger backlog lengthens catch-up."},{"q":"Design the fallback ladder for an AI feature whose only provider goes down.","a":"- **Rung 1:** automatic switch to a second provider with prompts validated on the eval set.\n- **Rung 2:** degraded service decided in advance, such as rules or retrieval-only answers with a banner.\n- **Rung 3:** a documented manual workaround with a named lead; **rung 4:** suspend and communicate, with a\n  backlog plan."},{"q":"When is a validated second model provider not worth it?","a":"- **When the function is non-critical** and can wait for the primary to return; running evals twice and\n  managing behaviour differences then costs more than the outage.\n- **It pays** once contractual penalties, enterprise deals or deprecation risk make a dated loss of the\n  model expensive."},{"q":"What would you measure to show a customer your continuity plan works?","a":"- **Time to activate the fallback** in drills, and drill findings closed with owners.\n- **Sustainable throughput of the manual mode,** for example half of peak, and the resulting backlog growth.\n- **Last successful live fallback exercise date** and the measured accuracy gap of the second provider."}],"tradeoffs":["**Thorough BIA vs owner time.** It focuses spend on the functions that matter, but takes real time from business owners; it flips to a lightweight version for small teams whose entire product is one function.","**Manual workarounds vs volume.** They keep the function alive without any system, yet sustain only a fraction of volume; worth documenting when the function is critical and volume is manageable.","**Validated second provider vs eval cost.** It gives a real exit and fallback, at the cost of running evals twice and accepting behaviour differences; it stops paying when the function is non-critical.","**Frequent drills vs team time.** They keep plans honest but consume engineering and operations time; the balance tilts to more drills as contractual penalties and regulatory exposure grow.","**Detailed vs short plans.** Detailed plans cover more scenarios but go stale faster; shorter plans with clear roles and a decision tree survive turnover better than hundred-page binders."],"pitfalls":["**Writing the plan for systems instead of functions.** The team restores the database while nobody handles the customers; the symptom is a \"successful recovery\" followed by churn and penalties.","**Fallbacks that exist on paper only.** The secondary provider was never run against the eval set, and its first production use reveals a large accuracy drop or broken tool schemas.","**Undefined activation authority.** Visible as an hour lost to \"who can approve this\" while the backlog grows; name the roles and deputies in advance.","**Single points of knowledge.** One engineer understands the prompts and the eval set, and the plan silently assumes they are reachable. Key-person loss is a continuity event.","**No recovery phase in the plan.** Work done manually during the event is never reconciled, visible weeks later as tickets, orders or approvals that exist on paper or in a chat thread but not in the system of record.","**Ignoring supplier-side disruptions** such as deprecations, quota cuts and terms changes because the dashboard is green; the symptom is a ninety-day deadline nobody planned for."],"when":"- **Do a BIA** the moment an AI feature supports a function someone depends on contractually or\n  operationally.\n- **Revisit it** when you add a supplier, drop a human from the loop, or sign an enterprise customer with\n  notification or recovery clauses.\n- **Keep it proportionate:** a one-page ladder with named roles beats a binder nobody reads.\n- **Measure** drill findings closed, time to activate the fallback, and the sustainable throughput of the\n  manual mode, since auditors and customers will ask for those numbers.\n- **Day to day:** the \"what do we do if the provider is down for a day\" conversation and the vendor-exit\n  checklist.\n- **Interviews:** BC is function-first and includes people, suppliers and communication; DR is the\n  technical restore underneath it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds and exercises the automatic fallback tier, keeps the second provider's prompts and evals current, and makes the degraded mode observable and switchable without a deploy.","architect":"Owns the dependency map and supplier concentration risk, sets the degradation ladder per function, and translates the BIA's tolerable downtime into system RPO and RTO.","fde":"Writes the customer-facing continuity answer for questionnaires, aligns the plan with the customer's own BC programme, and runs the joint tabletop that proves both sides know their roles."},"check":{"q":"Your assistant's only model provider announces that the model you use will be retired in ninety\ndays, and its replacement scores noticeably lower on your eval set. Is this a business continuity\nevent, and how should a mature plan have prepared for it?","a":"Yes. Nothing is down, but a critical function faces a dated loss of its primary dependency with\na quality regression attached, which is exactly a supplier disruption in BIA terms. A mature plan\nwould already hold an exit path: prompts and tool schemas kept portable, a second provider or a\nself-hosted model evaluated on the same set with a recorded gap, and the eval set itself versioned\nwith named owners so the comparison can be rerun this week rather than rebuilt. The response is then\nscheduled work: run the eval against the replacement and the alternates, pick the best, tune prompts\nwith the gap measured, canary the switch, and update the ladder so the retired model is no longer a\nfallback. Communication to customers who contractually care about model changes happens on the\nplan's timeline. Without that preparation, the same notice becomes a ninety-day scramble and a\nsilent quality drop."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the relationship between business continuity and disaster recovery?","options":["They are synonyms used by different industries","DR is the technical subset of BC that restores systems; BC also covers functions, people, suppliers and communication","BC is for small outages and DR is for large ones","DR is planned by the business and BC by engineering"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Continuity asks how the function keeps running, including by hand or through a different supplier. Recovery asks how the system comes back. A fast restore with nobody handling customers is DR without BC."},{"q":"Where should the RTO for a system come from?","options":["The cloud provider's published recovery figures for the service","The engineering team's estimate of how fast a restore can run","The business impact analysis's maximum tolerable downtime for the functions that system supports","The average duration of past incidents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Objectives are requirements set by business impact, then met by engineering. Deriving them from what the system can already do inverts the logic and hides the gap the plan exists to close."},{"q":"Which of these is a business continuity risk that uptime monitoring will not detect?","options":["A model provider returning 503 errors for ten minutes","A database primary failing over to its replica","The one engineer who maintains the prompts and the evaluation set resigning","A load balancer health check marking a node unhealthy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Key-person loss stops a function's ability to change or recover safely without any system going down. BC treats people and knowledge as dependencies to be documented and duplicated."}]},{"key":"reliability/durable-workflow-execution","sources":["Official docs: Temporal","Garcia-Molina & Salem, 1987 — Sagas","Helland, 2007 — Life beyond Distributed Transactions"],"why":"- **Real processes span time and systems.** Draft a reply with a model, wait for a human to approve,\n  send it, poll a vendor for confirmation, retry tomorrow if the vendor is down.\n- **One long function loses the process.** A worker dies halfway during a deploy: the draft was made and\n  paid for, the approval request was sent, and nobody knows which step completed.\n- **The hand-built fix grows the same bugs.** A `status` column plus a cron job that scans for stuck rows\n  and a retry loop around each step gives: steps that run twice because the status update raced with the\n  crash, timers reimplemented as polling, \"waiting for approval\" rows nobody sweeps, a state machine spread\n  across three services, and no way to change logic while thousands of processes are mid-flight.\n- **Queues give retries, not orchestration.** A queue cannot express \"wait 48 hours, then if no signal\n  arrived, escalate\".\n- **Agents make it acute.** Each model or tool call can take seconds to minutes, fail transiently and cost\n  real money. Re-running the loop duplicates side effects and token spend; abandoning it loses the user's\n  work. Durable execution resumes a crash at step 14 of 20 at step 14, with steps 1 to 13 decided and no\n  model re-calls.","how":"### The core idea: event sourcing applied to a program's execution\n- **Every decision becomes an event.** A durable workflow engine records \"activity X scheduled\",\n  \"activity X completed with result R\", \"timer T started\", \"signal S received\" in a durable store.\n- **Many engines, one model.** Temporal and its predecessor Cadence, AWS Step Functions, Azure Durable\n  Functions, Restate, Inngest, DBOS and others differ in surface but share it.\n- **Memory is reconstructible.** Workflow code never holds state that matters only in memory; it can be\n  rebuilt from the history.\n\n### Replay\n1. A worker crashes or is redeployed; another worker picks up the workflow.\n2. It re-executes the workflow code from the beginning *against the recorded history*.\n3. Each step whose result is already in the history gets the recorded result instead of running again.\n4. The code reaches the same point with identical local variables, then continues with the first step\n   that has no recorded outcome.\n\n- **Hence determinism.** No wall-clock reads, random numbers, direct network or database calls, or\n  iteration over unordered maps inside the workflow function, *because* a different decision during replay\n  would diverge from history and the engine would have to fail the workflow.\n- **Engines provide deterministic substitutes:** `workflow.now()`, seeded random, versioned patches.\n\n### Activities: where the real world lives\n- **Everything non-deterministic or side-effecting is an activity:** every LLM call, tool call, HTTP\n  request and database write.\n- **Activities run outside the replay machinery,** with timeouts and a retry policy, and their *results*\n  are what gets recorded.\n- **The engine guarantees** an activity is retried until it succeeds or the policy gives up, and that its\n  result is never recomputed on replay.\n- **It cannot guarantee exactly once.** A worker can complete the side effect and crash before reporting,\n  and the retry runs it again.\n- **So activities must be idempotent,** with a key derived from the workflow and step identity: the same\n  exactly-once limitation that applies to every distributed system.\n\n### Durable timers, signals and queries\n| Primitive | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Timer** | `sleep(30 days)` is an event, not a blocked thread; the worker is free and any worker resumes the workflow when it fires, across deploys |\n| **Signal** | delivers external input, such as a human approval or a webhook, into a running workflow |\n| **Query** | reads current state without affecting history |\n\n- **Together they replace cron-plus-polling** with code that reads as a straight-line program.\n\n### Task queues and workers\nWorkflows and activities are dispatched through named task queues that workers poll. This gives:\n- **natural bulkheads:** GPU-bound activities on one queue, email on another;\n- **per-queue concurrency limits** that act as backpressure;\n- **heartbeats** for long activities, so a dead worker is detected and its activity rescheduled rather\n  than waiting for the full timeout.\n\n### Living with long-running code\n- **Versioning.** Deploying new workflow code while old histories exist can break replay, so engines offer\n  a patch marker that lets new code take the old branch for histories recorded before the change.\n- **Continue-as-new.** Very long or unbounded loops, like an agent that may take thousands of steps, close\n  the current history and start a fresh one carrying only the needed state, *because* replaying millions\n  of events is slow and memory-hungry.\n\n### Failure and compensation\n- **Exhausted retries raise an error inside workflow code,** where ordinary control flow decides what\n  happens next.\n- **So the saga pattern is a `try`/`except`:** record a compensating action after each step that succeeds\n  (cancel the booking, refund the charge, retract the draft) and run them in reverse order if a later step\n  fails for good.\n- **Compensations are activities too,** so they are retried and must be idempotent.\n- **This is forward recovery:** the external world cannot be rolled back, only corrected, so the workflow\n  encodes how to correct it.\n\n### Compared with the alternatives\n| Approach | Gives | Lacks |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hand-built saga | compensation | resumption |\n| Queue | retries | orchestration |\n| Declarative state machine (Step Functions style) | durability with JSON-defined states; excellent for fixed pipelines | awkward for branching agent logic |\n| Code-first engine | ordinary programs | simplicity: determinism rules apply |","example":"### A Temporal-style approval workflow\nA Temporal-style workflow (Python SDK conventions; check names against the current docs) in which the\nLLM call and the send are activities, and the approval wait is a durable timer plus signal. A crash\nafter `draft_reply` completes resumes at the wait without calling the model again.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom temporalio import activity, workflow\nfrom temporalio.common import RetryPolicy\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def draft_reply(ticket_id: str) -> str:\n    return await llm.complete(f\"Draft a reply for ticket {ticket_id}\")       # your model client\n\n@activity.defn\nasync def send_reply(ticket_id: str, text: str) -> None:\n    info = activity.info()     # same workflow_id and activity_id on every retry of this step\n    await mailer.send(ticket_id, text, idempotency_key=f\"{info.workflow_id}:{info.activity_id}\")\n\n@workflow.defn\nclass TicketReplyWorkflow:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.approved: bool | None = None\n\n    @workflow.signal\n    def approve(self, ok: bool) -> None:          # delivered by the review UI\n        self.approved = ok\n\n    @workflow.run\n    async def run(self, ticket_id: str) -> str:\n        draft = await workflow.execute_activity(\n            draft_reply, ticket_id,\n            start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(seconds=90),\n            retry_policy=RetryPolicy(maximum_attempts=3, initial_interval=timedelta(seconds=2)),\n        )\n        try:   # durable wait: no worker is blocked, and it survives restarts and deploys\n            await workflow.wait_condition(lambda: self.approved is not None, timeout=timedelta(days=2))\n        except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n            return \"expired\"\n        if not self.approved:\n            return \"rejected\"\n        await workflow.execute_activity(send_reply, args=[ticket_id, draft],\n                                        start_to_close_timeout=timedelta(seconds=30))\n        return \"sent\"\n```\n\n- **Notice what is *not* in the workflow:** no `datetime.now()`, no HTTP, no database. Those would be\n  re-executed on replay.\n- **The model is called inside an activity** precisely because it is non-deterministic; its recorded\n  result is what the replayed workflow sees.\n- **The idempotency key comes from workflow and step identity, not the ticket,** *because* a ticket may\n  legitimately get a second reply from a later workflow, while a retry of this step must never send twice.\n\n### Trace one run through a redeploy\nThe reviewer is slow, and the worker is redeployed on day one. The history so far (simplified; real\nTemporal histories also interleave workflow-task and activity-started events):\n\n```\n1 WorkflowExecutionStarted  ticket_id=\"T-812\"\n2 ActivityTaskScheduled     draft_reply\n3 ActivityTaskCompleted     result=\"Hi Dana, the refund was issued on ...\"\n4 TimerStarted              2 days   (from wait_condition's timeout)\n```\n\n1. A new worker picks the workflow up and runs `run()` from the top.\n2. At `execute_activity(draft_reply)` the engine finds events 2 and 3 and returns the recorded draft\n   without calling the model.\n3. At `wait_condition` it finds the timer and simply waits.\n4. The `approve` signal arrives on day two and is appended as a `WorkflowExecutionSignaled` event; the\n   condition is now true.\n5. The code schedules `send_reply`, which has no recorded result and therefore really runs.\n\n- **One model call in total,** however many times the workers restarted.\n\n### A toy replay engine you can run\nPlain Python, no engine: a context records each step's result and, on replay, returns it instead of\nexecuting. It shows why steps before the crash cost nothing the second time, and how a code change\nsurfaces as a non-determinism error.\n\n```python\nclass Crash(Exception):\n    pass\n\nclass Ctx:\n    def __init__(self, history):\n        self.history, self.pos, self.executed = history, 0, []\n\n    def activity(self, name, fn):\n        if self.pos < len(self.history):                  # replay: return the recorded result\n            recorded, result = self.history[self.pos]\n            if recorded != name:\n                raise RuntimeError(f\"non-determinism: history has {recorded}, code asked {name}\")\n        else:                                             # new step: really run it, then record\n            result = fn()\n            self.executed.append(name)\n            self.history.append((name, result))\n        self.pos += 1\n        return result\n\ndef research(ctx, topic, crash_after=None):\n    notes = []\n    for i in range(4):\n        notes.append(ctx.activity(f\"step{i}\", lambda i=i: f\"{topic}-note{i}\"))\n        if crash_after == i:\n            raise Crash()\n    return notes\n\nhistory = []                                              # stands in for the durable store\ntry:\n    research(Ctx(history), \"kv\", crash_after=1)\nexcept Crash:\n    pass\nctx = Ctx(history)\nprint(research(ctx, \"kv\"), ctx.executed)\n# ['kv-note0', 'kv-note1', 'kv-note2', 'kv-note3'] ['step2', 'step3']\n```\n\n- **Only `step2` and `step3` ran after the crash;** the first two returned recorded results.\n- **Rename a step and replay fails loudly,** which is why real engines need versioning patches.","patterns":["**Model and tools in activities, decisions in the workflow.** Enforce it in code review and keep workflow modules free of network clients; Temporal's Python SDK runs workflows in a sandbox that blocks many non-deterministic calls. Alert on non-determinism errors after every deploy.","**Non-retryable errors and bounded retries.** Set a retry policy with a maximum attempts or expiry and list validation and auth error types as non-retryable (Temporal's `RetryPolicy` accepts `non_retryable_error_types`). Monitor retry counts per activity type and workflows open longer than their expected duration.","**Heartbeats on long model calls.** Call `activity.heartbeat()` periodically inside multi-minute activities and set a `heartbeat_timeout` well below the `start_to_close_timeout`, so a dead worker's activity is rescheduled quickly. Monitor heartbeat timeouts per worker pool.","**Task-queue bulkheads.** Route GPU-bound inference activities to their own task queue with a worker concurrency limit, and email or tool activities to another. Monitor queue backlog and schedule-to-start latency per queue, which is the backpressure signal.","**Continue-as-new on a step budget.** For agent loops, count steps in workflow state and call continue-as-new every few hundred steps, carrying only the needed state. Monitor history size and replay latency.","**References, not payloads.** Store full documents and long transcripts in blob storage and pass keys between activities, keeping history small. Monitor per-event payload size."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant W1 as Worker 1\n    participant H as History store\n    participant W2 as Worker 2\n    participant M as Model API\n    W1->>M: draft_reply activity\n    M-->>W1: draft text\n    W1->>H: append ActivityTaskCompleted and TimerStarted\n    Note over W1: redeployed, process dies\n    W2->>H: load history, replay run from top\n    H-->>W2: recorded draft, no model call\n    Note over W2: waits on timer or approve signal\n    W2->>H: append WorkflowExecutionSignaled\n    W2->>W2: run send_reply activity for real","caption":"A worker restart mid-run: replay returns recorded results for finished steps, so only the step with no\nrecorded outcome really executes."},"interview":[{"q":"What is durable execution, and how does it differ from a job queue with retries?","a":"- **Durable execution** records every step's result in an event history and resumes by replaying it, so a\n  crash continues from the interrupted step.\n- **A queue with retries** is at-least-once for the whole job, so a crash re-runs every earlier model and\n  tool call; it also cannot express waits like \"48 hours, then escalate\"."},{"q":"Why can't workflow code call `datetime.now()` or the model directly?","a":"- **Recovery re-executes the code against history.** A different clock value or model answer would take a\n  different branch, the history would no longer match, and the engine would fail the workflow.\n- **So** use the engine's deterministic substitutes for time and randomness, and put every model call in an\n  activity whose recorded result replay returns."},{"q":"After a deploy, workflows started failing with non-determinism errors. What happened and how do you fix it?","a":"- **Likely cause:** the new code changed the order or set of steps for histories recorded under the old\n  code, or added a non-deterministic call.\n- **Fix:** guard the change with a versioning patch so old histories take the old branch, remove the\n  non-deterministic call, and replay recorded production histories against new code in CI before deploying."},{"q":"When would you pick a declarative state machine over a code-first engine?","a":"- **Fixed pipelines** with few data-dependent branches, where JSON-defined states, no determinism\n  discipline and native cloud integrations win.\n- **Not for agent loops,** whose branching depends on model output; a code-first engine expresses that as\n  ordinary control flow."},{"q":"An agent workflow runs thousands of steps and replay is getting slow. What do you change?","a":"- **Continue-as-new on a step budget:** close the history and start a fresh one carrying only needed state.\n- **Shrink payloads:** store transcripts and documents in blob storage and pass references.\n- **Monitor history size and replay latency** so the budget is set from data, not guessed."}],"tradeoffs":["**Crash-proof resumption vs determinism rules.** You get readable straight-line orchestration in exchange for rules that are easy to violate; the trade pays off once a process has more than two steps or waits on humans or timers.","**Full audit trail vs history bloat.** Every step's inputs and outputs are persisted, but large payloads (full documents, long transcripts) bloat history; pass references to blob storage instead.","**Activity retries vs token spend.** Retries lift success rates, yet retrying an LLM activity re-spends tokens and may produce a different answer; make the activity idempotent on its own cache or accept the cost knowingly.","**Versioning vs permanent complexity.** It lets you change logic under running instances, but each patch stays until old histories drain; short-lived workflows can skip it, multi-week ones cannot.","**Declarative state machines vs branching.** They need no determinism discipline and integrate natively with cloud services, but become painful when branching is data-dependent, as in agent loops."],"pitfalls":["**Calling the model or a tool directly from workflow code.** It works in tests, then replays into a second real call, visible as duplicated emails or doubled token bills after a worker restart.","**Non-deterministic workflow code** (`time.time()`, `uuid4()`, dict iteration order, reading config at runtime), which surfaces as workflows failing with non-determinism errors only after a deploy.","**Activities without idempotency keys.** An at-least-once retry after a crash performs the side effect twice; the symptom is duplicates that correlate with deploys and worker restarts.","**Unbounded agent loops in one history.** Discovered as slowly growing replay latency and memory until the workflow stalls; use continue-as-new on a step budget.","**Retry policies left at defaults that retry forever.** An activity failing on bad input (a non-retryable error) keeps retrying for days; workflows never fail and never finish. Mark validation and auth errors non-retryable and set a maximum attempts or expiry.","**Missing heartbeats on long activities.** A dead worker's multi-minute inference is not rescheduled until the full timeout expires, inflating latency on every infrastructure hiccup."],"when":"- **Use when** a process spans more than one side-effecting step, waits on timers or humans, or must\n  survive deploys mid-flight: approvals, multi-step agent tasks, document pipelines, onboarding flows,\n  anything the business would describe as \"a case\".\n- **Avoid for** a single request-response call or pure data transformations, where a queue and an\n  idempotent consumer are enough.\n- **Measure** workflow completion rate, time in each activity, retry counts per activity type and history\n  size; alert on non-determinism errors after deploys.\n- **Day to day:** the discipline \"model and tools in activities, decisions in the workflow\". The\n  agent-reliability module covers checkpoint stores and agent-specific recovery; this lesson is the\n  general runtime they rest on.\n- **Interviews:** explaining replay and why determinism follows from it is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes activities for every model and tool call with timeouts, retries and idempotency keys, keeps workflow code deterministic, and applies continue-as-new on long agent loops.","architect":"Chooses between a code-first engine and a declarative state machine, defines task-queue bulkheads and concurrency limits, and sets the payload and history-size policies.","fde":"Maps a customer's multi-day approval and escalation processes onto workflows with signals, and shows the history as the audit trail their compliance team asked for."},"check":{"q":"A team runs a 30-step research agent as one function inside a job queue with retries. After a\ndeploy, users report duplicated documents and bills roughly double for the hour. Diagnose it and\nexplain how durable execution changes the picture.","a":"The queue restarted the job from step one after the deploy killed the worker mid-run. Every\nmodel call and tool call before the crash point was repeated, so tokens were spent twice and\nnon-idempotent tool calls such as \"create document\" ran again. Retries at the job level are\nat-least-once for the whole job, which is the wrong granularity. Under durable execution each of the\n30 steps is an activity whose result is recorded in the workflow history as it completes. On restart\nthe workflow replays: steps one to the crash point return their recorded results without executing,\nso no tokens are re-spent and no tools re-run, and only the interrupted step is retried. That step\nstill needs an idempotency key, because the crash could have happened after the side effect and\nbefore the result was recorded. The team should also move the loop under a step budget with\ncontinue-as-new and add heartbeats to long model calls."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must durable workflow code be deterministic?","options":["Because the engine executes it on a single thread for performance","Because recovery works by re-executing the code against the recorded history, and any divergence makes replay fail","Because non-deterministic code cannot be serialised to the database","Because activities require deterministic inputs to be retried"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Replay reconstructs state by running the code again and substituting recorded results. If the code takes a different branch the second time, the history no longer matches and the engine cannot continue."},{"q":"Where should an LLM call live in a durable workflow, and why?","options":["In the workflow function, so its result is recorded in history","In a signal handler, so it runs only when triggered","In an activity, because it is non-deterministic and side-effecting; its recorded result is what replay sees","In a query handler, so it does not affect workflow state"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Activities run outside replay, with timeouts and retries, and only their results enter the history. Calling the model in workflow code would re-invoke it on every replay and break determinism."},{"q":"An activity performs a payment and the worker crashes before reporting completion. What does the engine do, and what must the activity therefore guarantee?","options":["The engine detects the completed payment and records it automatically","The engine marks the workflow failed to avoid any risk of duplication","The engine retries the activity, so the activity must be idempotent to avoid charging twice","The engine rolls back the payment using the recorded history"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Durable execution gives at-least-once activity execution; the engine cannot see external side effects. Exactly-once effects come from idempotency keys the receiver deduplicates on, not from the engine."}]},{"key":"reliability/backpressure-and-load-shedding","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (Handling Overload; Addressing Cascading Failures)","Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW","Nichols & Jacobson, 2012 — Controlling Queue Delay","Bronson et al., 2021 — Metastable Failures in Distributed Systems"],"why":"- **Little's law makes overload arithmetic.** `L = λ × W`: requests inside equal arrival rate times time\n  spent. A service completing 50 requests per second and receiving 60 accumulates 10 per second forever.\n- **Queued requests die on arrival.** After one minute, 600 requests wait, each facing a 12-second wait\n  before service starts. With a 10-second client timeout, every one is already dead, yet the server still\n  serves them to clients that hung up and retried; real arrival is now 60 plus retries.\n- **That is congestive collapse:** goodput (useful work) falls toward zero while machines run at 100%.\n- **Trouble starts before capacity is exceeded.** Latency explodes as utilisation approaches 1, so a\n  service that looks fine at 70% falls apart during a 30% surge (see the M/M/1 numbers below).\n- **The naive fixes fail.** \"A bigger queue\" converts depth into latency and buys nothing once wait\n  exceeds what callers tolerate. \"Autoscale\" helps only if capacity arrives faster than the queue grows,\n  rarely true for GPU services where a replica takes minutes to pull weights and warm up. Fixed-quota rate\n  limiting handles abuse but not legitimate surges, and cannot see the server's real condition.\n- **LLM serving makes it concrete.** A GPU server batches a fixed number of sequences; beyond that,\n  requests queue, time-to-first-token inflates, and users retry or resend from another tab. The\n  provider's own 429s are backpressure aimed at you; honouring them with backoff rather than retrying\n  harder is the difference between a slow hour and a dead one.","how":"### Why latency explodes: the M/M/1 shape\nIn the simplest queueing model (M/M/1: random arrivals, one server), mean time in system is\n`W = S / (1 − ρ)`, where `S` is the service time and `ρ` the utilisation.\n\n| Utilisation ρ | Time in system |\n|---|---|\n| 50% | 2× service time |\n| 90% | 10× |\n| 95% | 20× |\n\n- **Real services are not M/M/1,** but the shape, latency exploding as utilisation approaches 1, is general.\n\n### What the system must do\nThe naive design does neither of these:\n1. **Tell the source to slow down** (backpressure).\n2. **Refuse work it cannot finish in time** (load shedding).\n\n### Backpressure: propagate the limit upstream\nBackpressure is the chain of signals that makes the fastest component in a pipeline run at the speed of\nthe slowest.\n\n| Where | Mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| Inside a process | a bounded queue: a producer calling `put()` on a full queue blocks or fails instead of allocating more memory |\n| Across a network | TCP flow control (the receive window), HTTP 429 or 503 with `Retry-After`, gRPC `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED`, a streaming protocol where the consumer pulls chunks at its own pace |\n| Message systems | consumer lag you watch, and a producer that pauses on it |\n\n- **Design rule:** every hop has a bounded buffer and a defined behaviour when it is full, *because* an\n  unbounded buffer anywhere silently absorbs the signal and the overload reappears there as memory\n  exhaustion.\n\n### Load shedding: refuse early, refuse cheap\nShedding drops or rejects some requests to keep the others within their SLO. The mechanics that matter:\n- **Reject at the front door.** The cheapest point to refuse is before any expensive work: before\n  retrieval, before tokenisation, before taking a GPU slot. A rejection that costs a millisecond\n  preserves capacity; one that happens after ten seconds of queueing has already spent it.\n- **Use queue wait time as the overload signal**, not CPU. For a GPU service CPU is meaningless, and\n  for any service the quantity users feel is how long a request waited. Bound the queue by time:\n  if the oldest waiting request has been there longer than the budget allows, start shedding.\n- **Size the queue from the SLO**: `max_queue ≈ service_rate × max_acceptable_wait`. At 50 req/s\n  and a 2 s wait budget, a queue deeper than 100 only manufactures timeouts.\n- **Shed by priority.** Classify work as interactive, background or batch, by tenant tier, or by\n  whether it is a retry. Under pressure drop batch first, then background, and protect health checks\n  and control-plane traffic absolutely. Priority turns shedding from random failure into a policy.\n- **Prefer LIFO or CoDel under overload.** A FIFO queue serves the oldest request, the one most likely\n  abandoned. Serving newest-first, or dropping when the minimum sojourn time exceeds a target for a\n  sustained interval (CoDel), keeps goodput up during the surge.\n- **Adaptive concurrency limits.** Instead of a fixed limit, raise it while latency stays flat and lower\n  it when latency rises (gradient or TCP-Vegas-style controllers). This tracks real capacity as model\n  sizes, prompt lengths and downstream speed change.\n\n### Where retries fit\n- **Retries are a load multiplier.** If a client retries each rejected request up to three times, a 10%\n  excess becomes roughly 40% at first order; because retries are themselves rejected, total attempts can\n  approach four times the original traffic before the budget runs out.\n- **Shedding only works if clients back off** on 429/503 with jitter, honour `Retry-After`, and keep a\n  retry budget.\n- **Client-side circuit breakers** stop the storm once rejection rates are high; **bulkheads** keep one\n  overloaded dependency from consuming every worker.\n\n### Metastable failure: why overload outlives its cause\nBronson et al. describe outages where a temporary trigger pushes the system into overload and a\n**sustaining effect** keeps it there after the trigger is gone.\n- **Triggers:** a surge, a brief dependency slowdown, a cold cache after a deploy.\n- **Sustaining effects:** retries (requests time out, clients retry, retries add load, more time out),\n  cache misses that make every request more expensive, work done for abandoned requests.\n- **Two stable states at the same offered load:** healthy and collapsed. Returning to healthy needs load\n  to drop well below normal, not just back to normal.\n- **Breaking the loop:** early rejection stops wasted work; client backoff and retry budgets remove the\n  amplification.\n\n### Goodput, not throughput\n- **The metric:** successful requests completed within the SLO per second.\n- **Throughput lies.** A server at 100% utilisation doing 50 req/s while 90% of responses arrive after the\n  client gave up has goodput near zero.\n- **Dashboards:** queue wait distribution, rejection rate by priority, and goodput side by side.\n\n### LLM-specific shape\n- **Capacity is concurrent sequences and tokens, not requests,** so admission should estimate cost\n  (prompt length, requested max tokens) and admit by a token budget.\n- **Streaming adds a second backpressure path.** A slow consumer of server-sent events does not slow\n  generation, so the server needs a bounded per-connection buffer and cancellation on disconnect, or it\n  generates tokens nobody will read.\n- **Long generations hold their slot for the whole duration,** so a few multi-minute requests can starve\n  interactive ones unless they are in a separate pool.","example":"### Admission control for a chat endpoint\nShed at the door by priority, bound the queue wait by the SLO, release the slot if the client left while\nwaiting. The limits would be derived from measured service rate and the latency budget, then adjusted by\nan adaptive controller.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request, Response\n\napp = FastAPI()\nMAX_INFLIGHT, MAX_QUEUE, MAX_WAIT_S = 32, 64, 2.0   # capacity, service_rate x wait budget, SLO slice\nslots = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_INFLIGHT)\nwaiting = 0                                          # single event loop: no lock needed\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(req: Request):\n    global waiting\n    priority = req.headers.get(\"x-priority\", \"interactive\")\n    batch_cap = MAX_QUEUE // 4                       # batch work gets a quarter of the queue\n    # 1) shed at the door: a 1 ms rejection instead of a 10 s timeout\n    if waiting >= MAX_QUEUE or (priority == \"batch\" and waiting >= batch_cap):\n        raise HTTPException(503, \"overloaded\", headers={\"Retry-After\": \"5\"})\n    waiting += 1\n    try:\n        # 2) bounded queue wait: beyond the budget the answer would arrive too late to matter\n        await asyncio.wait_for(slots.acquire(), timeout=MAX_WAIT_S)\n    except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n        raise HTTPException(503, \"queue wait exceeded\", headers={\"Retry-After\": \"5\"})\n    finally:\n        waiting -= 1\n    try:\n        if await req.is_disconnected():              # 3) client gave up while queued: do not spend the GPU\n            return Response(status_code=204)\n        return await run_inference(await req.json()) # your model client; token-cost admission goes here\n    finally:\n        slots.release()\n```\n\n- **Runtime caveat:** before Python 3.12, `asyncio.wait_for` could cancel `Semaphore.acquire()` at the\n  same instant it succeeded and leak the slot; on older interpreters wrap the acquire so a timed-out\n  acquisition releases what it won.\n- **Walk the numbers:** inference averaging 640 ms per request with 32 slots gives a service rate of\n  about 50 req/s. A 64-deep queue therefore represents roughly 1.3 s of wait, inside the 2 s budget, and\n  anything beyond is rejected in microseconds with a `Retry-After` the client should honour.\n- **Export** `waiting`, rejections by priority and queue wait time, and an overload becomes a flat goodput\n  line instead of a cliff.\n\n### Worked scenario: queue growth and sizing from Little's law\nPure Python, no dependencies: how fast an unprotected queue grows, when requests start dying, and how\ndeep the queue may be for a given wait budget.\n\n```python\ndef mm1_time_in_system(service_s, utilisation):\n    return service_s / (1 - utilisation)              # W = S / (1 - rho)\n\ndef unprotected_wait_s(arrival, service_rate, seconds_in):\n    backlog = max(0, (arrival - service_rate) * seconds_in)\n    return backlog / service_rate                     # time a new arrival waits before service\n\ndef max_queue(service_rate, wait_budget_s):\n    return int(service_rate * wait_budget_s)          # deeper only manufactures timeouts\n\nfor rho in (0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95):\n    print(f\"rho={rho:.2f}: {mm1_time_in_system(1.0, rho):.1f}x service time\")\nprint(unprotected_wait_s(60, 50, 60))                 # 12.0 s after one minute at 60 vs 50 req/s\nprint(max_queue(50, 2.0))                             # 100\n# rho=0.50: 2.0x   rho=0.70: 3.3x   rho=0.90: 10.0x   rho=0.95: 20.0x\n```\n\n| Second into a 60 vs 50 req/s overload | Backlog | Wait for a new arrival | With 10 s client timeout |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 10 | 100 | 2 s | served |\n| 50 | 500 | 10 s | at the limit |\n| 60 | 600 | 12 s | dead on arrival, and its retry adds load |","patterns":["**Semaphore plus wait timeout in front of every model client.** Size in-flight slots from measured capacity (for vLLM, the engine's own `max_num_seqs` caps batched sequences) and the queue from `service_rate × max_acceptable_wait`. Monitor queue wait p50/p99 and rejections by priority.","**Adaptive concurrency at the proxy or library.** Use a latency-gradient limiter such as Envoy's adaptive concurrency filter or Netflix's concurrency-limits library instead of a hand-tuned constant. Monitor the computed limit over time and alert when it collapses.","**Client SDK retry discipline.** Cap SDK retries (provider Python SDKs expose a `max_retries` setting), honour `Retry-After`, add full jitter, and keep a per-client retry budget. Monitor retries as a share of requests; a rising share during an incident is the metastable warning.","**Separate pools by class.** Run batch and long-generation work on its own queue or deployment with its own slot limit, and keep health checks and control-plane endpoints outside any shed queue. Monitor interactive time-to-first-token while batch load rises.","**Cancel on disconnect for streams.** Check for client disconnect while generating and abort the request in the engine, with a bounded per-connection buffer. Monitor tokens generated for disconnected clients."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Incoming request\"] --> P{\"Queue full for this priority?\"}\n    P -->|\"yes\"| X[\"503 with Retry-After, 1 ms\"]\n    P -->|\"no\"| Q[\"Wait for a slot\"]\n    Q -->|\"wait over budget\"| X\n    Q -->|\"slot acquired\"| D{\"Client still connected?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| F[\"Free slot, skip inference\"]\n    D -->|\"yes\"| G[\"Run inference on GPU\"]\n    G --> OK[\"Response within SLO\"]\n    X -.->|\"jittered backoff, retry budget\"| R","caption":"The admission path for one request: every rejection happens before the GPU is touched, and the slot is\nreleased if the client left while queued."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between backpressure and load shedding?","a":"- **Backpressure** propagates \"slow down\" upstream: bounded queues that block or fail, 429/503 with\n  `Retry-After`, flow control, so the source sends less.\n- **Load shedding** rejects some work early and cheaply so the admitted work meets its latency budget.\n- **You need both,** because backpressure only helps if the source listens, and shedding protects you when\n  it does not."},{"q":"How would you size the request queue for an LLM endpoint?","a":"- **Measure service rate,** for example 32 slots at 640 ms each is about 50 req/s.\n- **Apply `max_queue ≈ service_rate × max_acceptable_wait`:** with a 2 s wait budget, about 100; deeper\n  only manufactures timeouts.\n- **Bound it by time too,** shedding when the oldest waiter exceeds the budget, since prompt lengths change\n  real service time."},{"q":"A surge ended twenty minutes ago but errors are still high. What is happening and what do you do?","a":"- **Metastable failure:** retries, cold caches or work for abandoned requests sustain the overload after the\n  trigger is gone.\n- **Break the loop:** shed hard at the front door, disable or cap client retries, and let load fall well\n  below normal before reopening.\n- **Prevent recurrence** with retry budgets, jittered backoff and goodput alerts."},{"q":"Why not just autoscale instead of shedding?","a":"- **Scaling is too slow for GPU services:** a replica takes minutes to pull weights and warm up, while the\n  queue grows every second.\n- **Shedding buys the time:** it keeps admitted requests within SLO while capacity arrives, and autoscaling\n  can still run in the background."},{"q":"Which metric would you alert on for overload, and why not CPU or throughput?","a":"- **Goodput and queue wait percentiles:** successful requests within SLO per second, and how long requests\n  waited, are what users feel.\n- **CPU is meaningless** for GPU-bound work, and throughput stays high during collapse while nearly every\n  response arrives after the client gave up."}],"tradeoffs":["**Shedding vs queueing.** Shedding sacrifices some requests to keep latency bounded for the rest, which is right whenever a late answer is worthless; it flips to queueing when callers will genuinely wait, as in batch jobs.","**Blocking backpressure vs explicit rejection.** Blocking keeps every request eventually served but stalls producers and can deadlock if a producer also holds resources the consumer needs; explicit rejection is safer across services.","**Fixed vs adaptive limits.** Fixed concurrency limits are predictable and easy to reason about but go stale as prompts, models and dependencies change; adaptive limits track reality at the cost of a controller to tune.","**LIFO vs fairness.** LIFO serving maximises goodput under overload but starves old requests and breaks fairness; use it only during overload and return to FIFO when the queue drains.","**Priority shedding vs honest classification.** It protects the work that matters most, but once every team marks its traffic \"critical\", the mechanism degrades to random drops."],"pitfalls":["**Unbounded queues anywhere in the path** (an async job queue, an executor pool, a channel), visible as latency rising for minutes before a memory crash instead of a quick, clean rejection.","**Shedding after the expensive work.** Rejecting at the model call after retrieval and prompt assembly already ran, so the server stays saturated while returning errors.","**Clients retrying 429 and 503 immediately without backoff.** Every shed request returns within milliseconds and arrival rate doubles; the symptom is rejections climbing faster than traffic.","**Using CPU or request count as the overload signal for GPU-bound work.** The GPU is saturated at 20% CPU and queue wait time was the signal all along.","**Waiting for the trigger to pass in a metastable collapse.** The surge ends but errors stay high because retries keep load above capacity; the outage outlasts its cause and ends only when someone sheds hard or disables retries.","**Health checks sharing the shed queue.** Under load the load balancer marks healthy nodes dead and removes capacity exactly when it is needed, turning overload into an outage."],"when":"- **Every service with finite capacity needs both:** bounded buffers with a defined full behaviour at\n  every hop, and an admission decision at the front door driven by queue wait time and priority.\n- **Start** by measuring service rate and the latency budget, set queue bounds from them, and add adaptive\n  limits once prompt lengths or model mix vary.\n- **Choose** blocking backpressure inside a process or pipeline, explicit 429/503 across service\n  boundaries, and always pair shedding with client backoff and retry budgets.\n- **Measure** goodput, queue wait percentiles and rejection rate by priority; if rejections never happen,\n  the limits are either generous or missing.\n- **Day to day:** the semaphore in front of the model client and the `Retry-After` handling in the SDK.\n- **Interviews:** derive queue size from Little's law and explain why early rejection raises goodput.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Puts a bounded semaphore and a queue-wait timeout in front of every model and tool client, honours provider `Retry-After`, and exports goodput and rejection metrics by priority.","architect":"Defines the priority classes and tenant tiers that shedding enforces, sets latency budgets that queue bounds derive from, and places bulkheads so one workload cannot starve another.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their burst traffic sees 429s rather than slow answers, tunes priorities to their business rules, and sizes capacity against their measured peak arrival rate."},"check":{"q":"A GPU-backed summarisation service handles about 40 requests per second with p50 latency under\na second. A marketing email sends 90 requests per second for ten minutes. Clients time out at 8\nseconds and retry twice. Describe what happens without protection, then design the protection and\njustify each number.","a":"The queue grows by 50 requests per second, so a request arriving *t* seconds into the surge\nfinds about 50*t* ahead of it and waits roughly 50*t* ÷ 40 ≈ 1.25*t* seconds. From about 6.4 seconds\nin, new arrivals cannot be served before their 8-second timeout, and the server spends its capacity on\nabandoned requests while clients add two retries each, pushing effective arrival toward 270 per second. Goodput falls near zero though\nthe GPUs are fully busy, and the collapse can outlast the ten-minute surge because retries keep it\nfed, the metastable pattern. Protection: bound the queue by wait time, around 40 × (8 s minus\nservice time), so no admitted request can miss its deadline; shed above that at the front door with\n503 and `Retry-After`; classify the campaign's traffic as batch so it gets a small share and\ninteractive users stay protected; require the client SDK to back off with jitter and cap retries at a\nbudget; add an adaptive concurrency limit so the bound follows real latency; and alert on goodput and\nrejection rate rather than utilisation. The campaign then completes slower, not never, and nobody\nelse notices."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does adding a larger queue not fix an overloaded service?","options":["Larger queues consume GPU memory that the model needs","Queue depth converts directly into wait time; once wait exceeds what callers tolerate, the server serves requests that have already been abandoned","Queues only work for CPU-bound services, not GPU-bound ones","Larger queues reduce the service rate by increasing lock contention"],"answer":1,"explanation":"By Little's law, waiting requests equal arrival rate times wait time. A deeper queue lets wait time grow past client timeouts, so capacity is spent on work nobody will receive."},{"q":"Where should load shedding decisions be made, and why?","options":["After inference, so the server can return a partial answer instead of an error","At the client, because only it knows its own priority","As early and cheaply as possible, before retrieval and prompt assembly, so a rejection preserves capacity instead of spending it","In the database layer, where contention is easiest to measure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A rejection that costs microseconds protects the service. Rejecting after seconds of queueing or after expensive preprocessing has already consumed the resources shedding was meant to save."},{"q":"Which signal is the best trigger for shedding on a GPU-backed LLM endpoint?","options":["CPU utilisation above 80%","Total requests per second exceeding last week's average","Queue wait time of admitted requests approaching the latency budget","Number of open TCP connections"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Queue wait is what users experience and tracks real capacity regardless of model size or prompt length. CPU is irrelevant for GPU-bound work, and request counts ignore how expensive each request is."}]},{"key":"reliability/tenant-level-quotas-and-noisy-neighbor-isolation","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (chapter on handling overload)"],"why":"- **Shared pools, one bursty tenant.** Every tenant draws on the same scarce pools: provider\n  tokens-per-minute, GPU concurrency, workers, connections. A noisy neighbor is one tenant whose burst (a\n  backfill, a looping agent, a retry storm) fills a pool so everyone else's latency and errors rise.\n- **The opening incident.** A gateway fronts one provider key rated, illustratively, at one million tokens\n  per minute, shared by forty customers. Tenant A re-embeds 50,000 documents as fast as its client can. The\n  global limiter keeps upstream healthy, but its FIFO queue is now 20,000 deep with A's requests. B's\n  support chat (400 tokens, needs a two-second answer) waits behind them, B's p95 triples, and dashboards\n  show the provider perfectly healthy.\n- **The naive fixes fail.** \"Add a global rate limit\" protects the upstream, not the neighbors: it bounds\n  the total but not its division. \"Scale up\" fails when the scarce resource is a vendor quota or a fixed GPU\n  pool, and it rewards the misbehaving tenant with everyone's money. \"Ask tenants to behave\" ignores that\n  agents loop, clients retry, and a tenant's bug becomes your incident.\n- **AI workloads make it sharper than classic SaaS.** Work per request spans three or more orders of\n  magnitude (a 20-token classification versus a 100k-token summary), so counting requests measures almost\n  nothing. Agents fan out, so one user action can become fifty calls.\n- **You can only allocate, not add.** The binding resource often belongs to a vendor, so you cannot\n  engineer your way out by adding capacity; you can only divide what you already have.","how":"### Choose the units you meter\n| Unit | What it tracks | Failure it catches |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tokens per minute (input plus output) | provider limits and cost | large-prompt tenants |\n| Requests per minute | request rate | chatty clients |\n| Concurrent in-flight requests | provider concurrency, KV-cache memory, worker slots | whatever is actually saturating |\n| Spend per day | the bill | runaway cost |\n\n- **Meter tokens and concurrency at minimum,** because a tenant can stay under any request count while\n  consuming the whole token budget with large prompts.\n- **Concurrency matters for latency,** because latency collapse is a function of concurrency, not of\n  request rate.\n\n### Enforce at admission with a token bucket per tenant\n1. **Give each tenant its own bucket.** Capacity is the burst it tolerates; refill rate is the sustained\n   allowance.\n2. **Reserve the worst case.** Output size is unknown until the call finishes, so reserve prompt tokens\n   plus `max_tokens`.\n3. **Make the call, then reconcile** by refunding the unused part. Charging after the fact would let a\n   tenant go arbitrarily far over quota inside a single minute.\n4. **Reject immediately when the bucket cannot cover the reservation,** with 429 and a `Retry-After`\n   header, rather than queueing. A queue that grows past the budget just moves the noisy-neighbor problem\n   into the queue, which is exactly the opening example.\n\n### Isolate the shared path with lanes and fair scheduling\n- **Quotas alone are not isolation.** Forty tenants each under quota can still sum to more than the\n  provider allows, and a FIFO dispatcher still serves them in arrival order.\n- **One queue per tenant, drained round-robin** (or weighted by tier), a simple form of weighted fair\n  queuing. A tenant with 20,000 queued requests gets one turn per cycle, the same as a tenant with one.\n- **Add a per-tenant concurrency semaphore,** so a single tenant cannot hold every in-flight slot.\n- **Fair scheduling is work-conserving:** when only one tenant is active it receives everything, which hard\n  caps alone can never offer.\n\n### Be fair in the unit that is scarce\n- **Round-robin by request is unfair when sizes differ.** If A's queued requests are 20k-token summaries\n  and B's are 400-token chats, one turn each per cycle gives A fifty times B's share of the token budget.\n- **Deficit round robin fixes it.** Each tenant's queue earns a quantum of, say, 8k tokens per cycle into a\n  deficit counter. It dispatches from the head of its queue while the counter covers the (estimated) token\n  cost, carrying the remainder forward.\n- **Result:** B sends up to twenty chats per cycle; A's 20k request waits until its counter has accumulated\n  three cycles of quantum. Each active tenant receives an equal share of tokens, not of requests.\n- **Tiers are just weights:** a tier weight is a larger quantum.\n\n### Divide quotas hierarchically\n- **A tenant is rarely one actor.** An enterprise customer has teams, end users and agents inside its\n  quota, and one of them can starve the rest exactly as one tenant starves the fleet.\n- **The same mechanisms nest:** a bucket per organization, and inside it a bucket or fair-share lane per\n  user or API key. A request is admitted only if every level can cover it.\n- **Keep the levels few** (organization, then user or workload), because each one is another counter on the\n  hot path.\n\n### Tier the isolation\n| Tier | Mechanism | What it isolates |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pooled | shared buckets and lanes | quota share and queue turns |\n| Shuffle-sharded | each tenant gets a small random subset of workers (say 2 of 8) | blast radius of a poisonous tenant |\n| Dedicated | own provider key or model deployment, worker group, vector-index replica | everything, including vendor-side limits |\n\n- **Dedicated is the bulkhead pattern applied per tenant,** and the only level at which a vendor-side limit\n  (a per-key TPM cap) is genuinely isolated, because the vendor's limiter does not know your tenants exist.\n- **Shuffle sharding improves isolation combinatorially.** A tenant that poisons its workers (a\n  crash-inducing input, a pathological prompt) takes out its own shard. Another tenant shares *both* of\n  those workers only if it drew the same pair, which happens for 1 in 28 tenants with 2-of-8.\n- **It complements quotas rather than replacing them,** because it bounds the blast radius of a bad tenant\n  rather than dividing capacity fairly, and it avoids the cost of dedicated capacity.\n\n### Degrade before you deny\n- **A downgrade is often cheaper than a 429:** route to a smaller model, shed the tenant's batch-class\n  traffic while keeping interactive traffic, or lower its `max_tokens`.\n- **This requires classifying traffic at the edge** (interactive versus batch, user-initiated versus\n  agent-initiated) so the scheduler can shed the right thing first.\n\n### Make it observable and billable\n- **Tag every request, span and cost record with `tenant_id`.**\n- **Watch per-tenant signals:** queue wait, in-flight count, tokens consumed and 429 counts tell you whether\n  isolation is working, and they settle the \"you throttled us\" conversation with data.\n- **Quota disputes are a commercial problem,** and the only defense is a measurement both sides trust.","example":"### Admission layer: per-tenant token bucket and concurrency lane\nA single-process sketch: a per-tenant token bucket for tokens per minute with reserve-then-reconcile, plus a\nper-tenant semaphore for concurrency. In production the bucket state lives in Redis behind an atomic Lua\nscript so every gateway replica sees the same level; the logic is identical.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nclass QuotaExceeded(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, tenant, retry_after):\n        super().__init__(f\"tenant {tenant} over token quota\"); self.retry_after = retry_after\n\nclass TenantLimiter:\n    \"\"\"Per-tenant token bucket (tokens/min) + concurrency lane. Single-process version.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, tokens_per_min, max_in_flight):\n        self.rate, self.cap = tokens_per_min / 60.0, float(tokens_per_min)\n        self.level = defaultdict(lambda: self.cap)           # starts full: one burst is allowed\n        self.stamp = defaultdict(time.monotonic)\n        self.lanes = defaultdict(lambda: asyncio.Semaphore(max_in_flight))\n\n    def _refill(self, t):\n        now = time.monotonic()\n        self.level[t] = min(self.cap, self.level[t] + (now - self.stamp[t]) * self.rate)\n        self.stamp[t] = now\n\n    def reserve(self, t, est_tokens):\n        if est_tokens > self.cap:                            # can never fit: a 400, not a retryable 429\n            raise ValueError(f\"request needs {est_tokens} tokens; per-minute cap is {self.cap:.0f}\")\n        self._refill(t)\n        if self.level[t] < est_tokens:                       # reject now; queueing would hide overload\n            raise QuotaExceeded(t, retry_after=(est_tokens - self.level[t]) / self.rate)\n        self.level[t] -= est_tokens\n\n    def reconcile(self, t, est_tokens, used_tokens):\n        self.level[t] += est_tokens - used_tokens            # refund the unused reservation\n\nasync def call_llm(limiter, tenant, prompt_tokens, max_output_tokens, call):\n    est = prompt_tokens + max_output_tokens                  # worst case: output size unknown until done\n    limiter.reserve(tenant, est)\n    async with limiter.lanes[tenant]:                        # the tenant's own concurrency lane\n        try:\n            resp = await call()\n        except Exception:\n            limiter.reconcile(tenant, est, prompt_tokens)    # failed calls still charge the prompt\n            raise\n    limiter.reconcile(tenant, est, resp[\"usage\"][\"total_tokens\"])\n    return resp\n```\n\n- **Ordering matters:** the reservation happens before the lane is taken and before any provider call, so\n  a tenant over quota never occupies a slot.\n- **Failed calls still charge the prompt tokens,** so a retry storm counts against the tenant that caused it\n  rather than against the shared pool.\n- **The 429 carries an honest wait:** `retry_after` is computed from the refill rate, so well-behaved\n  clients get a real wait instead of a guess.\n- **Oversized requests are a client error.** A request larger than the whole bucket is rejected as a 400,\n  because a 429 for it would promise a retry that can never succeed and invite the client to loop.\n\n### Round robin vs deficit round robin, runnable\nA's backlog is 20k-token summaries, B's is 400-token chats. Count tokens served over 30 cycles.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef round_robin(queues, rounds):\n    served = {t: 0 for t in queues}\n    for _ in range(rounds):\n        for t, q in queues.items():\n            if q:\n                served[t] += q.popleft()          # one request per tenant per cycle\n    return served\n\ndef drr(queues, quantum, rounds):\n    deficit = {t: 0 for t in queues}\n    served = {t: 0 for t in queues}\n    for _ in range(rounds):\n        for t, q in queues.items():\n            if not q:\n                deficit[t] = 0                    # idle queues do not bank credit\n                continue\n            deficit[t] += quantum                 # tokens earned this cycle\n            while q and q[0] <= deficit[t]:\n                cost = q.popleft()\n                deficit[t] -= cost\n                served[t] += cost\n    return served\n\ndef backlog():\n    return {\"A\": deque([20_000] * 50), \"B\": deque([400] * 1000)}\n\nprint(\"round robin:\", round_robin(backlog(), rounds=30))   # A: 600000, B: 12000\nprint(\"DRR:        \", drr(backlog(), quantum=8_000, rounds=30))  # A: 240000, B: 240000\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: hard caps vs fair share for the backfill\nIllustrative numbers: the one-million-TPM key, forty tenants, and an assumed ~500 tokens per document for\nA's 50,000-document re-embed (25M tokens).\n\n| Policy | A's allowance at night (others idle) | Backfill time | B's chat during the day |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Global limit only (FIFO) | whole key | ~25 min | waits behind 20,000 queued requests |\n| Hard cap 1/40 each | 25,000 TPM | ~1,000 min (~17 h) | protected |\n| Fair share with soft caps | nearly the whole key | ~25 min | protected: one fair turn per cycle |\n\nFair share wins on both columns because it is work-conserving; hard caps only win when capacity has been\nsold to specific customers.","patterns":["**Shared bucket in Redis.** When more than one gateway replica exists, keep bucket state in Redis and update it with one Lua script (`EVAL`) that refills, checks and decrements atomically, keyed by tenant. Monitor per-tenant 429 rate, Redis latency on the hot path, and provider 429s after scale-out events.","**Concurrency leases, not bare counters.** For a distributed per-tenant semaphore, store each in-flight slot with an expiry so a crashed worker's slot frees itself. Monitor in-flight per tenant against its limit and slots reclaimed by expiry, which flag crashing workers.","**Separate batch and interactive lanes.** Classify at the edge; send backfills to a bounded batch queue or a provider's asynchronous batch endpoint, and give interactive traffic its own lane. Monitor batch lag and interactive p95 queue wait per tenant.","**Dedicated keys or deployments for premium tiers.** When a contract promises capacity, give that tenant its own provider key or model deployment so the vendor limit is isolated too. Monitor provider-side 429s per key and idle capacity cost.","**Per-tenant dashboards from tags.** Put `tenant_id` on every trace span and cost record; chart token share by tenant, queue wait, and 429s split by tenant and by upstream. Isolation works when one tenant's spike moves only that tenant's lines."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Request with tenant_id\"] --> C[\"Classify: interactive or batch\"]\n    C --> B{\"Tenant bucket covers worst case?\"}\n    B -->|\"no\"| E[\"429 with Retry-After\"]\n    B -->|\"yes, reserve\"| Q[\"Per-tenant queue\"]\n    Q --> D[\"Deficit round robin dispatcher\"]\n    D --> L[\"Tenant concurrency lane\"]\n    L --> P[\"Provider or GPU pool\"]\n    P --> RC[\"Reconcile: refund unused tokens\"]\n    RC -.->|\"refill\"| B","caption":"Each request passes the tenant's bucket before it can queue, then the dispatcher drains per-tenant queues\nfairly and the tenant's lane bounds its in-flight calls; the reconcile step refunds unused tokens."},"interview":[{"q":"How do you enforce a tokens-per-minute quota when you do not know the output length in advance?","a":"- **Reserve the worst case:** prompt tokens plus `max_tokens` are taken from the tenant's bucket before\n  the call, so the tenant cannot overshoot inside a minute.\n- **Reconcile afterwards:** refund the difference between the reservation and actual usage; on failure,\n  still charge the prompt so retry storms land on the tenant that caused them.\n- **Reject oversized requests as a 400,** because a 429 would promise a retry that can never succeed."},{"q":"After autoscaling the gateway from 3 to 12 replicas, provider 429s appear. What happened?","a":"- **Limiter state is probably per replica,** so each replica allows the full cap and effective quota is\n  cap times replica count, here four times what it was.\n- **Fix:** move bucket state to a shared store (Redis with an atomic script) so every replica decrements\n  the same level, and alert on provider 429s right after scale-out events."},{"q":"When would you choose hard per-tenant caps over fair-share scheduling?","a":"- **Hard caps** fit when capacity has been sold to specific customers and must be guaranteed, accepting\n  that idle shares are stranded (1/N each even when others are idle).\n- **Fair share with soft caps** fits many tenants with uncorrelated bursts, because it is work-conserving:\n  a lone active tenant gets the whole pool.\n- **Many systems combine them:** guaranteed floors for paying tiers, fair share for the remainder."},{"q":"Explain shuffle sharding and what it does and does not protect against.","a":"- **Mechanism:** each tenant is routed to a small random subset of workers, say 2 of 8, so a poisonous\n  tenant only takes out its own pair.\n- **Numbers:** 8 choose 2 is 28 pairs, so another tenant shares both of those workers only 1 time in 28.\n- **Limit:** it bounds blast radius from crashes or pathological inputs, but does not divide capacity\n  fairly, so quotas are still needed."},{"q":"Why is round-robin across per-tenant queues still unfair for LLM traffic, and what replaces it?","a":"- **Request sizes differ by orders of magnitude,** so one turn each gives a 20k-token tenant fifty times\n  the token share of a 400-token tenant.\n- **Deficit round robin** gives each queue a token quantum per cycle and dispatches while the deficit\n  covers the head request, so shares equalize in tokens; tier weights become larger quanta."}],"tradeoffs":["**Hard caps vs fair share.** Hard per-tenant caps guarantee isolation but strand capacity: if the caps sum to the provider limit, each tenant gets 1/N even when the others are idle. This flips toward soft caps with fair-share scheduling when tenants are many and bursts are uncorrelated, and back toward hard caps when you have sold specific capacity to specific customers.","**Dedicated pools vs cost.** Dedicated pools and provider keys per tenant give the strongest isolation, including against vendor limits, but carry per-tenant minimum cost and operational surface. Worth it when a tenant's revenue or contractual commitments exceed the cost of the idle capacity.","**Token metering vs simplicity.** Metering tokens is accurate but requires reserve-then-reconcile and a tokenizer at admission; metering requests is nearly free but gameable with large prompts. Request counting is adequate only when a schema bounds prompt size.","**Reject vs queue.** Rejecting at the cap pushes work back to the client's retry logic and keeps latency honest; queueing smooths bursts but hides overload and raises everyone's latency. Queue only batch traffic, and only with a bounded depth."],"pitfalls":["**Relying on a single global rate limit.** Symptom: the provider never returns 429, yet small tenants' p99 spikes precisely while one tenant runs a batch job. Fix: per-tenant buckets plus fair draining.","**Counting requests instead of tokens.** Symptom: one tenant with 100k-token prompts stays inside its request quota while the cost report attributes most of the monthly spend to a few percent of calls.","**Keeping limiter state per replica.** Symptom: effective quota equals cap times replica count and silently grows on scale-out; provider 429s appear right after autoscaling events. Fix: shared state.","**Attributing retries to the pool rather than the tenant.** Symptom: a tenant's retry storm multiplies upstream load and every other tenant pays for it. Fix: charge failed calls to the tenant.","**Fair-queuing by request count when request sizes vary.** Symptom: per-tenant turn counts look equal while the token share by tenant shows the large-prompt tenant taking most of the budget. Fix: DRR.","**Identifying tenants by API key alone when a customer has many end users.** Symptom: the customer complains that \"our app is slow for some users\", because one of their users is starving the rest inside a single quota. Fix: a nested per-user level.","**Not reserving output tokens.** Symptom: your bucket says the tenant is under quota while the provider rejects for exceeding tokens-per-minute, because long completions were never counted until they finished."],"when":"- **Use when** more than one tenant shares a provider key, GPU pool or worker fleet, or when a single\n  tenant mixes workload classes (batch backfills next to interactive chat).\n- **Start small:** a Redis token bucket keyed by tenant for tokens per minute plus a per-tenant concurrency\n  semaphore covers most early-stage services. Add per-tenant queues with fair draining when batch jobs\n  appear, and dedicated pools when a contract promises capacity.\n- **Measure** per-tenant queue wait, share of tokens by tenant, and 429s split by tenant and by upstream;\n  isolation is working when one tenant's spike moves only that tenant's lines.\n- **Interviews:** the prompt is usually \"a customer's batch job slowed everyone down; what do you change?\",\n  and the expected answer distinguishes bounding the total from dividing it fairly.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the shared token bucket and per-tenant semaphore in the gateway, reserves worst-case tokens before each call, and tags every span and cost record with the tenant.","architect":"Owns the tiering model: which units are quota'd, how quotas map to pricing plans and to vendor limits, and which tenants justify dedicated keys, deployments or worker pools.","fde":"Inside a customer's deployment the tenants are departments or business units; the FDE negotiates their quotas, explains the 429s, and builds the per-team usage dashboard that ends disputes."},"check":{"q":"A shared gateway fronts one provider key with an illustrative limit of one million tokens per\nminute for forty tenants. Tenant A starts an embedding backfill and tenant B's chat latency triples, but\nthe global rate limiter never fires. Explain why, and design the fix.","a":"The global limiter bounds total upstream load, not its division; the FIFO queue ahead of it is\ndominated by A, so B waits behind thousands of A's requests while the provider stays healthy. The fix\nhas three layers. First, per-tenant admission: a token bucket per tenant (say an illustrative 50k tokens\nper minute default) with worst-case reservation and a concurrency semaphore, so A is rejected with 429\nand `Retry-After` once over its share. Second, isolation on the shared path: one queue per tenant drained\nround-robin, so even under quota A gets one turn per cycle. Third, classify A's traffic as batch so it\nis shed first and only consumes leftover capacity, and move premium tenants to a dedicated key. Verify\nwith per-tenant queue-wait metrics: after the change, A's wait rises and B's is flat."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a single global rate limit fail to prevent noisy-neighbor problems?","options":["Global limits only count requests, never tokens","It bounds total upstream load but says nothing about how that load is divided among tenants","Rate limiters cannot run in a distributed gateway","Providers ignore client-side limits and apply their own"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A global limit protects the dependency, so the provider looks healthy, but the queue in front of it is still served in arrival order; one tenant's burst fills it and everyone else waits behind. Isolation needs per-tenant admission and fair scheduling, not a tighter total."},{"q":"Why should an LLM gateway meter tokens rather than only requests per minute?","options":["Token counts are easier to compute than request counts","Providers do not enforce request-rate limits","Cost and provider limits scale with tokens, so one large-prompt tenant can exhaust the budget while under its request quota","Request counting cannot be stored in Redis"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Work per request varies by orders of magnitude; a tenant sending 100k-token prompts at a modest request rate consumes the tokens-per-minute budget that other tenants need. Requests are a useful secondary signal, not the primary unit."},{"q":"Which mechanism keeps isolation work-conserving, so idle capacity is not stranded?","options":["Hard per-tenant caps that sum to exactly the provider limit","Per-replica limiters with no shared state","Fair-share scheduling across per-tenant queues with soft caps","A dedicated provider key for every tenant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Round-robin or weighted draining of per-tenant queues gives each active tenant an equal turn while a lone active tenant receives the whole pool. Hard 1/N caps leave capacity unused when others are idle, and per-replica limiters break isolation on scale-out."}]},{"key":"reliability/circuit-breakers","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It!","Fowler, 2014 — CircuitBreaker (bliki)","Official docs: Resilience4j"],"why":"- **Failing dependencies drain callers.** Every caller that keeps trying a failing or timing-out\n  dependency ties up threads and connections waiting for answers that will not come, and the retries make\n  recovery harder.\n- **Cascading failure has a precise mechanism.** An agent service calls a provider with a 30-second timeout\n  at 50 requests per second; the provider degrades to 100 percent timeouts. By Little's law the service\n  accumulates 50 × 30 = 1,500 stuck calls, far more than any sensible pool. Within a minute the pool is\n  exhausted, health checks fail, the orchestrator restarts pods, and a partial outage upstream becomes a\n  total outage here.\n- **Timeouts and retries are not enough.** Timeouts bound a single call but not the aggregate: 1,500 stuck\n  calls at 30 seconds each is the result *with* timeouts. Retries with backoff make the aggregate worse,\n  because they multiply attempts exactly when attempts are worthless. Waiting for the provider to fix\n  itself is not a strategy when your own service dies first.\n- **The breaker does two things at once.** It stops consuming the caller's resources immediately, because\n  rejected calls return in microseconds rather than seconds, and it removes load from the dependency,\n  which is struggling precisely because it is overloaded, so whatever is wrong there can clear.\n- **The name is literal.** Electrical breakers exist for the same reason: when a fault draws too much\n  current, the right response is to open the circuit, not to keep supplying power and hope.","how":"### Three states\nA breaker is a small state machine wrapped around a call.\n\n1. **Closed.** Calls flow through. Every outcome is recorded in a sliding window, either the last N calls\n   or the last T seconds. If the failure ratio in the window crosses a threshold *and* the window holds at\n   least a minimum number of calls, the breaker trips. The minimum exists because one failure out of one\n   call is a 100 percent failure rate and should not trip anything.\n2. **Open.** Every call is rejected immediately with a distinct error (`CircuitOpen`), no network\n   involved, for a reset interval. This is where resources are freed and the dependency gets relief.\n3. **Half-open.** When the interval elapses, a limited number of trial calls are allowed. If they succeed\n   the breaker closes and the window is cleared; if any fails it reopens and the timer restarts. The limit\n   matters because if every waiting caller rushed in on the first tick of recovery, a partly healed\n   dependency would be knocked down again.\n\n### What counts as a failure\n| Outcome | Count it? | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| Timeouts, connection errors, 5xx | yes | they describe the dependency's health |\n| 429 | judgment call | many teams count it (calling a rate-limited endpoint helps nobody); others route it to a separate rate-limit backoff so one noisy caller's quota exhaustion does not open the breaker for everyone sharing it |\n| 4xx client errors (malformed request, invalid schema) | no | they are your bug; counting them opens the breaker on a healthy provider |\n| Slow success | yes, for LLM traffic | a provider answering correctly in 25 seconds is, for an interactive product, down |\n\n- **Either way, a 429's `Retry-After` should inform the wait.**\n- **Slow calls need explicit handling.** Libraries such as Resilience4j expose a slow-call-rate threshold\n  for exactly this; if yours does not, a tight timeout turns slowness into a countable failure.\n\n### Consecutive count or failure ratio\n- **Consecutive count** (the one in Nygard's and Fowler's original descriptions) trips after N consecutive\n  failures. It needs no window and reacts fast to a hard outage, but a single success resets it, so a\n  dependency failing 70 percent of the time may never accumulate N in a row.\n- **Ratio over a sliding window** handles partial failure, which is the common case for overloaded\n  providers, at the cost of the minimum-calls guard and more state.\n- **Rule:** consecutive counts for low-traffic or all-or-nothing dependencies, ratios for anything with\n  steady traffic.\n\n### Choosing thresholds\n| Setting | Starting point | Reasoning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Window | 50 to 100 calls | large enough that a single burst of errors does not trip it |\n| Minimum calls | 20 | statistically meaningful at your traffic level |\n| Trip ratio | 50 percent failures | ordinary error rates (a few percent) never approach it |\n| Reset interval | 30 seconds | recovery is noticed within a minute |\n| Half-open probes | 3 | enough evidence without flooding |\n\n- **Low-traffic dependencies need longer time-based windows:** at two calls a minute a 20-call minimum would\n  take ten minutes to trip.\n\n### Time to trip\n- **A failure is recorded only when the call ends,** so a breaker cannot trip on timeouts sooner than one\n  timeout after the outage begins.\n- **Worked numbers:** with the defaults above, 50 requests per second and a 20-second timeout, the first 20\n  failures are recorded about 20 seconds in. By then roughly 50 × 20 = 1,000 calls have been started, and\n  all of them are still holding a worker.\n- **Fast errors trip fast:** connection refused or an immediate 503 trips the same breaker in well under a\n  second.\n- **Consequences:** the timeout belongs inside the breaker and should be set from the product's latency\n  need, not from the provider's worst case; and a bulkhead is still needed to cap occupancy during that\n  first window.\n\n### Granularity\n- **One breaker per failure domain, not one per service name.** A provider's `model-a` deployment in one\n  region can be failing while `model-b` is fine, so key the breaker by provider × model × region, and tool\n  APIs by endpoint.\n- **A single \"the provider\" breaker fails both ways:** it either trips on partial failures and takes healthy\n  paths with it, or never trips because healthy traffic dilutes the ratio.\n\n### What happens when it is open\n- **Without a fallback, a breaker converts slow failures into fast failures.** That is already worth having,\n  since it keeps the service and its health checks alive.\n- **The user experience is rescued by what you do with `CircuitOpen`:** route to a secondary provider or\n  smaller model, return a cached or degraded answer, or enqueue the work for later and say so.\n- **The breaker is the detector; the fallback is the recovery.**\n\n### Composition with retries, timeouts and bulkheads\n- **Retry outermost, then the breaker, then the timeout around the call.** This is a common arrangement and\n  the one Resilience4j documents as its default. Each retry attempt is counted by the breaker, and once it\n  opens the remaining attempts short-circuit instantly, provided the retry policy treats `CircuitOpen` as\n  non-retryable.\n- **Retry inside the breaker is wrong:** each counted \"call\" takes several timeouts to register, delaying\n  the trip while multiplying load.\n- **A bulkhead sits alongside:** a concurrency cap per dependency bounds how many callers can be stuck while\n  the breaker is still accumulating its minimum calls.","example":"### A compact asyncio breaker with fallback\nA count-based window, a minimum-calls guard, a capped number of half-open probes, and a fallback at the\ncall site. It is single-process; a fleet shares breaker state through a library or a small Redis record,\nbut the state machine is the same.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, collections, time\n\nclass CircuitOpen(Exception): pass\nclass UpstreamError(Exception): pass          # 5xx / connection-level failures from the provider\n\nclass CircuitBreaker:\n    def __init__(self, window=50, min_calls=20, fail_ratio=0.5, reset_after=30.0, probes=3):\n        self.window, self.min_calls, self.fail_ratio = window, min_calls, fail_ratio\n        self.reset_after, self.probes = reset_after, probes\n        self.results = collections.deque(maxlen=window)    # True = failed call\n        self.state, self.opened_at, self.probe_ok, self.probe_inflight = \"closed\", 0.0, 0, 0\n\n    def _allow(self):\n        if self.state == \"open\":\n            if time.monotonic() - self.opened_at < self.reset_after:\n                return False                                # fail fast: no network call at all\n            self.state, self.probe_ok, self.probe_inflight = \"half_open\", 0, 0\n        if self.state == \"half_open\":\n            if self.probe_inflight >= self.probes:\n                return False                                # only a few trial calls at a time\n            self.probe_inflight += 1\n        return True\n\n    def _trip(self):\n        self.state, self.opened_at = \"open\", time.monotonic()\n\n    def _record(self, failed):                              # failed=None: ignored outcome (e.g. a 4xx)\n        if self.state == \"half_open\":\n            self.probe_inflight -= 1                        # always release the probe slot\n            if failed is None: return\n            if failed: self._trip(); return                # still broken: reopen, timer restarts\n            self.probe_ok += 1\n            if self.probe_ok >= self.probes:                # enough evidence of recovery\n                self.state, self.results = \"closed\", collections.deque(maxlen=self.window)\n            return\n        if failed is None: return\n        self.results.append(failed)\n        n = len(self.results)\n        if n >= self.min_calls and sum(self.results) / n >= self.fail_ratio:\n            self._trip()\n\n    async def call(self, fn, *args, timeout=10.0, **kw):\n        if not self._allow():\n            raise CircuitOpen(\"dependency marked unhealthy\")\n        try:\n            result = await asyncio.wait_for(fn(*args, **kw), timeout)\n        except (asyncio.TimeoutError, ConnectionError, UpstreamError):\n            self._record(True); raise\n        except BaseException:                               # 4xx, cancellation: neither success nor failure\n            self._record(None); raise\n        self._record(False)\n        return result\n\nbreaker = CircuitBreaker()\n\nasync def answer(prompt):\n    try:\n        return await breaker.call(primary_llm, prompt, timeout=20)\n    except CircuitOpen:\n        return await secondary_llm(prompt)                   # other vendor, smaller model, or cached reply\n```\n\n- **The timeout lives inside `call`,** so slow responses become failures the window can see.\n- **The catch-all branch matters more than it looks.** Without it, a 4xx or a cancelled request during\n  half-open would never release its probe slot, and after three such calls the breaker would reject\n  everything forever while the dependency was healthy.\n- **Emit a metric on every state transition;** a breaker that opens silently is an outage nobody is\n  looking at.\n\n### Worked scenario: one minute of a provider outage\nIllustrative timeline for the service above (50 requests per second, 20-second timeout, minimum 20 calls,\n50 percent ratio) when the provider starts timing out every call at t = 0.\n\n| Time | Without breaker | With breaker (timeout inside) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 to 20 s | ~1,000 calls started, all holding workers | same: no failure has been recorded yet |\n| ~20 s | first timeouts land, retries add more attempts | first 20 failures recorded, breaker opens |\n| 20 to 50 s | in-flight calls keep growing toward pool size, health checks start failing | new calls rejected in microseconds and sent to the fallback |\n| ~50 s | pods restarted, outage spreads to routes that never used the provider | half-open: only 3 probes go out; they time out ~20 s later and the breaker reopens |\n\nThe breaker does not help with the ~1,000 calls started before it trips; a bulkhead caps those at its size.\n\n### Production config: the same policy in Resilience4j\nFor JVM services the library expresses these settings directly (Spring Boot property names).\n\n```yaml\nresilience4j:\n  circuitbreaker:\n    instances:\n      llmPrimary:\n        slidingWindowType: COUNT_BASED\n        slidingWindowSize: 100\n        minimumNumberOfCalls: 20\n        failureRateThreshold: 50\n        slowCallDurationThreshold: 5s       # slow success counts as a failure\n        slowCallRateThreshold: 50\n        waitDurationInOpenState: 30s\n        permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState: 3\n        ignoreExceptions:\n          - com.example.llm.BadRequestException   # your 4xx: not the provider's fault\n```","patterns":["**Key breakers by failure domain.** Keep a registry keyed by provider, model and region (and tool endpoint), creating breakers lazily. Monitor each breaker's call volume: a partition that never reaches its minimum calls is too fine to trip.","**Library breakers with shared state.** In Python, `pybreaker` offers a consecutive-failure breaker (`fail_max`, `reset_timeout`, `exclude` for ignored exceptions) and a Redis-backed state store so replicas agree; in the JVM, Resilience4j as above. Monitor rejected calls and time spent open per breaker.","**Make `CircuitOpen` non-retryable.** In Resilience4j add `CallNotPermittedException` to the retry policy's ignored exceptions; in your own code, exclude `CircuitOpen` from the retry predicate. Monitor that latency drops when a breaker opens; if it does not, the retry layer is still waiting.","**Mesh-level outlier detection for per-host faults.** Istio's DestinationRule `outlierDetection` (`consecutive5xxErrors`, `interval`, `baseEjectionTime`) ejects individual bad hosts from the pool, which complements an application breaker that decides when the whole dependency is down. Monitor ejected-host counts.","**Jitter the reset interval and alert on state.** Use `reset_after * random.uniform(0.8, 1.2)` so replicas do not probe on the same tick; export breaker state as a gauge per dependency and alert when a breaker has been open longer than its reset interval several times running."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Closed\n    Closed --> Closed: record outcome in window\n    Closed --> Open: min calls reached and failure ratio over threshold\n    Open --> Open: reject fast with CircuitOpen\n    Open --> HalfOpen: reset interval elapsed\n    HalfOpen --> Closed: all probes succeed, clear window\n    HalfOpen --> Open: any probe fails, restart timer","caption":"The three states and what moves the breaker between them; note that half-open lets only a few probes\nthrough before deciding."},"interview":[{"q":"Why don't timeouts alone prevent a cascading failure when a provider goes down?","a":"- **A timeout bounds one call, not the aggregate.** By Little's law, in-flight calls equal arrival rate\n  times duration: 50 requests per second with a 30-second timeout is 1,500 stuck calls.\n- **That exhausts the worker pool,** so routes that never touch the provider fail too and health checks\n  restart pods.\n- **A breaker fixes the aggregate** by rejecting in microseconds once the failure ratio is clear, and a\n  bulkhead caps occupancy before that."},{"q":"Consecutive-failure breaker or failure-ratio breaker: which would you pick and why?","a":"- **Consecutive count** is simple and reacts fast to hard, all-or-nothing outages, and suits low-traffic\n  dependencies.\n- **But a single success resets it,** so a dependency failing 70 percent of the time may never trip it.\n- **A ratio over a sliding window** catches the partial failures typical of overloaded providers, at the\n  cost of a minimum-calls guard and more state."},{"q":"How would you design breakers for an LLM gateway that uses two providers, three models and two regions?","a":"- **One breaker per failure domain:** provider × model × region, so a failing deployment does not take\n  healthy ones down and healthy traffic does not dilute the sick one's ratio.\n- **Check traffic per partition:** each breaker must reach its minimum calls; merge partitions or use\n  time-based windows where it cannot.\n- **Fallback map:** for each key, name the secondary (other region, other vendor, smaller model, cache)."},{"q":"In what order do you compose retry, circuit breaker and timeout, and what breaks if you get it wrong?","a":"- **Retry outermost, then breaker, then timeout around the call,** with `CircuitOpen` non-retryable, so\n  every attempt is counted and the remaining attempts short-circuit once it opens.\n- **Retry inside the breaker** means each counted call takes several timeouts to register, delaying the\n  trip while multiplying load on the failing dependency."},{"q":"When would you not add a circuit breaker?","a":"- **In-process calls,** which do not fail slowly over a network.\n- **When there is no fallback and the request must wait,** because rejecting work you will have to redo is\n  worse than a tight timeout plus a bounded queue.\n- **Very low-traffic dependencies** with ratio breakers, where the minimum calls is unreachable; use a\n  consecutive count or a time-based window instead."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast protection vs false trips.** Aggressive thresholds protect the caller quickly but trip on transient blips and reject requests that would have succeeded; the balance shifts toward longer windows and higher minimums as traffic per breaker falls.","**Fine vs coarse granularity.** Fine-grained breakers (per model, per region, per endpoint) isolate precisely, but each partition may never reach its minimum calls; coarse breakers trip reliably yet take healthy sub-dependencies down with the sick one. Partition only as finely as your traffic supports.","**Short vs long reset interval.** A short one notices recovery fast but re-hammers a dependency that is still down; a long one keeps you on the fallback longer than needed. The better your fallback, the longer you can afford to wait.","**Local vs shared state.** Local per-process breakers need no coordination, but every replica discovers the outage independently and small replicas may never accumulate enough calls; shared state fixes that at the cost of a network hop and a new dependency on the store."],"pitfalls":["**Counting 4xx client errors as failures.** Symptom: the breaker opens after you ship a bad prompt template or schema change while the provider's status page is green, and the fallback quietly serves degraded answers. Fix: ignore client errors.","**Omitting the minimum-calls guard.** Symptom: open/closed flapping in the state metric overnight, when traffic is low and a single failure is a 100 percent rate.","**Retrying across an open breaker.** Symptom: latency does not drop when the breaker opens because the retry policy keeps re-raising `CircuitOpen` with backoff, and logs fill with rejections.","**Ignoring slow success.** Symptom: worker and connection pools exhaust during a latency incident while the breaker stays closed because nothing returned an error. Fix: tight timeout or slow-call threshold.","**Opening without alerting or fallback.** Symptom: a sudden wall of fast 503s that nobody connects to an upstream problem, because the service's own error rate looks like the cause.","**Leaking half-open probe slots on outcomes the breaker ignores.** Symptom: a breaker stuck half-open, rejecting nearly all traffic, while direct calls to the dependency succeed.","**Letting every replica probe at the same instant.** Symptom: a dependency that recovers for ten seconds and collapses again on each reset tick; add jitter to the reset interval."],"when":"- **Use when** a synchronous call goes to a dependency that can fail slowly and whose failure you can do\n  something about: provider and model endpoints, tool and partner APIs, vector stores, anything over a\n  network.\n- **Avoid when** the call is in-process; think twice when there is no fallback and the request genuinely\n  must wait. There, a tight timeout plus a bounded queue is more honest than a breaker that rejects work you\n  will have to redo.\n- **Measure** state transitions, rejected-call counts, time spent open, and fallback rate, and alert when a\n  breaker has been open longer than its reset interval several times running.\n- **Interviews:** the expected depth is the three states, why half-open exists, what should and should not\n  count as a failure, and how the breaker composes with timeouts, retries and bulkheads.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wraps every provider, model and tool client in breaker plus timeout plus fallback, keys breakers by failure domain, and emits state-transition metrics.","architect":"Defines the failure domains that get their own breaker, the fallback policy for each dependency, and demands the cascading-failure walk-through in design reviews.","fde":"In a customer's environment the flaky dependencies are their own systems (CRM, ticketing, identity); the breaker keeps the agent responsive and the FDE explains why it said \"tool unavailable\" rather than hanging."},"check":{"q":"Your agent calls a customer's ticketing API. At their peak hour the API's p99 climbs to 40 seconds\nwithout returning errors, your breaker (set to open at 50 percent failures) never opens, and your\nservice's worker pool exhausts. What is wrong, and how do you fix it?","a":"The breaker only sees errors, and the failure mode here is slow success, so from its point of view\nnothing is failing. Fix it in layers. Put a timeout appropriate to the product, perhaps 5 seconds, inside\nthe breaker so slow calls become countable failures, or enable a slow-call-rate threshold if the library\nhas one. Add a bulkhead that caps concurrent calls to that API, so even before the breaker trips the\nstuck calls cannot take more than, say, 20 of 200 workers. Check the minimum-calls setting against this\nAPI's traffic so the trip is reachable. Then give `CircuitOpen` a fallback: queue the ticket write for\nasynchronous retry and tell the user it has been queued, rather than failing the whole turn. Finally,\nverify with metrics: the breaker should now open within a minute of the latency spike and worker-pool\nutilization should stay flat."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the purpose of the half-open state?","options":["To let all queued requests through at once when the reset interval ends","To test recovery with a limited number of trial calls without flooding a dependency that may still be unhealthy","To count client errors separately from server errors","To hold requests in memory until the dependency recovers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If every waiting caller rushed in at the first sign of recovery, a partly healed dependency would be knocked down again. A few probes give evidence of recovery cheaply; one failure reopens the breaker."},{"q":"Which outcome should NOT count toward tripping a breaker on an LLM provider call?","options":["A request that exceeded its timeout","A 503 Service Unavailable response","A 400 Bad Request caused by a malformed payload from your own code","A connection refused error"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The breaker measures the dependency's health. A 400 from your own bad request says nothing about the provider, and counting it would open the circuit, and switch to a fallback, while the provider is fine."},{"q":"A team places a 3-attempt retry policy inside the breaker (breaker outermost, retries inside). During an outage, what is the effect?","options":["Nothing changes; the order of resilience wrappers is irrelevant","The breaker trips faster because each attempt is counted","Each counted call takes roughly three timeouts to register as a failure, delaying the trip while retries triple load on the failing dependency","The retries are skipped automatically once the first attempt fails"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With retries inside, the breaker records one outcome per request, and only after all three attempts have timed out, so it reaches its minimum-calls threshold a third as fast as when every attempt is counted, and until it trips every request keeps sending three attempts to the dependency. With retry outside and `CircuitOpen` non-retryable, each attempt is counted, the trip comes sooner, and the remaining attempts short-circuit instead of reaching the network."}]},{"key":"reliability/bulkheads","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It!","Official docs: Resilience4j"],"why":"- **One slow dependency becomes a full outage.** A service with one pool of 200 workers calls an LLM, a\n  vector database and a customer's CRM API. The CRM hangs until the 30-second timeout. At only 5 CRM calls\n  per second, Little's law (concurrency = arrival rate × time in system) gives 5 × 30 = 150 stuck workers,\n  three quarters of the pool, reached within half a minute.\n- **Healthy paths die with it.** Add LLM traffic and the pool is full; requests that need only the\n  healthy LLM and vector store are rejected, health checks share the pool and fail, and pods restart.\n  Workload mixing does the same with no failure at all: a batch summarization job that is merely slow and\n  plentiful fills the pool, interactive users wait behind it, and nothing is broken.\n- **The usual fixes do not cap the aggregate.** A bigger pool only raises the threshold: occupancy under a\n  hang is rate × timeout, set by the caller's traffic, and retries push that rate up until any fixed pool\n  fills. A shorter timeout only shrinks the number, it does not cap it. A circuit breaker needs a minimum\n  number of observed failures first, and a hung dependency that never errors can take minutes to register.\n- **The missing property is a hard ceiling per compartment.** A ship's hull is divided into watertight\n  compartments so one breach floods one section, not the vessel; a software bulkhead does the same for\n  worker threads, connections, semaphores, queues and pods. Where a circuit breaker reacts after it\n  detects failure, a bulkhead bounds the damage whether or not anything detects it.","how":"### Size with Little's law\n```\ncap ≈ peak rate × p99 latency + burst headroom     (and a small fraction of the total pool)\ntolerated average latency = cap ÷ rate\n```\n- **Use a high percentile, not the mean,** because LLM latency has a long tail and the cap must absorb it.\n- **In the failure case latency equals the timeout,** so occupancy can never exceed the cap no matter how\n  slow the dependency gets. The formula tells you both how large a cap is safe and the worst damage the\n  dependency can do.\n- **Read it backwards.** A cap *C* on a dependency called at rate λ tolerates an average latency of up to\n  *C* ÷ λ before callers are rejected. A CRM cap of 20 at 5 calls per second admits everything until CRM\n  latency averages 4 seconds, about thirteen times its normal 300 ms; beyond that the bulkhead sheds.\n- **That number is the real design choice:** how much slowdown you will absorb before you would rather\n  degrade.\n\n### Oversubscription is fine; check the right condition\n- **Caps do not have to sum to the pool,** because dependencies rarely all hang at once.\n- **What must hold:** when any one compartment is pinned at its cap, the remaining capacity still covers the\n  healthy paths' demand. Include any pair that shares a plausible common cause, such as two APIs behind the\n  same customer VPN.\n- **Check that condition, not the sum.**\n\n### Five forms, from cheapest to strongest\n| Form | Mechanism | Use when | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Semaphore | counting semaphore per dependency; acquire before the call, release after; fail immediately or wait a very short bounded time | non-blocking clients; the default in async services | nearly free |\n| 2. Thread pool | separate executor per dependency | blocking clients and CPU-bound work (a synchronous SDK, a tokenizer, PDF parsing) that would stall the event loop or each other | threads and context switches |\n| 3. Connection pool | separate HTTP client with its own connection limits per upstream | always, *in addition to* a semaphore | a client object per upstream |\n| 4. Queue | separate queues and consumer groups per workload class (interactive versus batch, per tenant tier) | batch and interactive share a fleet | more queues and consumers |\n| 5. Deployment | separate pods, node pools, namespaces with resource quotas, or clusters; separate provider keys or model deployments | a tenant pays for isolation or a vendor limit must be isolated | highest |\n\n- **Why the connection pool needs its own compartment:** it is itself a shared resource. A hung upstream\n  holds its connections open, and if every dependency shares one client, the healthy ones cannot get a\n  socket even though their semaphores show headroom.\n- **Why queues isolate:** the consumer fleet is the compartment, so a flood of batch jobs can fill the batch\n  queue to any depth without delaying an interactive message.\n- **Why deployments are strongest:** they are the only form that also isolates vendor-side limits.\n\n### What to compartment by\n- **By dependency:** the most common, and the baseline.\n- **By workload class:** protects interactive latency from batch volume.\n- **By tenant tier:** how you sell isolation.\n- **By criticality, the one teams forget:** health and readiness endpoints, authentication and the\n  admission layer must never share a compartment with LLM calls, or an upstream outage turns into\n  restarts and lock-outs.\n\n### Reject or wait\n- **When a compartment is full, fail fast or wait a bounded, short time for a permit.**\n- **Unbounded waiting is forbidden** because it recreates the shared-pool problem one level up: the waiters\n  are now the stuck resource.\n- **A rejection should enable graceful degradation** (answer without the CRM context, serve from cache,\n  enqueue for later) or an honest 503 with `Retry-After`.\n\n### How it composes with breakers and load shedding\n| Mechanism | Kind | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bulkhead | static limit | bounds damage regardless of health; also caps the load you put on the dependency |\n| Circuit breaker | dynamic reaction | stops sending once failure is observed |\n| Load shedding | global admission control | drops the lowest-value work when the whole service is saturated |\n\n- **The bulkhead protects the dependency too:** a cap of 20 is the most concurrent load your service can\n  ever put on the CRM, which matters when the CRM's own capacity is the thing that is failing.\n- **They stack:** the bulkhead keeps a hung dependency from exhausting the pool while the breaker\n  accumulates its minimum calls; once the breaker opens, even the bulkhead's permits are freed; if the\n  whole service is saturated, shedding drops the lowest-value work first.","example":"### Per-dependency semaphore bulkheads with a degrade path\nSemaphore bulkheads with a short bounded wait, separate HTTP clients so the connection pool is partitioned\ntoo, and a degrade path when a compartment is full. The caps come from Little's law with illustrative\ntraffic numbers.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, httpx\n\nclass BulkheadFull(Exception): pass\n\nclass Bulkhead:\n    \"\"\"Caps concurrent calls to one dependency; a caller waits at most max_wait for a slot.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, name, max_concurrent, max_wait=0.25):\n        self.name, self.max_wait = name, max_wait\n        self.sem = asyncio.Semaphore(max_concurrent)\n\n    async def run(self, fn, *args, **kw):\n        try:\n            await asyncio.wait_for(self.sem.acquire(), self.max_wait)\n        except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n            raise BulkheadFull(f\"{self.name} at capacity\")     # fail fast; never queue without bound\n        try:\n            return await fn(*args, **kw)\n        finally:\n            self.sem.release()                                  # released on success, error or cancellation\n\n# Little's law: concurrency = peak rate x tail latency, plus headroom. Illustrative numbers:\n# LLM 20 rps x 3 s = 60 -> cap 100; vector DB 40 rps x 0.05 s = 2 -> cap 10; CRM 5 rps x 0.3 s = 1.5 -> cap 20\n# (generous headroom for the CRM, but still a tenth of the 200-worker pool, so a hang can take at most 20).\nLLM = Bulkhead(\"llm\", 100)\nVEC = Bulkhead(\"vector_db\", 10)\nCRM = Bulkhead(\"crm_api\", 20, max_wait=0.05)\n\n# Separate HTTP clients: the connection pool is a shared resource as well.\nllm_http = httpx.AsyncClient(limits=httpx.Limits(max_connections=100), timeout=30.0)\ncrm_http = httpx.AsyncClient(limits=httpx.Limits(max_connections=20), timeout=5.0)\n\nasync def handle(req):\n    docs = await VEC.run(search, req.query)\n    try:\n        account = await CRM.run(lookup_account, req.user_id)\n    except BulkheadFull:\n        account = None                                          # degrade: answer without CRM context\n    return await LLM.run(generate, req, docs, account)\n```\n\n- **Outcome with the CRM hung:** at most 20 permits plus 20 connections are ever tied up; the other 180\n  workers keep serving requests that need only retrieval and the model.\n- **Degradation is fast:** CRM-dependent requests degrade in 50 milliseconds instead of hanging for 30\n  seconds.\n- **The `finally` matters:** a permit leaked on cancellation is a compartment that slowly fills with ghosts.\n\n### Sizing calculator with the oversubscription check\nRunnable: computes normal demand, the latency each cap tolerates, and whether the healthy paths still fit\nwhen one compartment is pinned at its cap.\n\n```python\nPOOL = 200\ndeps = {                      # name: (peak_rps, p99_latency_s, cap)\n    \"llm\":       (20, 3.0, 100),\n    \"vector_db\": (40, 0.05, 10),\n    \"crm_api\":   (5, 0.3, 20),\n}\n\ndef demand(rps, latency):\n    return rps * latency      # Little's law: concurrency = rate x time in system\n\nfor name, (rps, p99, cap) in deps.items():\n    print(f\"{name}: normal {demand(rps, p99):.1f}, cap {cap}, \"\n          f\"sheds once avg latency > {cap / rps:.2f}s\")\n\nfor hung, (_, _, cap) in deps.items():   # pin one compartment at its cap\n    healthy = sum(demand(r, l) for n, (r, l, _) in deps.items() if n != hung)\n    left = POOL - cap\n    print(f\"{hung} hung: {left} workers left, healthy paths need {healthy:.1f} -> \"\n          f\"{'ok' if left >= healthy else 'CASCADE'}\")\n# crm_api: normal 1.5, cap 20, sheds once avg latency > 4.00s\n# crm_api hung: 180 workers left, healthy paths need 62.0 -> ok\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the CRM hang, before and after\n| Quantity | Shared pool | With bulkheads |\n|---|---|---|\n| CRM occupancy while hung | 5 × 30 = 150 workers | capped at 20 |\n| LLM demand | 20 × 3 = 60 workers | 60, served from the 180 left |\n| Total demand vs pool | 210 vs 200: saturated | 80 vs 200: fine |\n| CRM-dependent request | hangs 30 s, then fails | `BulkheadFull` in 50 ms, answered without CRM |\n| Health checks | miss, pods restart | own path, stay green |","patterns":["**Resilience4j bulkheads on the JVM.** A semaphore bulkhead per dependency with `maxConcurrentCalls` and a short `maxWaitDuration`; a thread-pool bulkhead (`maxThreadPoolSize`, `coreThreadPoolSize`, `queueCapacity`) for blocking SDKs. Monitor available permits and rejected calls per bulkhead.","**Blocking SDKs off the event loop.** In asyncio, run a synchronous client on its own executor via `loop.run_in_executor(executor, fn)` with a small dedicated `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=...)`, so a hang stalls only that executor. Monitor event-loop lag; spikes mean blocking code leaked in.","**One HTTP client per upstream.** Create a separate client with its own connection limits per upstream (as with `httpx.Limits` above) and its own timeout. Monitor connection-pool wait time per client.","**Separate queues for batch and interactive.** Give batch work its own queue and consumer group with its own concurrency, so batch depth never delays interactive messages. Monitor per-queue depth and age of the oldest message.","**Namespace quotas for deployment bulkheads.** In Kubernetes, give each workload class or tenant tier its own namespace with a `ResourceQuota`, or its own node pool, so one class cannot schedule over another. Monitor quota usage and pending pods per namespace."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"Incoming request\"] --> W[\"Worker pool: 200\"]\n    H[\"Health checks\"] --> HP[\"Dedicated health path\"]\n    W --> V[\"Vector DB bulkhead: 10\"]\n    W --> C{\"CRM bulkhead: 20 free?\"}\n    W --> L[\"LLM bulkhead: 100\"]\n    C -->|\"yes\"| CC[\"CRM client, 20 connections\"]\n    C -->|\"no, after 50 ms\"| D[\"Degrade: answer without CRM\"]\n    L --> LC[\"LLM client, 100 connections\"]\n    CC -.->|\"hung: at most 20 stuck\"| X[\"CRM API\"]","caption":"Each dependency gets its own permits and its own HTTP client, and health checks bypass them all; a full\ncompartment degrades its own requests instead of draining the shared pool."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a bulkhead, and why isn't a bigger shared pool an equivalent fix?","a":"- **Definition:** a hard concurrency ceiling per compartment (dependency, workload class, tenant tier), so\n  one exhausted compartment cannot drain capacity the others need.\n- **A bigger pool only moves the threshold,** because occupancy during a hang is rate × timeout, set by\n  caller traffic; retries raise the rate until any fixed pool fills.\n- **The bulkhead turns that unbounded number into one you chose.**"},{"q":"How would you size a bulkhead for an LLM dependency doing 20 requests per second at a 3-second p99?","a":"- **Little's law:** 20 × 3 = 60 concurrent calls in normal operation, so a cap around 100 leaves burst\n  headroom.\n- **Check what it tolerates:** 100 ÷ 20 = 5 seconds of average latency before shedding starts.\n- **Check the pool:** with that compartment pinned at 100, the remaining workers must still cover every\n  healthy path's demand."},{"q":"Every compartment shows free permits, yet the whole service, including health checks, is stalled. What do you suspect?","a":"- **A blocking call on the event loop,** such as a synchronous SDK inside async code; all semaphores live on\n  that loop, so nothing progresses.\n- **Or a shared HTTP client:** a hung upstream holds every connection in the common pool while semaphores\n  show headroom.\n- **Fix:** move blocking work to its own executor, give each upstream its own client, and watch event-loop\n  lag and connection-pool wait."},{"q":"When would you not use hard per-dependency bulkheads?","a":"- **When load is bursty and uncorrelated and idle capacity is costly:** hard caps strand permits, so use soft\n  partitions with a guaranteed minimum plus a shared overflow pool.\n- **When there is only one dependency and one workload class,** a single pool with timeouts and load\n  shedding is simpler and loses nothing."},{"q":"How do bulkheads, circuit breakers and load shedding work together during a dependency hang?","a":"- **Bulkhead first:** caps how many workers the hung dependency can hold while the breaker is still\n  accumulating its minimum calls.\n- **Breaker next:** once failures are confirmed it stops sending, freeing the bulkhead's permits.\n- **Shedding last:** if the whole service is saturated, global admission control drops the lowest-value\n  work first."}],"tradeoffs":["**Isolation vs utilization.** Capacity in one compartment cannot be borrowed by another, so the CRM's 20 permits sit idle when the CRM is quiet. When load is bursty and uncorrelated, soft partitions (a guaranteed minimum per compartment plus a shared overflow pool) recover most of the waste.","**Cheap vs strong forms.** Semaphores are cheap but only bound non-blocking work; thread pools isolate blocking and CPU-bound calls at the cost of threads; separate deployments isolate everything, including vendor limits, at the cost of money. Escalate only when the cheaper form demonstrably leaks.","**Short vs long wait.** A tiny `max_wait` fails fast but rejects during ordinary micro-bursts; a longer one absorbs bursts but ties callers up. Interactive paths want the short wait; batch paths can afford to queue, in their own queue.","**Fine vs coarse compartments.** Many fine compartments give precise isolation and metrics but multiply configuration and the chance of mis-sizing one; coarse compartments are simple but share blast radius. Partition by failure domain, not by code module."],"pitfalls":["**Sizing the cap from mean latency.** Symptom: steady trickle of `BulkheadFull` rejections on a healthy dependency at peak, because the tail of slow but successful calls holds permits the mean did not account for. Fix: size from p99.","**Setting the cap as a large fraction of the pool.** Symptom: a bulkhead of 150 out of 200 \"works\" until the dependency hangs, and the service still cascades because 150 stuck workers is an outage.","**Sharing one HTTP client across dependencies behind separate semaphores.** Symptom: the CRM semaphore shows five in flight while CRM calls hang, because the LLM's slow responses hold every connection in the common pool.","**Blocking calls inside async code.** Symptom: every compartment reports headroom, yet everything including health checks stalls, because a synchronous SDK call froze the event loop that all the semaphores live on.","**Letting health and readiness endpoints share the worker pool.** Symptom: during a dependency outage the orchestrator restarts healthy pods, converting a partial outage into a total one.","**No degrade path for `BulkheadFull`.** Symptom: user-visible error rate rises in lockstep with the share of requests that happen to touch the saturated dependency, even though the answer could have been given without it.","**Releasing the permit only on the success path.** Symptom: capacity drifts downward over days; the compartment reads full with no real work in flight, and a restart \"fixes\" it."],"when":"- **Use when** a service talks to more than one dependency with different latency or reliability, or\n  serves more than one workload class.\n- **Start with the cheap baseline:** a semaphore per dependency sized from Little's law, a separate HTTP\n  client per upstream, and a dedicated path for health checks. Promote to queue-level separation when\n  batch and interactive traffic share a fleet, and to deployment separation when a tenant pays for it or a\n  vendor key limit must be isolated.\n- **Measure** per-compartment in-flight count, rejection rate and wait time; alert on sustained saturation\n  of any one compartment, which is an early warning long before the breaker trips.\n- **Interviews:** the question is \"one slow dependency took down the whole service, how do you prevent\n  it\". The complete answer is timeouts to bound each call, bulkheads to bound the aggregate, and breakers\n  to stop sending, with the sizing arithmetic to show why each is needed.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures per-dependency semaphores and per-upstream clients, moves blocking SDKs onto their own executor, and writes the degrade path for every `BulkheadFull`.","architect":"Decides the compartment boundaries, by dependency, workload class, tenant tier and criticality, and whether each lives in-process, at the queue, or as a separate deployment.","fde":"Uses bulkheads so a customer's flaky legacy system cannot take down the agent, and separates the customer's bulk backfills from their users' interactive sessions inside one deployment."},"check":{"q":"An agent service runs one shared pool of 200 workers with 30-second timeouts. It calls an LLM\n(about 20 requests per second, 3 seconds each) and a CRM API (5 requests per second, normally 300 ms).\nThe CRM API hangs. Walk through what happens with numbers, then show how bulkheads change the outcome.","a":"With the CRM hung, each CRM call occupies a worker until the 30-second timeout. Little's law gives\n5 × 30 = 150 workers stuck in steady state, reached within about 30 seconds. LLM traffic needs 20 × 3 = 60\nworkers, so total demand is 210 against a pool of 200: the pool saturates, LLM-only requests queue or\nfail, health checks miss, and pods restart. With a CRM bulkhead capped at 20 permits and a 50 ms maximum\nwait, CRM occupancy can never exceed 20; 180 workers remain, more than the 60 the LLM path needs.\nCRM-dependent requests receive `BulkheadFull` within 50 ms and degrade by answering without CRM context.\nA separate HTTP client for the CRM keeps its hung sockets out of the LLM's connection pool. Shrinking the\nCRM timeout to 5 seconds additionally lowers worst-case demand to 25 even without the cap, and a circuit\nbreaker on top releases the 20 permits once failures are confirmed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the essential difference between a bulkhead and a circuit breaker?","options":["A bulkhead retries failed calls; a breaker does not","A bulkhead statically bounds resource use per compartment regardless of health; a breaker dynamically stops calls after observing failures","A bulkhead applies to databases and a breaker to HTTP APIs","They are two names for the same mechanism in different libraries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The bulkhead works before and without detection: a hung dependency can never hold more than its cap. The breaker needs evidence of failure first, then removes load entirely. They are complementary, not interchangeable."},{"q":"A dependency is called 10 times per second with a 20-second timeout. In the worst case, how many concurrent calls can it tie up without a bulkhead?","options":["10","20","200","2"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Little's law: concurrency equals arrival rate times time in system, and when the dependency hangs the time in system is the timeout, so 10 × 20 = 200. The bulkhead cap replaces that number with a ceiling you choose."},{"q":"Why give each upstream its own HTTP client even when per-dependency semaphores are already in place?","options":["Separate clients make requests faster by avoiding TLS handshakes","The connection pool is itself a shared resource, and a hung upstream's open connections can exhaust it for healthy upstreams","Semaphores do not work with HTTP clients","It is required by the HTTP/2 specification"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A semaphore bounds how many calls your code starts, but a shared client has its own connection limit; sockets held open by a hung dependency are unavailable to the others, so the healthy paths stall while their semaphores show plenty of permits."}]},{"key":"reliability/fmea","sources":["AIAG & VDA, 2019 — FMEA Handbook"],"why":"- **Teams discover failure modes one incident at a time.** Postmortems are reactive by definition, and\n  chaos experiments only test hypotheses you already hold. Something has to generate the hypotheses, and\n  \"we will add monitoring and handle issues as they come\" generates them at the worst possible moment.\n- **AI systems' worst failures are silent.** The service returns 200 OK with a plausible, wrong answer. The\n  embedding index stops refreshing and retrieval drifts stale over weeks. An agent's tool call succeeds,\n  against the wrong customer's record. Availability monitoring sees a healthy system throughout.\n- **Intuition ranks by vividness.** \"The provider goes down\" gets a failover project while \"the tenant\n  filter is missing on the new index\" gets nothing, even though the second is catastrophic and the first is\n  a nuisance with a retry.\n- **FMEA counters both mechanically.** Walking the request path component by component with a checklist of\n  ways to fail forces attention onto boring, high-consequence modes. Scoring detection separately surfaces\n  the key asymmetry: a failure you cannot see is more dangerous than one that pages you, because its effect\n  compounds until a customer reports it.\n- **The output is a ranked backlog, not a document.** It comes from safety engineering, and it is the\n  cheapest way to find the failures nobody is monitoring. The top rows become guards, evals, alerts and\n  chaos experiments, in that order of priority.","how":"### 1. Decompose the system into functions\n- **List the steps in the request path and the cross-cutting concerns.** For a retrieval-augmented agent:\n  ingestion, chunking, embedding, index refresh, query embedding, retrieval, tenant scoping, prompt\n  assembly, model call, output parsing and validation, tool execution, response delivery; plus\n  authentication, rate limiting, logging and the deployment process itself.\n- **Match granularity to the decisions you can act on:** \"the backend\" is too coarse, individual functions\n  in a file too fine.\n\n### 2. Enumerate failure modes per function\n- **A failure mode is *how* the function fails,** stated as an observable deviation, not as \"it breaks\".\n- **Use guide words to force breadth,** borrowed from hazard analysis: none (no output), more, less, late,\n  early, wrong, partial, stale, duplicated, to the wrong recipient, without authorization.\n- **Applied to retrieval** this yields \"returns nothing\", \"returns too many chunks and truncates the\n  prompt\", \"returns stale documents\", \"returns another tenant's documents\", \"returns the right documents\n  too slowly\". A team that only lists \"retrieval is down\" has not done the step.\n\n### 3. Score the three factors\n| Factor | Question | Scale (1 to 10) | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Severity (S) | effect at the user or business level | 9 to 10 irreversible or legal harm (money moved, data leaked, unsafe advice acted on); 6 to 8 a wrong result the user is likely to act on; 3 to 5 visible degradation or a failed request; 1 to 2 cosmetic | anchor each number with a written example so two raters agree |\n| Occurrence (O) | how often the causes produce the mode | 1 practically never to 10 constant | use data where it exists (provider error rates, eval failure rates, index-job history); mark guesses as guesses, because O is the number most often wrong |\n| Detection (D) | how likely current controls catch it *before* the effect reaches the user | 1 certain (a type check fails the build) to 10 no way to detect | inverted: a high D is bad |\n\n- **\"We have logs\" scores 8 or 9** unless someone is alerted by them, because logs nobody reads detect\n  nothing.\n\n### 4. Rank: Risk Priority Number and its limits\n```\nRPN = S × O × D          (1 to 1,000, sort descending)\n```\n- **Useful because it is simple, misleading in two specific ways.**\n- **The factors are treated as interchangeable.** A rare, hard-to-detect catastrophe (S 10, O 1, D 9 gives\n  90) ranks below a frequent, moderately visible annoyance (S 4, O 6, D 5 gives 120). Rows with identical\n  RPNs can carry completely different risks (S 10, O 2, D 5 and S 5, O 4, D 5 are both 100).\n- **The scales are ordinal.** A 6 is \"worse than a 5\", not 20 percent worse, so the product has no physical\n  meaning. Only 120 distinct values between 1 and 1,000 can occur at all, which makes gaps between RPNs\n  look larger than the judgment behind them.\n- **The industry moved on.** The automotive industry's 2019 handbook replaced RPN with an Action Priority\n  table that looks at severity first, then occurrence, then detection, for these reasons.\n- **Practical rule:** any row with severity 9 or 10 goes to the top regardless of RPN, then sort the rest by\n  RPN.\n\n### 5. Actions, owners and re-scoring\n| Action type | Lowers | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prevention | O | a schema that makes the bad input impossible |\n| Containment | S | a confirmation step before an irreversible tool, a spending cap |\n| Detection | D | an eval in CI, a freshness alert, a canary query |\n\n- **Assign an owner, re-score after the action,** and revisit the table on every architectural change.\n- **A score that never changes** is a sign the table has become decoration.\n\n### Design versus process FMEA\n- **Design FMEA** analyzes the system as built.\n- **Process FMEA** analyzes how it is operated and changed: deploys, prompt edits, model version upgrades,\n  index rebuilds, credential rotation.\n- **AI systems need both, and the process rows often score highest,** because a prompt edit or a model\n  version change can alter behavior everywhere at once without touching code, and without an eval gate\n  nothing stands between the edit and production.\n\n### Bottom-up, one failure at a time, and what that misses\n- **FMEA is bottom-up:** it starts from components and asks what each failure does to the whole.\n- **Its blind spot is combinations,** because each row considers a single failure mode: the index is stale\n  *and* the freshness alert's job is down; the provider is slow *and* retries double the load.\n- **Fault tree analysis is the complementary top-down method:** start from one unacceptable outcome (\"money\n  refunded to the wrong customer\") and decompose it with AND/OR gates into the combinations of events that\n  produce it.\n- **Use both:** FMEA for the breadth of modes, and a small fault tree for each severity-10 effect to check\n  that no single failure, or common pair, reaches it.","example":"### A support assistant with a refund tool\nA fragment of an FMEA for an assistant that answers from a document index and can issue refunds through a\ntool. Scores are the team's judgment calls on a 1 to 10 scale; the point is the ranking, not the precision.\n\n| Function | Failure mode | Effect | S | Cause | O | Current detection | D | RPN |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Index refresh | Serves stale policy docs | Agent quotes outdated refund terms; customer promised the wrong amount | 7 | Nightly rebuild job fails silently | 4 | Job logs only, no alert | 9 | 252 |\n| Refund tool | Refund issued against the wrong order | Money moved irreversibly | 10 | Model copies a similar-looking order id from context | 3 | None before execution | 8 | 240 |\n| Tenant scoping | Retrieval returns another tenant's documents | Data leak across customers | 10 | Filter omitted in a new retrieval client | 2 | None | 9 | 180 |\n| Model call | Provider timeout or 5xx | Request fails; user sees an error and retries | 4 | Provider incident | 5 | Error-rate alert pages on-call | 2 | 40 |\n| Output parsing | Response violates JSON schema | Reply dropped, user sees generic error | 3 | Prompt edit or model drift | 4 | Parser raises; counted and alerted | 3 | 36 |\n\n- **The planned-for failure ranks last.** The provider outage, which everyone had been planning around, is\n  loud, so it is already detected and handled. The three rows that matter are quiet.\n- **Stale documents:** add a freshness SLI (age of newest indexed document) with an alert. D drops from 9 to\n  3 and the RPN to 84.\n- **Refund tool:** pass the order id from the ticket's structured field rather than generating it from free\n  text, and add a confirmation step above a value threshold. O drops to 1, D to 2, and the RPN to 20, while\n  S stays 10 because the harm, if it happens, is unchanged.\n- **Tenant scoping:** make the tenant filter mandatory in the retrieval client's constructor and run an\n  automated cross-tenant canary query hourly. D drops to 2 and the row, still severity 10, is now contained.\n- **Cost:** the team spent one afternoon and re-prioritized a quarter.\n\n### Severity-gated prioritization, runnable\nThe same rows sorted the way the practical rule says: severity 9 or 10 first, then RPN. Pure RPN would put\nstale docs (252) above the refund tool; the gate does not.\n\n```python\nrows = [  # (function, failure mode, S, O, D)\n    (\"Index refresh\",  \"serves stale policy docs\",      7, 4, 9),\n    (\"Refund tool\",    \"refund against the wrong order\", 10, 3, 8),\n    (\"Tenant scoping\", \"returns another tenant's docs\", 10, 2, 9),\n    (\"Model call\",     \"provider timeout or 5xx\",        4, 5, 2),\n    (\"Output parsing\", \"violates JSON schema\",           3, 4, 3),\n]\n\ndef rpn(s, o, d):\n    return s * o * d\n\ndef prioritize(rows, severity_gate=9):\n    # rows at or above the severity gate first, then everything by RPN, descending\n    return sorted(rows, key=lambda r: (r[2] < severity_gate, -rpn(*r[2:])))\n\nfor fn, mode, s, o, d in prioritize(rows):\n    print(f\"{rpn(s, o, d):>4}  S{s:<2} O{o} D{d}  {fn}: {mode}\")\n# 240 refund tool, 180 tenant scoping, 252 index refresh, 40 model call, 36 output parsing\n\ndistinct = {s * o * d for s in range(1, 11) for o in range(1, 11) for d in range(1, 11)}\nprint(len(distinct), \"distinct RPN values between 1 and 1,000\")   # 120\n```","patterns":["**Two-hour launch workshop.** Before launch or before adding an irreversible tool, run a session with the owner of each stage and a spreadsheet with the columns above; walk the request path with the guide words. Track the number of open severity-9-and-10 rows without a control as a launch gate.","**D column as the monitoring backlog.** Every row with D of 7 or more becomes an alert, SLI or canary ticket, for example a freshness SLI on the newest indexed document or an hourly cross-tenant canary query. Monitor how many high-D rows remain open.","**Policy controls for severity 10.** Require a confirmation step, a spending cap or a kill switch for any tool on a severity-10 row, and draw a small fault tree for that effect. Monitor confirmation rates and kill-switch drills.","**Process FMEA wired into change management.** Prompt edits, model version upgrades and index rebuilds go through an eval gate in CI, because those process rows often score highest. Monitor eval pass rate per change and changes shipped without the gate.","**Re-score from incidents and chaos results.** Each postmortem updates O and D for its row and adds sibling modes; the top rows become chaos experiment hypotheses. Monitor the date each row was last re-scored."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    F[\"Decompose into functions\"] --> M[\"Failure modes via guide words\"]\n    M --> S[\"Score S, O and D per row\"]\n    S --> G{\"Severity 9 or 10?\"}\n    G -->|\"yes\"| T[\"Top of backlog, plus fault tree\"]\n    G -->|\"no\"| R[\"Sort rest by RPN\"]\n    T --> A[\"Actions: prevent, contain, detect\"]\n    R --> A\n    A --> O[\"Owner and re-score\"]\n    O -.->|\"on every architecture change\"| M","caption":"The loop runs from functions to a ranked backlog; note the severity gate before the RPN sort and the\nre-score that closes the loop."},"interview":[{"q":"What is FMEA, and why do it when you already have monitoring and postmortems?","a":"- **Definition:** a structured table of failure modes per function, with effects, causes and current\n  detection, scored by severity, occurrence and detection so the worst risks are fixed first.\n- **Postmortems are reactive and monitoring only sees what you thought to measure;** FMEA generates the\n  hypotheses, especially the silent ones (plausible wrong answers, stale retrieval, wrong-record tool calls)."},{"q":"How would you run an FMEA for a RAG agent you are about to launch?","a":"- **Decompose the request path:** ingestion through response delivery, plus auth, rate limiting, logging\n  and deployment.\n- **Apply guide words** (none, stale, wrong, partial, to the wrong recipient) to each function, then score\n  S, O and D with anchored scales and real data for O.\n- **Output:** severity-9-and-10 rows first, each with an owner and a prevention, containment or detection\n  action, and the D column turned into alerts."},{"q":"Two rows both score an RPN of 100. How do you decide which to fix first?","a":"- **Look at the factors, not the product:** S 10, O 2, D 5 and S 5, O 4, D 5 are both 100 but the first can\n  be catastrophic.\n- **Severity first,** as the Action Priority approach in the 2019 handbook does, then occurrence, then\n  detection.\n- **Also remember the scales are ordinal,** so the product has no physical meaning to compare precisely."},{"q":"When is FMEA the wrong tool, and what complements it?","a":"- **It considers one failure at a time,** so combinations (a stale index and a dead freshness alert) slip\n  through; draw a top-down fault tree for each severity-10 effect.\n- **It is not testing:** it tells you where to test and what to chaos-test.\n- **For throwaway prototypes** the workshop costs more than the risk it removes."},{"q":"Your team is upgrading the model version behind an agent. What does a process FMEA add?","a":"- **Process rows cover how the system changes,** and a model upgrade can alter behavior everywhere at once\n  without touching code.\n- **Typical modes:** output schema violations, changed refusal or tool-use behavior, quality regressions on\n  edge cases, each with low detection unless an eval gate exists.\n- **Action:** an eval gate in CI and a staged rollout lower D before the change reaches production."}],"tradeoffs":["**Breadth vs time.** A full FMEA across every component takes days, while a two-hour session over the request path finds most of the high-risk rows. Go deep when the system takes irreversible actions or carries regulatory exposure; stay shallow for an internal tool with a human in the loop.","**Numeric scores vs real precision.** They look objective but encode judgment; consistency between raters matters more than precision. With a small team, anchored scales or even high/medium/low buckets beat spurious decimals.","**RPN vs severity-first.** RPN gives one sortable number but hides rare catastrophes; severity-first ordering protects against catastrophe but can leave frequent, irritating failures unaddressed. Use severity gates first, RPN second.","**Living table vs one-off.** A living table stays useful across architecture changes but needs an owner and a review cadence; a one-off exercise is cheap but stale after the first major change, and a stale FMEA creates false confidence."],"pitfalls":["**Listing only \"component unavailable\" modes.** Symptom: the table reads like an uptime checklist and has no rows for wrong, stale, partial or cross-tenant output, which is where AI systems actually fail.","**Scoring detection as good because \"we log it\".** Symptom: incident reviews that find the signal was sitting in logs for days before a customer reported the problem.","**Running the analysis alone.** Symptom: the modes that bite live in areas the author does not own, such as the data pipeline, the vendor's change process or the customer's upstream system.","**Treating it as a compliance artifact.** Symptom: RPNs never re-scored, the action column empty, and the file last modified the week before an audit.","**Scoring occurrence from intuition when data exists.** Symptom: provider 429s scored as rare while the dashboard shows them daily, so the row is deprioritized and the retry budget never gets fixed.","**Treating rows as independent.** Symptom: every row has a control, yet an incident happens when two controls fail together, for example the canary and the alert both running on the same scheduler that stopped.","**Confusing cause with mode.** Symptom: rows like \"engineer forgot the filter\", which cannot be detected or scored for effect; the mode is \"retrieval returns another tenant's data\", the forgotten filter is one of its causes."],"when":"- **Use before** launching anything with tool side effects or external users, before adding any\n  irreversible tool to an agent, after a serious incident to find its siblings, and whenever you need to\n  decide what to monitor or what to chaos-test: the D column is a monitoring backlog and the top rows are\n  your first experiments.\n- **Skip it** for throwaway prototypes, and never let it substitute for testing; it tells you where to test.\n- **In day-to-day work** it shows up as a two-hour workshop with a spreadsheet and the people who own each\n  stage.\n- **Interviews:** the question is \"how would you figure out what could go wrong with this agent\"; walk the\n  request path with guide words, score a few rows aloud, and name the top three with their mitigations.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Enumerates failure modes for the stages they own, scores them with data from dashboards and evals, and turns the top rows into guards, regression evals and alerts.","architect":"Owns the system-level table across teams and vendors, sets the severity scale, and decides what severity-10 rows require by policy: confirmation steps, spending caps, kill switches.","fde":"Runs a joint FMEA with the customer before go-live, because the customer knows the business effects and their legacy systems' failure modes; the result becomes the hypercare monitoring plan."},"check":{"q":"You are prioritizing reliability work for an agent that reads a customer's CRM and drafts emails\nthat a human approves before sending. One engineer wants to spend the sprint on multi-provider failover.\nUsing FMEA reasoning, how do you evaluate that proposal?","a":"Score the row it addresses. Provider outage: severity about 4, since a human is waiting and can\nretry and nothing irreversible happens; occurrence from the provider's history, perhaps 4; detection 2,\nbecause error-rate alerts already fire. RPN around 32. Now score the quiet rows. \"Agent reads the wrong\ncustomer's record and drafts an email containing their data\": severity 9 if the approver misses it\n(personal data sent to the wrong recipient), occurrence 3 given lookups by fuzzy name, detection 8\nbecause nothing checks that the retrieved record matches the recipient. RPN around 216, and it is a\nseverity-9 row, so it leads regardless. \"CRM cache serves stale data\" lands in between. The right sprint\nis a cheap guard that asserts the recipient's account id equals the retrieved record's id, an eval that\nexercises near-duplicate names, and a freshness alert on the cache; failover can follow once the\ncatastrophic, undetected rows are contained. Show the table so the decision is about numbers, not\nopinions."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Detection score measure, and which direction is bad?","options":["How quickly the team responds after an incident is declared; low is bad","How likely current controls are to catch the failure before its effect reaches the user; high is bad","How many monitoring dashboards exist for the component; low is bad","How severe the failure is when it is finally noticed; high is bad"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Detection rates the controls that exist today, from 1 (certain to catch it) to 10 (no way to notice). The inversion trips people up: a high D multiplies the risk, which is why \"we have logs\" should not be scored as good detection unless someone is alerted by them."},{"q":"Why can sorting purely by RPN mislead, and what is the standard correction?","options":["RPN ignores occurrence, so frequent failures are under-ranked; weight occurrence double","RPN treats the factors as interchangeable, so a rare undetectable catastrophe can rank below a frequent cosmetic bug; gate on severity first, then sort by RPN","RPN only works for hardware, so software teams should use mean time between failures instead","RPN overweights detection, so detection should be dropped from the formula"],"answer":1,"explanation":"S 10 × O 1 × D 9 is 90 while S 4 × O 6 × D 5 is 120, yet the first row, a rare catastrophe nobody would notice, is the one that ends the company. Severity-first ordering, as in the Action Priority approach, keeps catastrophic rows at the top; doubling a weight only moves the distortion elsewhere."},{"q":"Which of these is correctly stated as a failure mode rather than an effect or a cause?","options":["The engineer forgot to add the tenant filter to the new retrieval client","A customer sees a competitor's pricing document in their answer","Retrieval returns documents belonging to another tenant","The vector database was upgraded last week"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A mode is the observable deviation in the function's output. The forgotten filter and the upgrade are causes; the customer seeing the document is the effect. Keeping the three apart is what makes occurrence, severity and detection scorable."}]},{"key":"reliability/chaos-engineering-and-resilience-testing","sources":["Basiri et al., 2016 — Chaos Engineering (IEEE Software)","Rosenthal & Jones, 2020 — Chaos Engineering: System Resiliency in Practice"],"why":"- **Fallback code is the least-tested code in any system.** A team ships multi-provider failover. Six\n  months later the primary degrades, the fallback fires for the first time in production, and fails: the\n  secondary's API key expired, its model does not accept the same tool-call format, or its rate limit is a\n  tenth of the primary's.\n- **Existing tests do not reach it.** Every layer of resilience is another untested path. Unit tests mock\n  the dependency. End-to-end tests run the happy path. Staging has neither production traffic patterns nor\n  production data volumes nor the real vendor quotas.\n- **Letting incidents validate the design is the naive alternative.** You learn, but the lesson arrives at\n  three in the morning, at full scale, uncontrolled, with customers watching. Chaos engineering's founding\n  observation: the failure will happen anyway; the only choice is whether it happens while you are\n  watching, at one percent of traffic, with a kill switch, or later.\n- **Emergent failures need whole-system tests.** Retry storms, thundering herds on recovery, cache\n  stampedes, and cost amplification when traffic falls to a pricier fallback model are predicted by no\n  component test; only exercising the whole system under fault reveals them.\n- **Systems drift.** A fallback verified in March is unverified by June after three model upgrades and a\n  vendor's quota change. Verification has to be repeated, which means it has to be cheap and automated.","how":"### Resilience testing: proving the mechanisms you built\nThis pre-production family comes first, because there is no point running experiments against mechanisms\nthat do not exist yet. In pre-production it covers fault injection, load, stress and soak tests, failover\ndrills and game days.\n\n| Test | What you do | What it proves |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fault injection | at the dependency boundary, make the provider client return 429s, 5xxs, 8-second latencies or truncated completions for a configurable fraction of calls | timeouts, breakers, backoff and fallbacks behave as designed |\n| Load | expected peak | SLOs hold |\n| Stress | beyond peak | where the knee is (latency explodes) and whether shedding engages |\n| Soak | hours at steady load | leaks, including agent-specific ones: conversation state that never expires, token budgets that creep upward per session |\n| Failover and recovery drills | kill the primary database, region or model deployment on a schedule | actual recovery time and data loss against disaster-recovery targets |\n| Continuous fallback canaries | a synthetic request through the secondary provider every few minutes | expired credentials, renamed models and quota changes are caught before a real failover |\n| Game days | scheduled, cross-team scenario exercises including paging, runbooks, communication, decision-making | the humans work too |\n\n- **Canaries are the single cheapest control against fallback rot.**\n- **Game days matter because** a perfect failover with a confused on-call is still an outage.\n\n### Chaos engineering: discovering what you did not build for\nThe discipline is defined by its method, not by breaking things.\n\n1. **Define steady state as user-facing output.** Success rate, p95 latency, task completion rate, cost\n   per task: the SLIs. Not CPU or queue depth, because the hypothesis is about whether users notice.\n2. **State a falsifiable hypothesis.** \"If the primary provider returns 429 on 30 percent of calls, success\n   rate stays at or above 99 percent and p95 rises by less than 20 percent.\" A hypothesis makes the result\n   a finding either way; \"we killed a pod and nothing happened\" teaches nothing.\n3. **Only hypothesize resilience you believe you have.** If you already expect the system to fall over, the\n   experiment has nothing to discover and the right move is to fix it first.\n4. **Inject realistic events.** Latency, errors, instance loss, network partition, dependency\n   unavailability, resource exhaustion, zone loss, clock skew. Prefer the events your FMEA ranked highest;\n   that table is the experiment backlog.\n5. **Minimize blast radius.** Start in staging, then one percent of production, an internal tenant, one\n   availability zone. Define an abort condition and a kill switch before starting. Run during business\n   hours with on-call informed, because an experiment indistinguishable from an incident is one.\n6. **Compare experiment and control.** Route a slice of traffic through the fault and an equal slice\n   without, and compare the SLIs, as in an A/B test. This separates the fault's effect from background\n   noise, but only with enough traffic in each arm (see the sizing note below).\n7. **Fix, then widen, then automate.** Each refuted hypothesis becomes a fix and a regression test. Verified\n   hypotheses graduate to larger blast radius and eventually to continuous automated runs, because the\n   system will drift away from the verified state.\n\n### Sizing the experiment\n- **Telling 99 percent success from 98 percent** needs on the order of a thousand requests per arm: the\n  standard error of a 99 percent rate over 1,000 requests is about 0.3 points.\n- **So a one-percent slice of a low-traffic service may need hours to reach a verdict.** Size the slice and\n  the duration together.\n\n### Injection mechanisms\n| Level | Tools and techniques | Best for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Application | hooks in your own provider and tool clients | AI systems: you cannot make a vendor's API go down, but your client can behave as if it had |\n| Network | a proxy (Toxiproxy is a common choice) or a service mesh's fault-injection rules: latency, bandwidth caps, connection resets | internal dependencies |\n| Infrastructure | kill instances and pods, fill disks, hog CPU: the Chaos Monkey lineage, continued by Kubernetes-native LitmusChaos and Chaos Mesh, commercial platforms, and cloud providers' fault-injection services | instance and zone loss |\n| Data (AI-specific, usually hand-built) | serve a stale index snapshot, drop a fraction of retrieved chunks, revoke a tool's credentials, or return refusals, truncated text and malformed JSON from a model stub | guards against wrong answers |\n\n### What is different about LLM and agent systems\n- **Faults are semantic as well as operational.** A model returns plausible nonsense; retrieval returns the\n  wrong tenant's documents; a tool reports success with wrong data. Infrastructure chaos never produces\n  these, so the experiment must inject them and the hypothesis must be about whether guards (schema\n  validation, groundedness checks, confirmation steps) catch them.\n- **Amplification.** Injecting 429s should reveal whether backoff, jitter and step budgets hold, or whether\n  an agent loops fifty times and a retry storm forms.\n- **Cost.** A fault that pushes traffic to a fallback model has a dollar blast radius as well as a latency\n  one, so experiments need a spending cap and the cost SLI belongs in the hypothesis.","example":"### Fault-injecting client wrapper and experiment runner\nA wrapper around the real model client, controlled by environment variables so it is inert by default,\nplus a minimal runner with an abort condition. Run once as a control and once with a fault, and compare\nthe two results against the hypothesis.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, os, random, time\n\nclass RateLimitError(Exception): pass\n\nclass ChaosLLM:\n    \"\"\"Wraps the real client. Injects faults on a fraction of calls; inert unless CHAOS_RATE is set.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, client):\n        self.client = client\n        self.rate = float(os.getenv(\"CHAOS_RATE\", \"0\"))\n        self.mode = os.getenv(\"CHAOS_MODE\", \"latency\")\n\n    async def complete(self, **kw):\n        if random.random() < self.rate:\n            if self.mode == \"latency\":\n                await asyncio.sleep(8.0)                 # provider at 8 s: do timeouts and breakers fire?\n            elif self.mode == \"rate_limit\":\n                raise RateLimitError(\"429\")              # do backoff, jitter and step budgets hold?\n            elif self.mode == \"garbage\":\n                return {\"content\": \"{not json\", \"usage\": {\"total_tokens\": 5}}   # does validation catch it?\n        return await self.client.complete(**kw)\n\nasync def experiment(handler, requests, min_success=0.95):\n    \"\"\"Runs traffic through `handler`, aborts if steady state is violated, reports the SLIs.\"\"\"\n    latencies, ok = [], 0\n    for req in requests:\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        try:\n            await handler(req); ok += 1\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        latencies.append(time.perf_counter() - t0)\n        if len(latencies) >= 20 and ok / len(latencies) < min_success:\n            raise RuntimeError(\"steady state violated; experiment aborted\")   # the kill switch\n    latencies.sort()\n    return {\"success_rate\": ok / len(latencies),\n            \"p95_s\": latencies[int(0.95 * (len(latencies) - 1))]}\n\n# Control run: CHAOS_RATE=0. Experiment: CHAOS_MODE=rate_limit CHAOS_RATE=0.3.\n# Hypothesis: success_rate >= 0.99 and p95_s < 1.2 x the control's. Anything else is a finding.\n```\n\n- **In production the same wrapper sits in the deployed service** behind a per-tenant or per-request flag,\n  so the fault can be applied to one percent of real traffic.\n- **The runner's abort check becomes an alert** on the experiment slice's SLIs that flips the flag off.\n\n### How long must the experiment run? A runnable sizing check\nA standard two-proportion power calculation (two-sided 5 percent, 80 percent power) for telling a 99\npercent control from a 98 percent fault arm, and what that means in hours per arm.\n\n```python\nfrom math import ceil, sqrt\n\ndef se(p, n):\n    return sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n)          # standard error of a success rate\n\ndef n_per_arm(p_control, p_fault, z_alpha=1.96, z_power=0.84):\n    \"\"\"Requests per arm to tell two success rates apart (two-sided 5%, 80% power).\"\"\"\n    var = p_control * (1 - p_control) + p_fault * (1 - p_fault)\n    return ceil((z_alpha + z_power) ** 2 * var / (p_control - p_fault) ** 2)\n\nprint(f\"SE of 99% over 1,000 requests: {100 * se(0.99, 1000):.2f} points\")   # 0.31\nn = n_per_arm(0.99, 0.98)\nprint(\"requests per arm, 99% vs 98%:\", n)                                     # 2313\nfor rps, slice_ in [(10, 0.01), (10, 0.10), (200, 0.01)]:\n    hours = n / (rps * slice_) / 3600\n    print(f\"{rps} rps service, {slice_:.0%} slice: {hours:.1f} h per arm\")\n# 10 rps at 1%: 6.4 h | 10 rps at 10%: 0.6 h | 200 rps at 1%: 0.3 h\n```\n\n| Service | Slice | Time per arm |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10 requests per second | 1% | ~6.4 hours |\n| 10 requests per second | 10% | ~0.6 hours |\n| 200 requests per second | 1% | ~0.3 hours |\n\nThe formal answer (~2,300 per arm) matches the \"order of a thousand\" rule of thumb; widen the slice once the\nabort condition has proven it fires, rather than running a tiny slice for so long that drift muddies it.","patterns":["**Fallback canary on a schedule.** Send one synthetic request through every fallback provider and model every few minutes from a scheduler, tagged so it is excluded from user SLIs. Alert on any failure; monitor canary latency and the secondary's response schema.","**Flag-gated client faults.** Ship the `ChaosLLM`-style wrapper in production behind a feature flag scoped to an internal tenant or a request header, with rate and mode as flag values. Monitor the experiment slice's SLIs separately and auto-disable the flag when the abort threshold is crossed.","**Proxy faults for internal tools.** Put Toxiproxy between the agent and a tool API in staging and add a latency toxic (for example `toxiproxy-cli toxic add -t latency -a latency=8000 <proxy>`), or use a service mesh's fault-injection rules (Istio's VirtualService `fault` with `delay` or `abort` and a percentage). Monitor breaker transitions and bulkhead rejections.","**Quarterly game day with the humans.** Script a scenario (primary provider down, SSO connector cut), page the real on-call, and time detection, decision and recovery against the runbook. Track action items to closure like postmortem actions.","**Spending cap on every experiment.** Before any drill that routes traffic to a pricier fallback, set a budget for the experiment slice and put cost per task in the hypothesis. Monitor spend per slice in near real time."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Steady state: user-facing SLIs\"] --> H[\"Falsifiable hypothesis\"]\n    H --> F[\"Pick fault from FMEA backlog\"]\n    F --> B[\"Blast radius, abort rule, kill switch\"]\n    B --> X[\"Run fault slice and control slice\"]\n    X --> A{\"Abort condition hit?\"}\n    A -->|\"yes\"| K[\"Kill switch: stop, investigate\"]\n    A -->|\"no\"| C{\"Hypothesis held?\"}\n    C -->|\"no\"| R[\"Fix plus regression test\"]\n    C -->|\"yes\"| W[\"Widen blast radius, then automate\"]\n    R -.->|\"re-run\"| X","caption":"The experiment loop: the hypothesis and abort condition come before any fault, and both outcomes (held or\nrefuted) produce work."},"interview":[{"q":"Why run chaos experiments in production at all, when staging is safer?","a":"- **Staging lacks the real conditions:** production traffic patterns, data volumes and vendor quotas, which\n  is where emergent failures like retry storms and herds on recovery live.\n- **The failure will happen anyway;** the choice is whether it happens at one percent of traffic, during\n  business hours, with a kill switch, or at full scale at night.\n- **But graduate there:** staging first, and production only with abort conditions, per-slice observability\n  and a kill switch."},{"q":"Design a chaos experiment for an agent whose provider starts returning 429s.","a":"- **Steady state:** success rate, p95, task completion and cost per task on the experiment slice.\n- **Hypothesis:** with 30 percent injected 429s, success stays at or above 99 percent and p95 rises less than\n  20 percent; also provider calls per user request stay bounded (no loop).\n- **Blast radius and abort:** an internal tenant at one percent, abort below 98 percent success, a spending\n  cap; compare against a control slice."},{"q":"How long would you run an experiment on a 1 percent slice of a 10-request-per-second service?","a":"- **Size from the effect you want to see:** 99 versus 98 percent success needs roughly 2,300 requests per arm\n  at 5 percent significance and 80 percent power.\n- **A 1 percent slice of 10 requests per second is 0.1 per second,** so about 6.4 hours per arm.\n- **Options:** widen the slice once the abort rule has proven it fires, or accept a larger detectable effect."},{"q":"What faults would you inject that infrastructure chaos tools never produce?","a":"- **Semantic faults:** plausible-but-wrong output, refusals, truncated text, malformed JSON from a model\n  stub, stale index snapshots, dropped chunks, cross-tenant retrieval, revoked tool credentials.\n- **Why:** an LLM product's worst failures are silent wrong answers, so the hypothesis is whether schema\n  validation, groundedness checks and confirmation steps catch them, judged by trustworthy evals."},{"q":"When would you not invest in chaos engineering yet?","a":"- **No SLO, no steady state:** for a prototype there is nothing to defend.\n- **Mechanisms missing:** if you already expect the system to fall over, fix it first; the experiment has\n  nothing to discover.\n- **No per-slice observability:** the hypothesis could not be evaluated, so build that before the first run."}],"tradeoffs":["**Production signal vs risk.** Production experiments give true signal, with real traffic, data and vendor limits, at real risk; staging is safe but misses emergent behavior. Start in staging and graduate to production only once abort conditions, per-slice observability and a kill switch exist.","**Small blast radius vs evidence.** A small slice limits harm but also limits evidence: the smaller the slice, the longer the experiment must run to separate the fault's effect from noise. Widen the slice once the abort condition has proven it fires, rather than running a tiny slice for so long that drift muddies the comparison.","**Coverage vs cost.** Every experiment costs engineering time and sometimes vendor dollars, because faults push traffic to fallbacks and retries; prioritize by FMEA risk rather than by what is easy to inject.","**Continuous vs scheduled.** Continuous automated chaos catches drift but demands a mature on-call and can be mistaken for a real incident; scheduled game days are cheaper and clearer but only test what someone remembered to schedule.","**Semantic vs infrastructure faults.** Semantic fault injection tests the guards that matter for AI but needs trustworthy evals to judge success; infrastructure fault injection is easy to measure but blind to wrong-answer failures. Do both, in that order of importance for an LLM product."],"pitfalls":["**Running without a steady-state hypothesis.** Symptom: a log of experiments none of which produced a fix, and nobody able to say what was verified.","**No abort condition or kill switch.** Symptom: a postmortem whose root cause is the chaos experiment itself.","**Injecting only infrastructure faults into an AI system.** Symptom: pods survive every drill, then the first real 429 storm triggers agent loops and a cost spike nobody tested for.","**Never canarying the fallback.** Symptom: the first real failover fails for a configuration reason, an expired key, a renamed model, a lower quota, that a five-minute synthetic check would have caught.","**Experimenting without per-slice observability.** Symptom: the hypothesis cannot be evaluated because the experiment's traffic is indistinguishable from everything else in the dashboards.","**Applying faults to shared customer traffic without regard to contracts.** Symptom: a customer's SLA breached by your own experiment, and a difficult conversation about who caused the outage.","**Ignoring the cost blast radius.** Symptom: a full-traffic primary-failure drill that routes an hour of traffic to the expensive fallback model, visible on the next invoice."],"when":"- **Begin once** the basic mechanisms exist and observability can show SLIs for an experiment slice; before\n  that, build the mechanisms.\n- **Progression:** dependency fault injection in staging for the top three FMEA rows, synthetic canaries\n  through every fallback path, one game day per quarter for failover and disaster recovery, then production\n  chaos at one percent for the most critical mechanisms, growing toward automation.\n- **Avoid** for a prototype or a service with no SLO, because there is no steady state to defend.\n- **Measure** hypotheses verified and refuted, weaknesses found per experiment, time to detect and time to\n  abort.\n- **Interviews:** describe one experiment end to end: steady state, hypothesis, fault, blast radius, abort\n  condition, result, and the fix it produced.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the fault-injection hooks into provider and tool clients, writes the experiment and its abort check, and turns every refuted hypothesis into a regression test.","architect":"Decides which failure modes must be proven rather than assumed before launch, sets the blast-radius and approval policy, and ties experiments to SLOs and the FMEA backlog.","fde":"Runs a game day with the customer before go-live, cutting their SSO or ERP connector to show the agent degrading gracefully, so their operations team has seen the behavior and the runbook works in their environment."},"check":{"q":"You have shipped multi-provider fallback for an agent. How would you prove it works without\nwaiting for the primary provider to fail, and what would you watch for?","a":"Three layers. First, a synthetic canary every few minutes through the secondary path, which\ncatches expired keys, renamed models and quota changes continuously. Second, staging fault injection:\nforce 100 percent 503s from the primary through the client hook with the hypothesis that success rate\nstays at or above 99 percent, p95 stays within budget and cost per request stays under twice the\ncontrol; watch specifically for tool-call format incompatibilities, differing context limits and\nschema differences between the two models. Third, production chaos on one percent of an internal\ntenant's traffic with 30 percent injected 429s, aborting if success drops below 98 percent. Throughout,\nwatch retry amplification (provider calls per user request), fallback rate, breaker state transitions\nand spend, and sample the fallback outputs through your quality evals because the secondary model may be\nmeasurably worse. Finally test recovery: when the primary returns, traffic should move back through the\nbreaker's half-open probes without a thundering herd."},"quiz":[{"q":"What distinguishes chaos engineering from ordinary fault-injection testing?","options":["Chaos engineering only runs in production, while fault injection only runs in staging","A falsifiable steady-state hypothesis measured on user-facing SLIs, a bounded blast radius, and comparison against a control","Chaos engineering uses random faults, while fault injection uses chosen ones","Chaos engineering requires a dedicated tool such as Chaos Monkey"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fault injection is a mechanism; chaos engineering is an experimental method built on it. The hypothesis is what turns \"we broke it and watched\" into a finding, and the blast radius and control group are what make running it on real systems defensible."},{"q":"Which class of fault is most specific to AI systems and most often skipped in drills?","options":["Killing a pod in the inference deployment","Losing an availability zone","Semantic faults such as plausible-but-wrong output, malformed JSON and stale or cross-tenant retrieval","Filling the disk on a worker node"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Infrastructure faults are easy to inject and measure, but an LLM product's worst failures are silent wrong answers. Only injecting those, via model and retrieval stubs, tests whether validation, grounding checks and confirmation steps actually catch them."},{"q":"Why run a synthetic request through the fallback provider continuously rather than only during drills?","options":["To keep the fallback provider's cache warm for lower latency","Because vendors require minimum usage to keep an account active","Fallback paths rot through credential expiry, model renames and quota changes, and a canary detects that before a real failover does","To accumulate usage data for negotiating a better contract"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The fallback carries no traffic in normal operation, so nothing exercises it between drills. A cheap periodic request turns months of silent decay into an alert within minutes of the configuration breaking."}]},{"key":"reliability/slas-slos","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Beyer et al., 2018 — The Site Reliability Workbook","Hidalgo, 2020 — Implementing Service Level Objectives"],"why":"- **Without targets, reliability is argued by anecdote.** Product wants features, operations wants\n  stability, every incident is a crisis and no incident is ever acceptable.\n- **\"100 percent reliable\" is not a target.** It is impossible, each additional nine costs roughly an order\n  of magnitude more, and past a point users cannot perceive the difference because their own network and\n  devices fail more often than you do.\n- **An SLO makes the trade explicit.** Pick the number users actually need, measure it from their side,\n  and treat the remainder as a budget to spend on velocity: deploys, model upgrades, experiments. When the\n  budget is gone, reliability work takes priority. That policy, not the number, is what changes behavior.\n- **SLAs exist for a different reason.** Enterprise customers need a promise with teeth before procurement\n  will sign, so the SLA states a target and the credit paid if it is missed. The internal SLO must be\n  stricter, because the gap is the margin to detect and fix problems before they become contract events; a\n  team whose SLO equals its SLA has legal in every incident review.\n- **AI breaks the classic availability SLI.** The share of non-5xx responses is nearly meaningless for a\n  model endpoint: a confident wrong answer in 800 milliseconds counts as a success, and an SLO built only on\n  that stays green while customers churn. LLM SLIs must cover latency as users feel it, quality measured by\n  evals, and often cost, each breakable by a vendor model change with no code change of yours.","how":"### The three layers, precisely\n| Layer | What it is | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SLI** | a ratio: good events ÷ valid events | the fraction of requests answered correctly within two seconds |\n| **SLO** | SLI target plus a window | 99.5 percent over 30 days; or 99.9 percent of valid requests good over a rolling 30 days |\n| **SLA** | a subset of the SLOs, looser, legally reviewed, with defined remedies and exclusions (scheduled maintenance, customer-caused load) | internal SLO of 99.9 percent behind an external SLA of 99.5 percent |\n\n- **Define \"good\" in one sentence a query can implement,** for example status 2xx *and* total latency under\n  3 seconds *and* output passed schema validation.\n- **\"Valid\" excludes what should not count,** such as malformed client requests, but excluding too much\n  hides real failures.\n- **Measure as close to the user as you can,** at the load balancer or in the client, because the server\n  cannot count requests it never received.\n- **Rolling windows suit engineering decisions,** because a calendar window resets everyone's attention on\n  the first and exhausts by the twentieth. Calendar windows remain common for SLAs and business reporting\n  because contracts and invoices are monthly; many teams run rolling internally and report calendar\n  externally.\n- **A service usually carries several SLOs:** availability, latency, and for AI products a quality SLO.\n\n### Error budget arithmetic\n```\nbudget = (1 − SLO) × window\n99.9% over 30 days: 30 × 24 × 60 = 43,200 minutes × 0.001 = 43.2 minutes of full outage\n                    (or 1,000 bad requests per million)\n```\n\n| SLO | Budget per 30 days |\n|---|---|\n| 99 percent | 7.2 hours |\n| 99.5 percent | 3.6 hours |\n| 99.9 percent | 43.2 minutes |\n| 99.95 percent | 21.6 minutes |\n| 99.99 percent | 4.32 minutes |\n\n- **The last row is why 99.99 percent requires automated failover:** a human cannot be paged, understand the\n  problem and act in four minutes.\n\n### Dependencies cap what you can promise\n- **Serial dependencies multiply.** A request path that needs a provider with a 99.9 percent SLA and a vector\n  store with 99.95 percent has a ceiling of about 99.85 percent before your own code fails once.\n- **Your SLO cannot honestly exceed that ceiling without redundancy.** An independent fallback provider\n  raises the provider term toward 1 − (0.001 × 0.001), though true independence is rare and the figure is\n  illustrative.\n\n### Burn rate and alerting\n- **Burn rate** is how fast you consume budget relative to the pace that would exhaust it exactly at the end\n  of the window. A burn rate of 1 spends the whole budget in 30 days; 10 spends it in 3 days.\n- **Alert on burn rate, not raw error rate.** A two-minute blip at 5 percent errors consumes a sliver of\n  budget and should not page anyone. A persistent 0.3 percent error rate on a 99.9 percent SLO is a 3× burn\n  that will breach the SLO in ten days, and should.\n\n| Tier (SRE Workbook multi-window scheme) | Long window | Burn rate threshold | Budget consumed | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fast burn | 1 hour | 14.4 | 2 percent of a 30-day budget in an hour | page |\n| Medium burn | 6 hours | 6 | 5 percent | page |\n| Slow burn | 3 days | 1 | 10 percent | ticket |\n\n- **Each long window is paired with a short one,** so the alert clears once the problem stops.\n\n### The error budget policy\n- **The numbers do nothing by themselves.** The policy says what happens when the budget is spent: freeze\n  risky launches, require review for prompt and model changes, prioritize reliability work until the budget\n  recovers.\n- **And when it is plentiful:** ship faster, run chaos experiments, which spend budget on purpose.\n- **Agreeing this in advance with product leadership is most of the work.**\n\n### SLIs for AI systems\n| SLI | Shape | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Latency** | time to first token for streaming; total time per response; for agents, time to task completion | task completion can take minutes and needs its own target |\n| **Availability** after resilience | fraction completing without a provider or tool error once retries and fallbacks had their chance | measured after the resilience layer |\n| **Quality** | fraction of a sampled slice passing automated evals (groundedness, schema, policy), calibrated by a smaller human-labeled sample | sampled because evaluating every response costs as much as serving it |\n| **Cost** per successful task | paired with quality | so neither can be gamed by sacrificing the other |\n| **Side-effect correctness** for agents | actions reconciled against intent | tickets created equals tickets requested |\n\n- **The quality SLO is the one most likely to move without a deploy,** which is why model version pinning\n  and evaluation gates exist.\n- **Vendor SLAs usually cover availability only,** with service credits. They do not promise latency or\n  quality, so your latency and quality SLOs must assume you absorb those risks yourself.","example":"### Worked month: an assistant on a 99.9 percent SLO\nSLO: 99.9 percent \"good\" responses over a rolling 30 days, where good means 2xx, total latency under 4\nseconds, and schema-valid output.\n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Traffic | 20 million requests per month |\n| Budget | 20,000 bad responses, equivalent to 43.2 minutes of total outage |\n| Average rate | 20,000,000 ÷ 43,200 minutes ≈ 463 requests per minute |\n\n1. **Day 6, provider degradation.** 25 minutes with 60 percent of requests failing or timing out. Bad\n   responses: 25 × 463 × 0.6 ≈ 6,945, which is 35 percent of the month's budget gone in 25 minutes.\n2. **The fast page fires correctly.** Burn rate during the incident is 0.6 ÷ 0.001 = 600, so the one-hour\n   14.4× page fires within the first few minutes. Afterwards 13,055 bad responses remain for 24 days.\n3. **Day 9, a prompt edit.** Schema-validation failures rise from 0.02 percent to 0.15 percent,\n   persistently: a bad fraction of 0.0015 against an allowed 0.001, a burn rate of 1.5. Nothing is on\n   fire, and no page fires.\n4. **But the budget will run out.** At 463 × 0.0015 ≈ 0.69 bad responses per minute, 13,055 ÷ 0.69 ≈ 18,800\n   minutes, about 13 days, so the SLO will be breached before the window closes.\n5. **The slow-burn tier catches it.** The three-day 1× ticket alert fires once the new 1.5× burn dominates\n   its window, roughly two days in (around day 11), long before the budget is gone. The paging thresholds\n   never fire, which is the point of having a slow-burn tier.\n6. **The policy applies.** The prompt change is rolled back or guarded with a repair step, and model\n   upgrades are frozen until the rolling budget recovers.\n7. **The SLA was never at risk.** The external SLA of 99.5 percent, allowing 100,000 bad responses, was\n   never close to breach; the internal SLO forced action two weeks before a customer could have noticed.\n\n### Budget table and multi-window burn-rate alerts, runnable\nReproduces the budget table and evaluates the three alert tiers for the two incidents above (a rule fires\nonly when both its long and short windows exceed the threshold).\n\n```python\nWINDOW_MIN = 30 * 24 * 60                     # 43,200 minutes\n\ndef budget_minutes(slo, window_min=WINDOW_MIN):\n    return (1 - slo) * window_min\n\ndef burn_rate(bad, total, slo):\n    return (bad / total) / (1 - slo)          # 1 = exactly exhausts the budget at window end\n\n# Multi-window rules (SRE Workbook): long window, short window, threshold, action\nRULES = [(\"1h\", \"5m\", 14.4, \"page\"), (\"6h\", \"30m\", 6, \"page\"), (\"3d\", \"6h\", 1, \"ticket\")]\n\ndef evaluate(counts, slo=0.999):\n    \"\"\"counts: window -> (bad, total). A rule fires only if BOTH its windows exceed the threshold.\"\"\"\n    return [(action, long_w, thr) for long_w, short_w, thr, action in RULES\n            if burn_rate(*counts[long_w], slo) > thr and burn_rate(*counts[short_w], slo) > thr]\n\nfor slo in (0.99, 0.995, 0.999, 0.9995, 0.9999):\n    print(f\"{slo:.2%}: {budget_minutes(slo):7.2f} min per 30 days\")   # 432, 216, 43.2, 21.6, 4.32\n\nrpm = 463                                     # requests per minute in the worked month\ndef window(minutes, bad_fraction):\n    total = minutes * rpm\n    return (bad_fraction * total, total)\n\n# Day 9: persistent 0.15% bad. Only the slow-burn ticket fires.\nslow_burn = {w: window(m, 0.0015) for w, m in\n             [(\"5m\", 5), (\"1h\", 60), (\"30m\", 30), (\"6h\", 360), (\"3d\", 4320)]}\nprint(\"prompt regression:\", evaluate(slow_burn))     # [('ticket', '3d', 1)]\n\n# Day 6, 25 minutes into the 60%-bad incident: each window's bad fraction.\noutage = {\"5m\": window(5, 0.6), \"1h\": window(60, 0.25), \"30m\": window(30, 0.5),\n          \"6h\": window(360, 0.042), \"3d\": window(4320, 0.0035)}\nprint(\"provider outage:  \", evaluate(outage))        # both pages and the ticket\n```","patterns":["**Count good and valid at the edge.** Emit two counters from the load balancer or gateway (valid requests, and good ones meeting the one-sentence definition) and compute the SLI as a ratio of their rates in your metrics system. Monitor the gap between edge and in-service counts; a large gap means the service is missing failures users feel.","**Generate burn-rate alerts from an SLO spec.** Rather than hand-writing alert rules, describe each SLO once and let a generator produce the multi-window rules (open-source tools such as Sloth or Pyrra do this for Prometheus). Monitor alert precision: pages that did not need action mean thresholds or windows are wrong.","**Sampled quality SLI from an eval pipeline.** Route a fixed random sample of production responses to automated evals (groundedness, schema, policy) and a smaller slice to human labeling for calibration; report pass rate with its confidence interval. Monitor judge agreement with the human sample.","**Pin model versions and gate changes on budget.** Call dated model snapshots rather than floating aliases, and make prompt or model changes check the remaining budget in CI or the deploy tool. Monitor eval pass rate per model version.","**Budget review in sprint planning.** Show budget remaining per SLO at planning and in a monthly SLO review; apply the agreed policy (freeze or accelerate) mechanically. Monitor how many months the budget was exhausted and whether shipping behavior changed."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    E[\"Requests at the edge\"] --> G[\"Count good and valid\"]\n    G --> S[\"SLI: good over valid\"]\n    S --> B[\"Error budget remaining vs SLO\"]\n    B --> R[\"Burn rate per window\"]\n    R --> F{\"Which tier fires?\"}\n    F -->|\"over 14.4 in 1h or 6 in 6h\"| P[\"Page on-call\"]\n    F -->|\"over 1 in 3 days\"| T[\"Ticket\"]\n    F -->|\"none\"| N[\"Ship, run experiments\"]\n    P --> Y[\"Error budget policy\"]\n    T --> Y\n    Y -->|\"budget spent\"| Z[\"Freeze risky changes\"]","caption":"Counters feed the SLI, the SLO turns it into a remaining budget, burn rate decides who gets alerted, and\nthe policy decides what the team does next."},"interview":[{"q":"Define SLI, SLO and SLA, and explain how they relate.","a":"- **SLI:** a ratio of good to valid events measured from the user's side, such as requests that are 2xx,\n  under 3 seconds and schema-valid.\n- **SLO:** the internal target for that SLI over a window, for example 99.9 percent over a rolling 30 days;\n  1 minus the SLO is the error budget.\n- **SLA:** a looser external contract on a subset of SLOs with credits and exclusions; it sits below the SLO\n  so there is margin to act before a contract event."},{"q":"What SLIs would you propose for an LLM chat assistant beyond uptime?","a":"- **Latency as felt:** time to first token for streaming and total response time, as fractions under a\n  threshold, not means.\n- **Quality:** pass rate of a sampled slice through automated evals (groundedness, schema, policy),\n  calibrated against human labels.\n- **Cost per successful task,** paired with quality, and availability measured after retries and fallbacks."},{"q":"Where does the 14.4 burn-rate threshold come from, and why use burn rate at all?","a":"- **14.4 means 2 percent of a 30-day budget in one hour:** 30 days is 720 hours, and 0.02 × 720 = 14.4.\n- **Burn rate scales alerts to budget impact,** so a two-minute blip does not page while a persistent 3×\n  burn that breaches in ten days does.\n- **The 3-day 1× ticket tier** catches slow burns, like a prompt edit at 1.5×, that no page threshold sees."},{"q":"A team runs a 99.9 percent SLO on a service with 2,000 requests a month, and alerts flap constantly. What would you change?","a":"- **The budget is two bad requests,** so one flaky client exhausts it and burn rates swing wildly.\n- **Options:** a longer window, a looser target, or synthetic probes to supply enough events for a stable\n  ratio."},{"q":"What does 99.99 percent availability imply operationally, and when would you refuse to promise it?","a":"- **4.32 minutes of budget per 30 days:** no human can be paged, diagnose and act in that time, so failover\n  must be automated.\n- **Dependencies cap it:** serial dependencies at 99.9 and 99.95 percent give a ceiling near 99.85 percent,\n  so without independent redundancy the promise is not honest.\n- **Refuse, or price in redundancy,** and note vendor SLAs usually cover availability only."}],"tradeoffs":["**Tighter SLO vs cost and velocity.** A tighter SLO pleases users but costs roughly an order of magnitude more per extra nine, in redundancy and in lost velocity from a smaller budget; it stops paying off once users' own client reliability is worse than yours.","**Edge vs server measurement.** Measuring SLIs at the edge or client is truthful but includes noise you do not control; server-side measurement is clean but blind to the failures users feel. Use the edge for availability and the server for latency breakdowns.","**Request-based vs time-based SLOs.** Request-based SLOs weight by traffic, so a ten-minute outage at peak costs more than one at night; time-based SLOs (\"minutes with error rate under x\") treat them equally. Request-based reflects user impact better; time-based is simpler to put in a contract.","**Sampled evals vs human review.** Quality SLIs via sampled automated evals are affordable but noisy and judge-dependent; full human review is accurate but too slow and expensive for a 30-day window. The sample size sets the confidence, so size it to the decision you need to make.","**Signing an SLA vs freedom.** An SLA unlocks enterprise deals but makes every dependency's SLA your ceiling and converts internal misses into contract events. Worth it when revenue justifies buying the redundancy."],"pitfalls":["**Defining the SLI as \"not a 5xx\".** Symptom: a green SLO dashboard during a quality incident while support tickets climb.","**Measuring inside the service.** Symptom: the service reports 99.99 percent while the load balancer shows 2 percent 502s during every deploy.","**Setting a high-nines SLO on a low-traffic service.** Symptom: at 2,000 requests a month a 99.9 percent SLO allows two bad requests, so one flaky client exhausts the budget and burn-rate alerts flap. Fix: a longer window, a looser target, or synthetic probes to supply enough events.","**Using mean latency as the SLI.** Symptom: p50 is fine while a tenth of users wait 20 seconds; use the fraction of requests under a threshold instead.","**Calendar-month windows.** Symptom: relaxed attention on the first, a burned budget by the twentieth, every month.","**Setting the SLO equal to the SLA.** Symptom: legal joins engineering incident reviews and every internal miss becomes a credit negotiation.","**Having no error budget policy.** Symptom: the budget is exhausted three months running and nothing about how the team ships has changed.","**Alerting on raw error-rate thresholds.** Symptom: pages at three in the morning for two-minute blips that consumed a fraction of a percent of budget, while a slow burn that will breach the SLO goes unnoticed for a week.","**Assuming a vendor's SLA covers what your SLO needs.** Symptom: the provider \"met its SLA\" during a ten-fold latency degradation that blew your latency SLO, because their SLA only promises availability.","**Setting a quality SLO without pinning model versions.** Symptom: the eval pass rate drops with no deploy in the changelog, and the cause is a silent vendor model update."],"when":"- **Every user-facing service** needs at least one availability SLO and one latency SLO; an AI service adds a\n  quality SLO as soon as it has an eval it trusts, and a cost-per-task target soon after.\n- **Set the first SLO from measured history,** slightly below current performance, rather than from\n  aspiration, then tighten with evidence.\n- **Sign SLAs only** for SLIs you can measure from the customer's vantage point and that your dependencies'\n  SLAs can support.\n- **Day to day** this looks like checking budget remaining in sprint planning, burn-rate alerts on the\n  pager, and a monthly SLO review.\n- **Interviews:** define the three terms crisply, compute an error budget from a target and a window without\n  hesitation, explain why burn-rate alerting beats threshold alerting, and propose SLIs for an LLM product\n  that go beyond uptime.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments good and valid counters at the edge, builds the eval sampling pipeline for the quality SLI, implements multi-window burn-rate alerts, and checks the budget before risky changes.","architect":"Chooses SLOs per service tier, derives them from dependency SLAs, decides where redundancy is required to meet them, and owns the error budget policy agreed with product.","fde":"Translates a customer's SLA request into SLIs measurable inside their environment, explains why 99.99 percent needs redundancy they have not bought, and builds the shared dashboard that governs hypercare."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks for a 99.95 percent availability SLA on your agent product. Your LLM provider\noffers a 99.9 percent SLA, your vector database 99.95 percent, and you have no fallback provider. How\ndo you respond, and what would have to change?","a":"Start with the ceiling. The request path needs both dependencies, so availability is bounded by\nroughly 0.999 × 0.9995 ≈ 0.9985, about 99.85 percent, before any failure of your own code. Signing\n99.95 percent, whose budget is 21.6 minutes per 30 days, would be a promise one provider incident breaks.\nThe honest response offers 99.5 percent contractually with an internal SLO of 99.9 percent now, and a\npath to 99.95 percent priced to include what it costs: an independent fallback provider, which lifts the\nprovider term toward 1 − 0.001² if the two are truly independent, a vector-store replica, and a\ndefinition of \"available\" that counts degraded-mode answers from cache or fallback. Add exclusions for\nscheduled maintenance and customer-caused overload, and measure from their side of the network. Finally\nclarify scope: an availability SLA says nothing about latency or answer quality, so propose separate\nlatency and quality SLOs, visible on a shared dashboard but not under penalty until both sides trust\nthe measurement."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the error budget for a 99.9 percent SLO over a 30-day window, expressed as full-outage time?","options":["4.32 minutes","43.2 minutes","7.2 hours","21.6 minutes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"30 days is 43,200 minutes; 0.1 percent of that is 43.2 minutes. The 4.32-minute figure belongs to 99.99 percent, 21.6 minutes to 99.95 percent and 7.2 hours to 99 percent; the arithmetic is the same multiplication each time."},{"q":"Why should the internal SLO be stricter than the external SLA?","options":["Because SLAs are measured over longer windows than SLOs","Because customers expect the published number to be rounded up","The gap is the margin in which problems are detected and fixed before they become contract events with penalties","Because vendors require it in their own terms of service"],"answer":2,"explanation":"If the SLO equals the SLA, every internal miss is already a breach and legal enters every incident review. A stricter SLO forces action while there is still budget left before the contractual line."},{"q":"Why alert on error-budget burn rate rather than on a raw error-rate threshold?","options":["Because error rates cannot be computed in real time","Burn-rate alerts page only when the budget is genuinely threatened, catching both fast outages and slow persistent degradation","Because burn rate is required for SLA reporting","Because raw error rates are always noisier than burn rates"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A two-minute blip consumes a sliver of budget and should not wake anyone; a persistent 0.3 percent error rate on a 99.9 percent SLO burns at 3× and will breach within the window. Multi-window burn-rate alerts distinguish the two; a single threshold cannot."}]}]});
